{
  id:'pedep-gp-1', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'When evaluating a child for stunting, which growth chart you will use?',
  options:['height for age charts','weight for age charts','weight for length charts','body mass index charts','mid arm circumference measurements'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Stunting reflects chronic malnutrition, which primarily impairs linear growth. Therefore, height-for-age is the correct indicator, not weight-based charts.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the vocabulary the examiner is testing.** Three anthropometric words are constantly confused, and each one is tied to a different chart:\n\n| Term | What it means | The chart that shows it | Time course |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Stunting** | Low **height (length) for age** | Height-for-age | **Chronic** undernutrition — months to years |\n| **Wasting** | Low **weight for height** | Weight-for-height | **Acute** undernutrition — weeks |\n| **Underweight** | Low **weight for age** | Weight-for-age | Mixed — cannot separate the two |\n\nLinear growth is the slow variable. A child who is short for age has been undernourished, or chronically ill, long enough for the skeleton to fall behind — which is exactly why height-for-age is the marker of chronicity. Weight moves quickly in both directions and therefore reports the present, not the past.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Weight-for-age charts* detect underweight but cannot tell you whether a light child is short-and-proportionate (stunted) or tall-and-thin (wasted), so they answer a different question. *Weight-for-length charts* are the wasting chart — the opposite end of the same axis, and the correct answer to a question about acute malnutrition. *Body mass index charts* are used from about 2 years upward mainly to classify overweight and obesity; `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` states that “Body mass index (BMI) is the most reliable and most commonly used clinical indicator of obesity”, with obese above the 95th centile, overweight 85th–95th and healthy 5th–85th. *Mid-arm circumference* is a rapid field screen for acute severe malnutrition — `16) P.E.M.txt` lists it under anthropometric data with a cut-off of 11.5 cm — and again reports wasting, not stunting.\n\n**Grounding.** `13) Short stature.txt` sets out which chart to use for whom: the WHO chart is “Recommended for 0-24 months”, the CDC chart “Recommended for 2-19 years”, both age- and sex-specific, with disease-specific charts for conditions such as Down syndrome. It also insists that “A single measurement is less important than growth trend”, so the answer is not merely *which chart* but *plotted serially*.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Short-for-age means look back — nutrition over years, chronic disease, an endocrine cause. Thin-for-height means look at the last few weeks — intake, losses, acute illness.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Stunting is low height-for-age and marks chronic undernutrition; wasting is low weight-for-height and marks acute undernutrition.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.30'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-2', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 4-year-old girl with Down syndrome, Saima, attends her routine annual follow-up with her mother. She is generally well, except for a history of constipation. On plotting her growth on a Down syndrome-specific chart, her height has dropped from the 75th to the 25th centile, while her weight has increased from the 50th to the 75th centile. Which of the following investigations is the most appropriate to perform?',
  options:['Lipid profile','Abdominal ultrasound','Full blood count','Thyroid function tests','Vitamin D levels'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children with Down syndrome are at high risk for hypothyroidism. The combination of growth faltering, weight gain, and constipation is classic for thyroid dysfunction.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the single rule that decides this question.** `13) Short stature.txt` prints the discriminator as a two-line rule, and it is worth memorising exactly as written:\n\n- “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease”\n- “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”\n\nSaima’s height has fallen two centile bands while her weight has *risen* one. That is the endocrine pattern, not the systemic one. The same slide adds the corollary under obesity: nutritional obesity gives a child who is “Obese + Tall”, whereas endocrine causes give “Obese + Short/poor height velocity”. A child growing fatter and shorter at the same time has a hormone problem until proved otherwise.\n\n**Why hypothyroidism specifically.** Two things converge. First, `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists the later problems of Down syndrome, which include short stature and a broad susceptibility to associated disease; autoimmune thyroid disease is the commonest acquired endocrinopathy in this group and is the reason Down syndrome children are screened for it periodically *(the routine-screening interval itself is not stated in the cached decks — not taken from the course material)*. Second, the accompanying symptom fits: `52) hypothyrodism.txt` lists constipation, lethargy and poor feeding among the presenting features, and describes the hypothyroid child as “Short with infantile proportions” with coarse features, a large tongue and an umbilical hernia.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Lipid profile** — dyslipidaemia is a *consequence* of untreated hypothyroidism, not the test that diagnoses it, and it changes nothing in a 4-year-old.\n- **Abdominal ultrasound** — reasonable if you suspected an obstructive or structural cause for constipation, but it cannot explain falling height with rising weight.\n- **Full blood count** — would be the move if she were losing weight or looked systemically unwell; anaemia and chronic disease depress weight *more* than height, which is the opposite pattern.\n- **Vitamin D levels** — rickets impairs linear growth, but it does not raise weight, and it is not the discriminating test here.\n\n**A second Down-syndrome question sits in this section and keys differently.** The bank prints a near-identical vignette in which a Down syndrome child drops **one** centile in height but **two** in weight, and there the key is a coeliac screen. The direction of the weight line is the entire difference: weight falling faster than height sends you to gut and systemic disease, weight rising while height falls sends you to the thyroid. Read the weight before you answer.\n\n**Filing note.** Filed under growth and puberty because the question is answered by reading a growth chart; the secondary chapter is **endocrine**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Height falling while weight rises is the endocrine growth pattern — in Down syndrome, check thyroid function first.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.32'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-3', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which is a characteristic of constitutional growth delay?',
  options:['Adequate bone age','More common in girls','Anal height is affected','A delayed onset of puberty','Presentation is in infancy'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Constitutional growth delay presents with normal health, slowed growth, delayed bone age, and late puberty. It is more common in boys and improves with age.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note — the printed option C.** The bank prints option C as **“Anal height is affected”**, which is plainly a typo for **“Final height is affected”**; it is printed identically again later in the same section, so it is the source’s own error rather than a scan artefact. The option is reproduced here exactly as printed — a printed typo survives verbatim in this bank — and the meaning is unchanged: final height is *not* affected in constitutional delay, which is why C is wrong.\n\n**Expansion — constitutional delay of growth and puberty (CDGP) in one table.** This is the single most examined comparison in the chapter, and `13) Short stature.txt` devotes a slide to it:\n\n| | **Familial short stature** | **Constitutional delay (CDGP)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parents | Short — height sits within the mid-parental range | Normal height, but often a “late bloomer” parent |\n| Bone age | **Equal to chronological age** | **Delayed** (often by 2 years or more) |\n| Puberty | Normal timing | **Delayed** |\n| Growth velocity | Normal | Normal but low, then a late catch-up |\n| Final adult height | Short — as predicted by the parents | **Normal** |\n| Sex | Either | **Commoner in boys** |\n\nThe lecture’s Case 2 is exactly this question in clinical form — a 13-year-old boy, no puberty yet, father a “late bloomer”, Tanner stage 1, bone age delayed 2 years, normal labs — and the answer given is “Constitutional delay of growth and puberty”, managed by “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Adequate bone age** — the reverse of the truth. `13) Short stature.txt` states the bone-age rule outright: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.” A normal bone age argues *against* CDGP.\n- **More common in girls** — it is commoner in boys, which is also why the classic vignette is a teenage boy who is the shortest in his class.\n- **Final height is affected** — the defining reassurance of CDGP is that adult height is normal. Growth is postponed, not lost.\n- **Presentation is in infancy** — CDGP presents in **late childhood and adolescence**, when peers pull ahead. A growth problem dating from infancy points to a small-for-gestational-age baby, a syndrome, or chronic disease.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Delayed bone age + delayed puberty + normal parents + normal labs = wait, reassure, and offer short-course testosterone only if the psychological burden warrants it. Do not investigate for growth hormone deficiency first; `13) Short stature.txt` warns in bold: “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay = delayed bone age and delayed puberty in a boy with normal parents, and a normal final adult height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.34'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-4', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What would be the shape of the head if craniosynostosis affects the lambdoid suture?',
  options:['Wide and broad','Long and narrow','Prominent forehead','Flattening of the skull'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Localized Craniosynostosis: Sagittal suture fusion -> Long, narrow skull (scaphocephaly). Coronal suture fusion -> Asymmetrical skull (anterior plagiocephaly). Lambdoid suture fusion -> Posterior flattening of the skull (posterior plagiocephaly).\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the whole map, because the examiner rotates the suture.** Craniosynostosis is premature fusion of a cranial suture. The skull cannot grow *across* a fused suture, so it compensates by growing *along* it — that single principle generates every named shape, and it is worth reasoning from rather than memorising:\n\n| Suture fused | Growth blocked | Resulting shape | Name |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Sagittal** (front-to-back, midline) | Side-to-side widening | Long and narrow | **Scaphocephaly** (dolichocephaly) |\n| **Coronal**, one side | Forward growth on that side | Asymmetrical, flattened forehead on the fused side | **Anterior plagiocephaly** |\n| **Coronal**, both sides | Forward growth altogether | Short, wide, tall skull with a prominent forehead | **Brachycephaly** |\n| **Lambdoid** | Backward growth | **Flattening of the occiput** | **Posterior plagiocephaly** |\n| **Metopic** | Forehead widening | Triangular, keel-shaped forehead | **Trigonocephaly** |\n\n*(The metopic and bilateral-coronal rows are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the endpoint file’s own box covers only the sagittal, coronal and lambdoid rows.)*\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Wide and broad* is bilateral coronal fusion (brachycephaly). *Long and narrow* is sagittal fusion (scaphocephaly) — by far the commonest single-suture synostosis, and the trap answer because it is the shape most students remember. *Prominent forehead* belongs to brachycephaly and to metopic fusion, not to the lambdoid suture, which sits at the back of the skull.\n\n**The clinical discrimination that matters more than the names.** True lambdoid synostosis is **rare**; the overwhelming majority of flat occiputs in infancy are **positional (deformational) plagiocephaly** from lying supine, which is benign, needs no surgery, and resolves with repositioning and tummy time. The distinction is made on the shape of the whole head from above: positional flattening pushes the ipsilateral ear and forehead *forward* (a parallelogram), while true lambdoid synostosis pulls the ipsilateral ear *backward and down* with a ridge over the suture (a trapezoid) *(not taken from the course material)*. `1) Normal development.txt` illustrates “TOTAL CRANIOSTENOSIS” among the conditions recognisable from the face, so the syllabus expects the entity to be recognised, but it prints no suture-by-suture table.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Fused suture → skull grows parallel to it. Lambdoid is posterior, so the deficit is posterior — a flat occiput.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A fused suture blocks growth across it, so lambdoid synostosis flattens the occiput (posterior plagiocephaly).',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.36'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-5', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'How is a very low birth weight infant defined?',
  options:['Birth weight less than 2500 g','Birth weight less than 1500 g','Birth weight less than 1750 g','Birth weight less than 1000 g','Birth weight less than 500'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Classification: Low Birth Weight (LBW): < 2500 g. Very Low Birth Weight (VLBW): < 1500 g. Extremely Low Birth Weight (ELBW): < 1000 g.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note.** On this printing option E reads “Birth weight less than 500” with no unit; the bank reprints the same question later in the section with “less than 500 g”, and the complete form is used here.\n\n**Expansion — the three definitions, and the two other axes they are always confused with.** `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` prints the birth-weight classification exactly as the box gives it:\n\n- “LBW - < 2500 grams · VLBW - < 1500 grams · ELBW - < 1000 grams”\n\nThe examiner’s favourite manoeuvre is to mix this axis with the other two ways a newborn is classified, so hold all three apart:\n\n| Axis | Categories |\n|---|---|\n| **Birth weight** | LBW < 2500 g · VLBW < 1500 g · ELBW < 1000 g |\n| **Gestational age** | Preterm < 37 weeks · Term 37–42 weeks · Post-term > 42 weeks |\n| **Weight for gestational age** | SGA · AGA · LGA |\n\nThese are independent. A 2200 g baby born at 40 weeks is LBW **and** term **and** SGA. A 2200 g baby born at 32 weeks is LBW **and** preterm **and** appropriate for gestational age. Answering a birth-weight question with a gestational-age category is the commonest error in this topic.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *< 2500 g* is plain LBW — the umbrella term, and the trap for anyone who has learned only one number. *< 1750 g* is not a recognised category at all; it is a manufactured distractor sitting between the two real ones. *< 1000 g* is ELBW. *< 500 g* is below the usual threshold of viability and is not a defined class.\n\n**Why the categories exist.** They are risk strata, not labels. `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` sets out what falls on the small preterm infant: respiratory distress syndrome type I (“Lung is the last organ to mature into a functional state”), apnoea of prematurity, patent ductus arteriosus, necrotising enterocolitis (“Incidence inversely related to GA”), intraventricular haemorrhage and periventricular leukomalacia — “Both are usually silent in presentation” — hypothermia from a large surface area and thin skin, hypoglycaemia from low glycogen stores, hypocalcaemia from an immature parathyroid, retinopathy of prematurity, anaemia and hyperbilirubinaemia. The lower the weight band, the more of that list applies.\n\n**Filing note.** Kept in growth and puberty because it is a growth-measurement definition and the bank prints it here; the secondary chapter is **neonatal**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'LBW < 2500 g, VLBW < 1500 g, ELBW < 1000 g — a weight axis independent of gestational age and of SGA/AGA/LGA.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.38'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-6', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 2-year-old girl is brought to the clinic for a routine check-up. Her mother states that she does not like to eat but prefers eating only a few spoons of pasta each day. What is the best method to check her nutritional status?',
  options:['Head circumference','Height','Weight','Weight-for-height','Skinfold thickness'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Weight-for-height reflects acute malnutrition (wasting), unlike height-for-age (chronic). It is the best tool to assess current nutritional status in toddlers.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note.** On this page the explanation box is laid over the end of the last line and option E prints as “Skinfold thicknes”. The bank reprints the same question later in the section clear of the box, where the option reads “Skinfold thickness”; the complete form is used here.\n\n**Expansion — why a *ratio* beats any single measurement.** A single number cannot separate a small-but-healthy child from a starving one. A 2-year-old on the 3rd centile for weight may be perfectly nourished if she is also on the 3rd centile for height. Weight-for-height removes body size from the equation and asks the only question that matters acutely: **is there enough flesh on this skeleton?**\n\n| Index | What it detects | What it misses |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Weight alone** | Nothing on its own — needs a reference | Cannot separate small from thin |\n| **Height alone** | Stunting, i.e. chronic undernutrition | Blind to acute wasting |\n| **Weight-for-height** | **Wasting — acute undernutrition** | Blind to chronicity (a stunted child can be proportionate) |\n| **Head circumference** | Brain growth | Nutrition, except in prolonged severe deprivation |\n| **Skinfold thickness** | Body fat reserve | Poor reproducibility; observer-dependent |\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Head circumference* answers a different question altogether — the endpoint file’s own summary slide states that occipitofrontal circumference “Reflects brain growth” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.8), and it is preserved until malnutrition is severe and prolonged. *Height* is the stunting measure and moves far too slowly to report a few weeks of poor intake. *Weight* on its own is uninterpretable without a reference for size. *Skinfold thickness* is a genuine measure of fat stores and `16) P.E.M.txt` lists it under anthropometric data (“Others: skin fold thickness, etc....”), but it needs calipers, training and an experienced observer, so it is a research and specialist tool rather than the best clinic method.\n\n**Where this sits in the malnutrition framework.** `16) P.E.M.txt` grades protein–energy malnutrition by the Wellcome classification on weight as a percentage of the 50th centile: 60–80% without oedema is underweight (mild to moderate), below 60% without oedema is **marasmus**, 60–80% *with* oedema is **kwashiorkor**, and below 60% with oedema is **marasmic-kwashiorkor**. The same lecture lists the anthropometric panel as “Length / Height (use percentile charts), Weight for age & weight for height, Head, chest & abdominal circumf., Mid-arm circumference (11.5 cm)”.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A fussy toddler with normal weight-for-height is a feeding-behaviour problem, not a nutritional one — and that distinction changes the consultation from investigation to reassurance and dietary advice.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Weight-for-height is the clinic measure of acute nutritional status; height-for-age reports chronicity instead.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.40'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-7', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'When evaluating a child for stunting, which growth chart will you use?',
  options:['Height-for-age charts','Weight-for-age charts','Weight-for-length charts','Body mass index charts','Mid-arm circumference measurements'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Stunting reflects chronic undernutrition, which primarily affects linear growth (height) rather than just weight.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in the same section — once near the start and again here — with the options in Title Case and the stem reworded from “which growth chart you will use” to “which growth chart will you use”. Same five options, same key, a differently worded box. Both printings are staged and held as separate entries in this bank.\n\n**Expansion — the four-cell grid the examiner draws from.** Every anthropometric question in this section can be answered from one grid: pick the numerator, pick the denominator.\n\n| Index | Numerator ÷ denominator | Names the condition | Time course |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Height-for-age | height ÷ age | **Stunting** | Chronic |\n| Weight-for-height | weight ÷ height | **Wasting** | Acute |\n| Weight-for-age | weight ÷ age | **Underweight** | Mixed, non-specific |\n| BMI-for-age | weight ÷ height² ÷ age | **Overweight / obesity** | Chronic |\n\n“Stunting” names the *height* axis, so the denominator must be age and the numerator must be height. No other option can be right by definition.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Weight-for-age* is the underweight chart and cannot distinguish short from thin — the Wellcome classification in `16) P.E.M.txt` is built on it precisely because it is a blunt population screen. *Weight-for-length* is the wasting chart, the acute counterpart. *BMI charts* are the obesity instrument; `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` states that BMI “is the most reliable and most commonly used clinical indicator of obesity”, that it is “age- and sex-specific” in children, and gives the cut-offs — obese above the 95th centile, overweight 85th–95th, healthy 5th–85th. *Mid-arm circumference* is the rapid screen for acute severe malnutrition (`16) P.E.M.txt`, cut-off 11.5 cm) and again reports wasting.\n\n**A practical point the lecture insists on.** `13) Short stature.txt` requires that the right chart also be the right *reference*: WHO charts for 0–24 months (based on breast-fed infants), CDC charts for 2–19 years, and disease-specific charts where they exist — a Down syndrome child must be plotted on a Down syndrome chart, or normal syndromic short stature will be read as pathology. It also states the interpretive rule that outranks any single plot: “A single measurement is less important than growth trend.”\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Short for age = look for months or years of undernutrition, chronic disease, or an endocrine cause. One height on one visit tells you very little; two heights six months apart tell you almost everything.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Stunting is assessed on height-for-age charts, plotted serially and against the right reference for the child.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.42'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-8', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 7-year-old boy is brought by his grandmother to the pediatrician due to concerns about his growth. According to her, the boy’s mother is of average height, while the father is slightly shorter than the mother. On examination, the boy is happy, playful, and shows no dysmorphic features. His height is 110 cm, just below the 3rd centile, and his weight is on the 3rd centile. Physical examination is otherwise normal. What is the most likely cause of his short stature?',
  options:['Achondroplasia','Constitutional delay of growth and puberty','Familial short stature','Growth hormone deficiency','Vitamin D deficiency'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> He has proportionate height and weight both at low centiles, no dysmorphism, and a short parent -> consistent with familial short stature.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this vignette is the lecture’s own Case 1.** `13) Short stature.txt` prints it almost word for word: an 11-year-old boy, “Height 3rd centile, Normal growth velocity, Parents short, MPH= 165 cm, Normal proportions, Normal puberty stage, Bone age = chronological age”, and the answer given is **“Familial short stature”**, with the accompanying questions — is this pathological? do we investigate? — both answered no.\n\nFour features in this boy’s history do the work:\n\n1. **Height and weight are on the same low centile.** Proportionate smallness means the child is a small *normal* child, not a sick one. The lecture’s discriminator is explicit: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine.” Neither applies here.\n2. **No dysmorphic features.** Examination in short stature is a search for syndromes; `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Dysmorphic features” and “Skeletal abnormalities” as specific things to look for, and names Turner, Russell–Silver, Down and Noonan as the syndromic causes.\n3. **The parents are short.** Short parents give a low mid-parental height, and a child tracking within about two centile spaces of it is growing to plan.\n4. **He is well.** Happy, playful, normal examination — chronic disease and psychosocial deprivation both leave marks that are absent.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Achondroplasia** — a *disproportionate* short stature: short limbs with a relatively long trunk, macrocephaly, frontal bossing and a depressed nasal bridge. `13) Short stature.txt` gives it its own slide under “Disproportionate short stature”. This boy is proportionate.\n- **Constitutional delay of growth and puberty** — possible in a boy, but CDGP is defined by a **delayed bone age** and **delayed puberty**, and it typically declares itself in adolescence when peers begin their growth spurt. At 7 with short parents, familial is the better fit. The two are separated by one X-ray: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.”\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — would show *falling* centiles and a poor height velocity. `13) Short stature.txt` gives the threshold for even investigating it: “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean), Height affected > Weight, Height >1.5 SD below MPH, Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year).” None is met.\n- **Vitamin D deficiency** — rickets is a systemic disease with bone signs (widened wrists, rachitic rosary, bowing) and a poor velocity, not a well child tracking a low centile.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Short but proportionate + well + short parents + tracking the centile = a normal variant. `13) Short stature.txt` closes with the same message: “Most short children are normal variants.”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Proportionate short stature in a well child with short parents, tracking a low centile, is familial short stature.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.44'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-9', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the most important factor affecting growth during the "infantile phase" in humans?',
  options:['Maternal nutrition','Infant nutrition','Size at birth','Vitamins','Parental size'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> During infancy, nutrition is the main driver of growth, unlike later childhood (GH-driven) or fetal life (placental/maternal factors).\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the four phases of growth and what drives each.** This is the backbone of the whole chapter, and the bank returns to it repeatedly with different option sets, so learn the drivers rather than any one answer:\n\n| Phase | Timing | Main driver | Share of final height |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fetal** | Conception to birth | Placental function, maternal nutrition and uterine environment | **30%** — and the fastest growth velocity of life |\n| **Infantile** | Birth to about 2 years | **Nutrition** (with thyroid hormone and insulin permissive) | **15%** |\n| **Childhood** | ~2 years to puberty | **Growth hormone / IGF-1**, with thyroid hormone | ~40% |\n| **Pubertal** | Puberty to epiphyseal fusion | **Sex steroids** — testosterone and oestradiol | **15%** |\n\nThe endpoint file’s own summary slides give two of these figures directly: “Fetal phase (30% of final height, Fastest period of Growth)” and “Infantile phase (15% of final height)” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7). `14) Puberty.txt` supplies the pubertal share: the growth spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, with “Estrogen more than androgens”, and it “Add[s] 15% to final adult height” before “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. `13) Short stature.txt` lists the hormonal set for childhood growth as GH/IGF-1, thyroid hormone, insulin, sex steroids and glucocorticoids.\n\n**Why the answer is *infant* nutrition and not *maternal* nutrition.** The distinction is the whole question. Maternal nutrition governs the **fetal** phase, through the placenta. Once the cord is cut the determinant becomes what actually reaches the baby’s gut — breast milk or formula, in adequate volume, absorbed. That is why growth faltering clusters in infancy, and why the first response to a faltering infant is a feeding history rather than a hormone assay.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Maternal nutrition* is the fetal-phase driver. *Size at birth* sets the starting point and predicts catch-up (or its absence — `13) Short stature.txt` notes that “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”), but it does not drive the *rate* of infant growth. *Vitamins* matter only where a specific deficiency exists, such as vitamin D; they are not the main determinant of normal infant growth. *Parental size* becomes visible during the childhood phase, when a baby “finds” the centile predicted by mid-parental height, and it is the reason infants legitimately cross centiles early on.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A faltering infant is a nutritional problem until proved otherwise. A faltering 6-year-old with a normal appetite is far more likely to be endocrine.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Growth is driven by maternal/placental factors in fetal life, nutrition in infancy, GH in childhood and sex steroids in puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.46'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-10', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the main drive for growth in the childhood phase?',
  options:['Nutrition','Growth hormone','Testosterone','Thyroid hormones','Testosterone and estrogen'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> From 2 years to puberty, growth depends mostly on GH. Pubertal hormones dominate later.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ The bank prints this question three times in this section**, always with the same five options and the same key, once with “drive” and once with “driver” in the stem. It is held once in the app.\n\n**Expansion — the childhood phase belongs to the GH/IGF-1 axis.** From about 2 years until the pubertal growth spurt, height accrues steadily at roughly 5–7 cm a year, and the pacemaker is growth hormone acting largely through hepatic IGF-1. `13) Short stature.txt` devotes a slide to the “GH/IGF-1 axis” and lists the hormonal influences on childhood growth as “GH/IGF-1, Thyroid hormone, Insulin, Sex steroids, Glucocorticoids”. It also tells you how the axis is interrogated, and in what order:\n\n| Step | Tests |\n|---|---|\n| **Step One** | CBC, ESR, renal functions, electrolytes, LFT |\n| **Step Two** | TSH, fT4, coeliac screen, **IGF-1 and IGFBP-3**, **bone age**, **karyotyping (all girls)** |\n| **Step Three** | **GH testing** — provocation |\n\nwith the warning printed across the slide: “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n\n**Why growth hormone and not the other four.** *Nutrition* is the infantile driver; by school age a well child in a food-secure home is no longer nutrition-limited, which is why an isolated fall in height with a preserved weight in this age group points at hormones rather than intake. *Testosterone* and *testosterone and estrogen* belong to the **pubertal** phase — `14) Puberty.txt` states that the pubertal spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, that oestrogen contributes “more than androgens”, and that these steroids ultimately close the epiphyses. Offering them for the childhood phase is a phase error. *Thyroid hormones* are the strongest distractor and are genuinely required — hypothyroidism is a classic cause of childhood growth failure, and `52) hypothyrodism.txt` opens by emphasising “linear growth and brain” development — but thyroid hormone is **permissive**: it is needed for GH to work, and its deficiency causes growth failure, yet it does not set the childhood growth rate. GH does.\n\n**How GH deficiency actually presents.** `13) Short stature.txt` gives the profile: severe short stature more than 3 SD below the mean, height affected more than weight, a poor growth velocity below 4 cm per year, low IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, a delayed bone age, and confirmation by GH provocation — because “No use of basal GH level ! GH secreted in pulses”, requiring two provocation tests (glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine). It also lists the red flags for an underlying cause: neonatal hypoglycaemia, prolonged jaundice and microphallus; craniofacial abnormalities; cranial irradiation, head trauma or CNS infection; consanguinity or an affected relative — with craniopharyngioma, “a developmental tumor arising from squamous remnant of Rathke’s pouch”, accounting for about 4%.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Phase tells you which hormone to suspect. Infancy → food. Childhood → GH (and thyroid). Puberty → sex steroids.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Childhood-phase growth is driven by growth hormone acting through IGF-1, with thyroid hormone permissive.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.48'
},
{
  id:'pedep-gp-11', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following statements regarding measuring and plotting children’s growth is true?',
  options:['Correction for prematurity should be made up to the age of 6 months','Final height usually approximates the father’s height','Once a child is 5 years old, height does not usually deviate from the centile line','It is normal for an infant’s weight to cross centiles in the first months of life'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Early infant growth is variable, so centile crossing is common. By age 5, growth usually stabilizes along a centile.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — each of the four statements, taken in turn.**\n\n**D is true — early centile crossing is normal.** Birth weight is set largely by the intrauterine environment; the centile a baby will actually track is set by genetics. In the first months the infant therefore migrates from one to the other, upward if the baby was constitutionally small in utero (catch-up) or downward if the baby was constitutionally large (catch-down). This is why a single crossing in early infancy in a well, feeding baby is not growth faltering. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` puts a threshold on when crossing *does* matter: faltering growth is “present if an infant’s weight falls across two centile spaces” — and it insists that faltering growth is a “Description, not a diagnosis”.\n\n**A is false — but only just, and the qualifier is what the examiner wants.** `13) Short stature.txt` prints the rule for correcting for gestational age as: “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age.” `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` states the developmental counterpart plainly: “Correction is not required after age of 2 years.” Six months is too short on either statement.\n\n**B is false — final height follows the *mid-parental* height, not the father’s.** Mid-parental height is calculated from both parents (`13) Short stature.txt` lists “Mid-parental height calculation” under history, and defines short stature partly as “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”). Taking the father alone would systematically over-predict a daughter’s height and mis-predict any child of parents with dissimilar heights.\n\n**C is false as an absolute, and it is the most tempting distractor** because it is *nearly* true. After the early infant migration, a child does normally track a centile — but growth can and does deviate after 5 years, and when it does it is a red flag, not a normal event. That is exactly the situation the rest of this chapter tests: a child falling from the 50th to below the 3rd centile between 2 and 3 years, or a 7-year-old dropping from the 15th to the 5th. `13) Short stature.txt` includes “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” among the *definitions* of short stature, which is only meaningful because crossing is abnormal at that age rather than impossible.\n\n**The interpretive rule underneath all four.** “A single measurement is less important than growth trend” (`13) Short stature.txt`). Every statement here is really a statement about the trend line, and the discipline being taught is to plot serially on the correct chart — WHO for 0–24 months, CDC for 2–19 years, and a disease-specific chart where one exists.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Crossing centiles in the first few months: watch. Crossing centiles after the toddler years: investigate.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Centile crossing is normal in early infancy and abnormal in later childhood; final height follows mid-parental, not paternal, height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.50'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-12', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following is a character of familial short stature?',
  options:['Height falls within 2 centile spaces of mid-parental height','More common in girls','Delayed onset of puberty','Presentation is in adolescence'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children with familial short stature track a low centile consistent with genetic potential. Puberty is normal and not delayed.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — familial short stature is a *diagnosis of concordance*.** The child is short, and the child is short **in proportion to the parents**. That is the entire concept, and the mid-parental height is the instrument that measures it. `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Mid-parental height calculation” as a required part of the history, and defines short stature in part as “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height” — the mirror image of this option, which says the height falls *within* the expected band.\n\n| | **Familial short stature** | **Constitutional delay (CDGP)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parental height | **Short** | Usually normal, often a late-developing parent |\n| Height vs mid-parental height | **Concordant** | Discordant, then catches up |\n| Bone age | **Equal to chronological age** | **Delayed** |\n| Puberty | **Normal timing** | **Delayed** |\n| Age at presentation | Any — usually pre-school or school entry | **Adolescence** |\n| Sex | Either | **Boys** |\n| Final adult height | **Short**, as predicted | **Normal** |\n\nThe lecture’s Case 1 is the clinical form of the correct option: an 11-year-old boy on the 3rd centile with normal growth velocity, short parents, MPH 165 cm, normal proportions, normal puberty stage and a bone age equal to chronological age — answered as “Familial short stature”, with no investigation required.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *More common in girls* — familial short stature has no sex predilection; it is **constitutional delay** that is commoner in boys, and this option is lifted straight from the CDGP column to catch a student who has learned the two lists as one. *Delayed onset of puberty* is the defining feature of CDGP and is precisely what is **not** seen in familial short stature; a short child who enters puberty on time and remains short is short because that is the family’s height. *Presentation is in adolescence* is again CDGP: a short child with short parents is usually noticed early, often at school entry when height is compared with a whole class, whereas the constitutionally delayed child looks unremarkable until peers begin their pubertal spurt and pull away.\n\n**The single investigation that separates them.** A bone age on an X-ray of the left wrist and hand, read against Greulich and Pyle. `13) Short stature.txt` gives the rule in two lines: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.” Note that a delayed bone age does not by itself mean CDGP — endocrine disease delays it too, which is why the thyroid and the GH/IGF-1 axis sit in the same investigation step.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Short parents + normal bone age + normal puberty + tracking the centile = a normal variant that needs plotting, not testing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Familial short stature is height concordant with mid-parental height, with a normal bone age and normally timed puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.52'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-13', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 13-year-old girl is brought to the clinic for concerns about delayed puberty. She is in the 10th percentile for height and 25th percentile for weight. Physical examination reveals no signs of puberty, and her bone age is mildly delayed. Her mother also had a past history of delayed puberty. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Turner syndrome','Constitutional growth delay','Growth hormone deficiency','Hypothyroidism','Familial short stature'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Delayed puberty + mildly delayed bone age + family history = constitutional growth delay, a benign variant.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — first, is this delayed puberty at all?** `14) Puberty.txt` gives the definition to apply before anything else: delayed puberty is “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by 13 yrs of age in females or by 14 yrs of age in males.” At 13 with no pubertal signs, this girl has just crossed that line, which is what makes the consultation legitimate.\n\n**Three findings then converge on constitutional delay.**\n\n1. **A delayed bone age.** `13) Short stature.txt`: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.” The delay tells you her skeleton is younger than her birth certificate — she has more growing time left than her age suggests, which is the whole reason her final height will be normal.\n2. **A family history of delayed puberty.** CDGP runs in families; the lecture’s Case 2 makes the point with a father who was a “late bloomer”.\n3. **Her height and weight are only modestly reduced and are proportionate** — 10th and 25th centile. She is not severely short, and weight is not falling behind height, so neither the systemic nor the endocrine pattern of `13) Short stature.txt` (“Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”) applies.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Turner syndrome** — the most important thing to exclude in *any* short girl, and `13) Short stature.txt` puts “Karyotyping (All girls)” in its investigation ladder for that reason. But Turner is **hypergonadotropic** hypogonadism: gonadotrophins are raised, and there is usually severe short stature well below the 3rd centile plus stigmata — `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists neck webbing, widely spaced nipples, a wide carrying angle, lymphoedema of the hands and feet, spoon-shaped nails and coarctation of the aorta. A girl on the 10th centile with no stigmata and a *delayed* bone age fits CDGP far better.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — would give severe short stature (more than 3 SD below the mean by the lecture’s own threshold), height affected more than weight, and a growth velocity under 4 cm a year. It also does not classically run in the family as delayed *puberty*.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — gives the endocrine pattern with weight normal or rising while height falls, plus constipation, lethargy and cold intolerance (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`). This girl’s weight centile sits *above* her height centile only modestly and she is otherwise well.\n- **Familial short stature** — would give a **normal** bone age and **normally timed** puberty. The delayed bone age alone excludes it.\n\n**Management, because the examiner asks it next.** `13) Short stature.txt` answers the Case 2 management question directly: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” In girls the equivalent is a short course of low-dose oestrogen where the psychological burden justifies it *(the female regimen is not stated in the cached decks — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Delayed puberty with a delayed bone age and a family history of late puberty is constitutional delay, and needs reassurance not investigation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.54'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-14', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 4-month-old infant is brought in for concerns about small size. The infant was born at 32 weeks gestation and has been primarily formula-fed. Which of the following is the first most important factor to consider in managing this infant’s growth?',
  options:['Adjusting for corrected gestational age','Introducing solid foods early','Switching to a higher-calorie formula','Recommending breastfeeding'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> For preterm babies, growth assessment must use corrected age (chronological age – weeks of prematurity) until about 2 years.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Note on the box and the lectures.** The box states the correction applies “until about 2 years”. `13) Short stature.txt` is more precise and slightly narrower: correction for gestational age is “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age.” `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` gives the developmental counterpart as “Correction is not required after age of 2 years.” The bank states the wider rule without the qualifier; the lectures’ version is the one to reproduce in an examination that asks for the duration, and the box is not wrong so much as unqualified.\n\n**Expansion — do the arithmetic before you do anything else.** This baby was born 8 weeks early. At a chronological age of 4 months (about 17 weeks), the corrected age is roughly **2 months**. Plotting a 2-month-old on a 4-month-old’s chart manufactures growth failure out of nothing, and the intervention that follows — extra calories, early solids, investigation — is then a response to an artefact. `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` makes the same point for development: “The anticipated developmental skills of a 9-month-old baby (chronological age) born 3 months early at 28 weeks’ gestation are more like those of a 6-month-old baby (corrected age).”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong, and why they are wrong in this *order*.**\n\n- **Introducing solid foods early** — actively harmful here. `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` places complementary feeding “after 6 months”, with pureed, mashed and semisolid foods at 6 months, finger foods at 8 months and family foods at 12 months. At a corrected age of 2 months this infant is neurologically nowhere near able to handle solids, and early introduction risks choking and displaces milk calories.\n- **Switching to a higher-calorie formula** — a legitimate tool in a genuinely faltering preterm infant, but it is a treatment, and you have not yet established that there is anything to treat. Doing this before correcting for gestational age is treating the chart rather than the child.\n- **Recommending breastfeeding** — breast milk is preferred, and the lecture material devotes a whole deck to it, but at 4 months of formula feeding this is neither the immediate step nor a realistic single intervention, and it does not address the question asked.\n\n**When correction has been applied and the baby is *still* small.** Then the standard faltering-growth approach begins. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines faltering as a weight fall “across two centile spaces”, lists the red flags — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — and gives management as “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with “Investigations should be guided by history and examination.”\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Correct the age, re-plot, then decide. Most “small preterms” are the right size for their corrected age.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Plot preterm infants at corrected age before calling growth abnormal — through the first year, and to 2 years if extremely preterm.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.56'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-15', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the fastest period of growth in a normal human?',
  options:['Fetal','Infantile','Childhood','Prepubertal','Pubertal'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The fetal period has the highest growth velocity, with rapid cell division and tissue growth. Postnatal phases are slower.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ The bank contradicts itself on this fact, and the contradiction is worth knowing.** Elsewhere in this same section the endpoint file asks “Which is the fastest period of growth throughout life?” over a four-option list that **does not offer *Fetal* at all** (Infancy / Childhood / Puberty / Adolescence), keys **Infancy**, and prints a box claiming infancy surpasses the fetal period. That claim is wrong on the file’s own evidence: the present question keys **Fetal**, the bank prints this same Fetal-keyed question a second time later in the section, and the file’s own summary slide states “Fetal phase (30% of final height, **Fastest period of Growth**)” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7). Both keys are reproduced as printed, per the rule that the bank’s printed key is what the entry carries; the discrepancy is recorded here so that a learner meeting the other question knows why its key differs. In an examination, answer **fetal** whenever fetal is on the list, and **infancy** only when it is not.\n\n**Expansion — the velocity curve, in numbers.** Growth velocity is highest before birth and falls almost continuously afterwards, with one interruption:\n\n| Phase | Approximate velocity | Share of final height |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fetal** | Fastest of life — the second trimester carries the peak linear velocity | **30%** |\n| **Infantile** (0–2 y) | Very rapid but decelerating: length rises steeply in year one, then slows | **15%** |\n| **Childhood** (2 y–puberty) | Slowest and steadiest, roughly 5–7 cm/year | ~40% |\n| **Pubertal** | A second peak — the growth spurt — then epiphyseal fusion | **15%** |\n\n*(The centimetre-per-year figures are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the cached decks give the percentage shares and the drivers, not the velocities.)*\n\n**The pubertal spurt is a *second* peak, not the highest one.** `14) Puberty.txt` states that the spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, that oestrogen contributes “more than androgens”, and that it “Add[s] 15% to final adult height” before “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. It also notes that “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys” — which is why girls are temporarily taller in early adolescence and why boys, given two extra pre-pubertal years of childhood growth, end taller.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Infantile* is the fastest **postnatal** phase and is the answer only when fetal is not offered. *Childhood* is the slowest phase. *Prepubertal* is the tail of childhood — slower still, immediately before the spurt. *Pubertal* is the second peak, and it is the answer to “fastest **postnatal**” only in the sense of an acceleration, never in absolute terms against fetal life.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The fetal period has the highest growth velocity of life and supplies 30% of final height; puberty is a second, smaller peak.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.58'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-16', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 3-year-old boy’s height has fallen from the 50th percentile to below the 3rd percentile over the past year. What is the most likely cause?',
  options:['Familial short stature','Constitutional growth delay','Growth hormone deficiency','Turner syndrome'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Crossing down centiles is always abnormal. A significant drop in height (not weight) is most suggestive of GH deficiency.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the discriminator is *crossing*, not *being short*.** Two of the four options describe children who are short but who have always been short; only the pathological causes make a child *become* short. `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” among the definitions of short stature, alongside “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging” — both of which are statements about the slope of the line rather than its position. A drop from the 50th to below the 3rd centile in one year is a catastrophic loss of velocity and cannot be a normal variant.\n\n**Why growth hormone deficiency.** The lecture gives the indications for suspecting it, and this boy meets three of the four: “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); **Height affected > Weight**; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; **Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)**.” The height-not-weight pattern is the same rule that runs through the whole chapter — “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine.”\n\n**How it would be worked up.** Step One screening bloods (CBC, ESR, renal function, electrolytes, LFT), then Step Two — TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, **IGF-1 and IGFBP-3**, bone age, and karyotype in any girl — and only then Step Three, GH provocation testing. `13) Short stature.txt` is emphatic that a random GH level is useless: “No use of basal GH level ! GH secreted in pulses”, and “2 GH provocation tests (sequential or on separate days) are required”, using glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine. In deficiency you expect “Low levels of IGF-1 and IGFBP-3” and a “Delayed bone age”. Once confirmed, look for a cause: “MRI brain (pituitary view), Fundus: papilledema, Visual field assessment” — bitemporal hemianopia points at the optic chiasm and a craniopharyngioma, which accounts for about 4% and is “Not truly malignant, but locally invasive”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Familial short stature** — a child who has *always* tracked a low centile in line with short parents. It cannot produce a fall from the 50th centile.\n- **Constitutional growth delay** — declares itself in **adolescence**, not at 3, and its hallmark is delayed puberty with a delayed bone age; the growth line runs low but roughly parallel rather than plunging.\n- **Turner syndrome** — a real cause of falling height, and the option to take seriously in a **girl**. This patient is a boy, so it is excluded on sex alone. (Note how the bank sets the same trap in reverse elsewhere: “karyotyping (All girls)”.)\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Crossing centiles downward in a young child is pathological until proved otherwise. Ask first whether it is the weight line or the height line that is falling — that single observation splits systemic disease from endocrine disease.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A falling height centile with a preserved weight centile is the endocrine pattern — in a young child, suspect GH deficiency.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.60'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-17', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following statements is FALSE as regards growth in males?',
  options:['Adult males are taller than adult females','Males have a longer childhood growth phase than females','The peak height at puberty is higher in males than in females','Growth stops earlier in males'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Growth stops later in males because puberty occurs later and lasts longer.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the whole sex difference in adult height comes from two facts, and this question tests both.**\n\n**Fact one: boys start puberty later.** `14) Puberty.txt` states it directly — “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys.” Because the childhood phase runs from about 2 years until the pubertal spurt begins, a later start means a **longer childhood phase** — which is option B, and it is true. Those extra pre-pubertal years are spent growing at 5–7 cm a year, and they are the larger half of the male height advantage.\n\n**Fact two: the male spurt is bigger.** The pubertal growth spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)” and adds about 15% to final adult height (`14) Puberty.txt`). In boys the peak height velocity is higher than in girls — option C, also true.\n\nPut together: boys grow for longer *and* spurt harder, so adult males are taller than adult females — option A, true. And because puberty starts later and takes longer to complete, **epiphyseal fusion happens later in boys**, so growth stops **later**, not earlier. Option D is therefore the false statement and the correct answer to a question that asks which statement is FALSE.\n\n| | **Girls** | **Boys** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Puberty onset | Earlier | **About 2 years later** |\n| Childhood phase | Shorter | **Longer** |\n| Peak height velocity | Lower | **Higher** |\n| Epiphyseal fusion | Earlier | **Later** |\n| Final adult height | Shorter | **Taller** |\n\n**The mechanism of stopping, because it is counter-intuitive.** Growth ends when the epiphyseal growth plates fuse, and `14) Puberty.txt` attributes that to the sex steroids themselves: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth.” The same hormones that drive the spurt also end it — which is exactly why untreated precocious puberty produces a tall child who becomes a short adult, and why GnRH agonists preserve final height by postponing that fusion.\n\n**Read the stem twice.** This is a negatively worded question (“FALSE”), and three of the four options are true statements. Marking a true statement because it *looks* right is the commonest way this item is lost.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In boys, later start + bigger spurt + later fusion = taller adults. Anything that brings puberty forward steals height by closing the plates early.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Boys enter puberty about two years later, grow for longer and fuse their epiphyses later — so male growth stops later, not earlier.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.62'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-18', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'You are giving a lecture to medical students about the treatment of precocious puberty and its importance in preserving height. You discuss certain population segments where treatment would be most indicated and beneficial. Which of the following patient populations would benefit most from GnRH agonist therapy?',
  options:['Younger children with rapidly progressing noncentral precocious puberty','Younger children with rapidly progressing central precocious puberty','Older children with rapidly progressing noncentral precocious puberty','Older children with rapidly progressing central precocious puberty','Younger boys with slowly progressing central precocious puberty'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> GnRH agonists are most effective in young children with fast-progressing central precocious puberty, as treatment delays bone maturation and helps preserve adult height.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — three separate decisions are hidden in this one option list.** Work through them in order and only one option survives.\n\n**Decision 1: central or peripheral?** A GnRH agonist works by continuously stimulating the pituitary GnRH receptor, which paradoxically **downregulates** it and switches off LH and FSH release. It therefore acts *only* on disease driven by the hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal axis. `14) Puberty.txt` defines central precocious puberty as “a result of the early activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis”. Peripheral (noncentral, gonadotrophin-independent) precocity is driven by sex steroid produced **downstream** of the pituitary — an adrenal or gonadal tumour, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, McCune–Albright syndrome, or an exogenous source such as a parent’s testosterone gel — and in that setting the gonadotrophins are already suppressed, so there is nothing for a GnRH agonist to switch off. **Options A and C are eliminated.**\n\n**Decision 2: younger or older?** The purpose of treatment is to preserve adult height by preventing early epiphyseal fusion — `14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth.” A younger child has far more growth remaining to protect, and a bone age that has not yet run away. In an older child close to fusion the height is largely already spent and treatment buys little. **Option D is eliminated.**\n\n**Decision 3: rapidly or slowly progressing?** Slowly progressing central precocity often behaves benignly, with a bone age that keeps pace with height and a normal predicted adult height; these children are observed rather than treated. **Option E is eliminated** — and note it is doubly wrong, being both slowly progressing and, in a boy, the group in which a structural cause must be hunted rather than a benign course assumed.\n\nWhat is left is **B: younger children with rapidly progressing central precocious puberty.**\n\n**Why boys deserve a second thought.** `14) Puberty.txt` gives the epidemiology that changes practice: central precocious puberty “occurs 5-10 folds more frequently in girls than in boys because ovaries are very sensitive to gonadotrophin secretion”; “A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty has been reported in girls (90%)”, whereas “structural central nervous system (CNS) abnormality can be demonstrated in up to 75% of boys”. So in a boy, central precocity is a brain scan until proved otherwise — the deck lists “MRI brain with contrast for pituitary gland in gonadotropin-dependent PP”, and warns that with brain tumours “The only sign is precocious puberty”, with neuroendocrine features such as diabetes insipidus, hyperthermia, gelastic seizures, obesity and cachexia, and visual field defects.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** GnRH agonist = central disease only, and the younger and faster the child, the more height there is to save.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'GnRH agonists work only in central precocious puberty, and benefit most the young child whose disease is progressing rapidly.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.64'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-19', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following is NOT associated with short stature?',
  options:['Chronic renal failure','Hypothyroidism','PEM','Thalassemia major','G6PD deficiency'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Conditions like chronic renal failure, hypothyroidism, PEM, and thalassemia major impair growth. G6PD deficiency only causes hemolysis during oxidative stress but does not affect linear growth.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the principle: growth fails when a disease is *continuous*.** Any illness that imposes a sustained metabolic, nutritional or endocrine burden costs height. G6PD deficiency is the exception on this list because it is **episodic**: the enzyme defect is silent until an oxidant challenge — fava beans, certain drugs, infection — precipitates an acute haemolytic crisis, after which the child returns to baseline. Between crises there is no chronic anaemia, no chronic inflammation, and therefore no growth cost.\n\n**Why each of the other four does impair growth.**\n\n- **Chronic renal failure** — a classic multi-mechanism cause: acidosis, renal osteodystrophy from disordered vitamin D and phosphate handling, anorexia and poor intake, anaemia, and GH resistance. `13) Short stature.txt` places “Renal functions” and “Electrolytes” in **Step One** of the investigation of short stature, which is exactly why: renal disease is common, silent, and treatable.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — the endocrine cause the chapter returns to repeatedly. `52) hypothyrodism.txt` opens with an emphasis on “linear growth and brain” development and describes the child as “Short with infantile proportions”, with constipation, lethargy, coarse features, a large tongue and an umbilical hernia. It produces the signature endocrine pattern of falling height with preserved or rising weight.\n- **PEM (protein–energy malnutrition)** — the commonest cause of poor growth worldwide. `16) P.E.M.txt` lists “Growth failure” as the first clinical feature of both marasmus (weight below 60% of the 50th centile, loss of subcutaneous fat, muscle wasting, no oedema) and kwashiorkor (weight 60–80% with oedema, hair and skin changes, hepatomegaly), and records among the consequences “Permanent delay in intellectual developement”.\n- **Thalassaemia major** — chronic severe anaemia with tissue hypoxia, a huge marrow expansion and metabolic demand, and — the part most often forgotten — **transfusional iron overload damaging the pituitary**, producing GH deficiency and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, so growth fails both from the anaemia and from an acquired endocrinopathy *(the iron-overload mechanism is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**How to attack an “NOT associated” stem.** Do not look for the odd one out by feel. Ask of each option: *is this disease continuously present, and does it cost energy, oxygen or hormone?* Four here answer yes. G6PD deficiency answers no.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Chronic disease of any organ system is a cause of short stature, which is why the first tier of investigation is a systemic screen — CBC, ESR, renal function, electrolytes, LFT — before any hormone is measured.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Continuous chronic disease costs height; an episodic disorder such as G6PD deficiency does not impair linear growth.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.66'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-20', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 6-month-old infant has an upper-to-lower segment ratio of 1.7:1. What does this indicate?',
  options:['Normal','Long trunk','Short trunk','Disproportionate limbs'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> At birth, the ratio is 1.7:1, gradually decreasing with age as limbs grow faster than the trunk. Thus, 1.7:1 at 6 months is expected and normal.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — what the segments are, and why the ratio moves.** The **lower segment** is measured from the top of the pubic symphysis to the floor; the **upper segment** is the total height (or length) minus the lower segment, and represents head plus trunk. A newborn is built head- and trunk-heavy — an expression of the cephalocaudal direction of growth that `1) Normal development.txt` lists as the first principle of development (“Directional Pattern: Cephalocaudal Pattern (Head to Tail)”). Through infancy and childhood the **limbs grow faster than the trunk**, so the ratio falls steadily:\n\n| Age | Approximate upper : lower segment ratio |\n|---|---|\n| Birth | **1.7 : 1** |\n| 3 years | About 1.3 : 1 |\n| 7–10 years | About **1 : 1** |\n| Adult | Slightly below 1 : 1 |\n\n*(The numeric ratios other than the one printed in the bank’s own box are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material. `13) Short stature.txt` requires the measurement but prints no table: under Examination it lists “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)” without giving values.)*\n\n**Why the measurement is taken at all — it splits short stature in two.** Every child with short stature is first classified as **proportionate** or **disproportionate**, and that single division reroutes the whole work-up:\n\n| | **Proportionate short stature** | **Disproportionate short stature** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Segments | Ratio appropriate for age | Ratio abnormal for age |\n| Typical causes | Familial, constitutional delay, endocrine (GH deficiency, hypothyroidism), chronic disease, malnutrition, Turner, SGA without catch-up | **Skeletal dysplasias** — achondroplasia and its relatives; rickets; spinal disease |\n| Next step | Growth chart, bone age, systemic screen, hormones | Skeletal survey and genetic assessment |\n\n`13) Short stature.txt` gives “Disproportionate short stature — Achondroplasia” its own slide, which is the entity this measurement exists to catch: short limbs against a relatively preserved trunk, giving a **persistently high** upper-to-lower ratio well past the age at which it should have fallen.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Long trunk* and *short trunk* both misread the ratio as a statement about the trunk in isolation; the ratio is a comparison, and at 6 months the value given is simply the expected one. *Disproportionate limbs* would be the answer if this ratio were found in an older child — 1.7:1 at seven years old would be strongly abnormal and would send you looking for a skeletal dysplasia — but in a 6-month-old it is normal, which is precisely the point of the question: **a segment ratio is only interpretable against age.**\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Measure the segments in every short child. A normal ratio for age keeps you on the common path; an abnormal one moves you straight to the skeleton.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The upper-to-lower segment ratio is about 1.7:1 at birth and falls to 1:1 by school age; it separates proportionate from disproportionate short stature.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.68'
},
{
  id:'pedep-gp-21', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Janine, a 9-month-old female infant, is seen by her family doctor because of concern that she is not growing fast enough. She is on the 5th centile for height and 2nd centile for weight. What is the greatest influence on her growth rate at this age?',
  options:['Genes','Growth hormone','Nutrition','Oestrogen','Testosterone'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> At 9 months of age, nutrition is the most important factor influencing growth. Poor intake or malabsorption can lead to weight and height centile drop despite normal hormones and genes.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the age in the stem is the answer.** Nine months places Janine squarely in the **infantile phase**, which runs from birth to about two years and is nutrition-driven. The endpoint file’s own summary slide gives it 15% of final height (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7), and the driver is what reaches the gut, not what the pituitary secretes.\n\n| Phase | Age | Driver |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fetal | to birth | Placenta, maternal nutrition, uterine environment |\n| **Infantile** | **birth to ~2 y** | **Nutrition** |\n| Childhood | ~2 y to puberty | Growth hormone / IGF-1 (thyroid permissive) |\n| Pubertal | puberty to fusion | Sex steroids — oestradiol and testosterone |\n\n**Note also which centile is lower.** Her weight (2nd) has fallen below her height (5th). `13) Short stature.txt` gives the rule: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine.” Weight leading the fall points to intake, losses or systemic illness — consistent with a nutritional cause and *against* an endocrine one, which reinforces the same answer from a second direction.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Genes** — genetic potential is real and becomes visible during the **childhood** phase, when an infant settles onto the centile predicted by mid-parental height. It is also precisely why early centile crossing is normal. But it does not set the *rate* of growth at 9 months, and it cannot be modified.\n- **Growth hormone** — the childhood driver. GH-dependent growth becomes established from around the second half of the first year onwards and dominates from about 2 years; congenital GH deficiency causes remarkably little growth failure at birth. `13) Short stature.txt` sets a high bar before GH is even investigated — severe short stature more than 3 SD below the mean, height affected more than weight, growth velocity under 4 cm a year — and warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n- **Oestrogen** and **testosterone** — pubertal hormones. `14) Puberty.txt` attributes the growth spurt to “GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, adding 15% to adult height and then closing the epiphyses. In a 9-month-old they are quiescent apart from the brief neonatal “mini-puberty” the same deck illustrates, which does not drive somatic growth.\n\n**What to do about Janine.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` frames the assessment: faltering growth is “Description, not a diagnosis”, present when weight falls “across two centile spaces”; ensure “accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart”; screen for the red flags — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, chronic respiratory signs, developmental delay; and manage with “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with investigations “guided by history and examination” rather than fired off as a panel.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In an infant, take a feeding history before you take blood.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Growth at 9 months is nutrition-driven; genes and growth hormone dominate later, and sex steroids only at puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.70'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-22', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'John, a 3-year-old boy, is referred to paediatric outpatients because of concern about the development of pubic and axillary hair. His testes are 1.5 mL in size (prepubertal). His blood pressure is 100/75 mmHg. Gonadotrophin levels are undetectable (prepubertal), but his bone age is 5 years. What is the most likely cause of his early pubertal development?',
  options:['Adrenal tumor','Brain tumor','Idiopathic precocious puberty','Prader-Willi syndrome','Testicular tumor'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Early pubic hair, advanced bone age, and undetectable gonadotropins suggest peripheral (gonadotropin-independent) precocious puberty. An adrenal tumor can produce androgens causing early virilization without testicular enlargement.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the testes are the switch, and everything else follows from them.** In a boy, testicular volume tells you whether the gonad is being *driven* or *bypassed*. `14) Puberty.txt` includes a slide on “Testicular examination in precocious puberty” for exactly this reason, and describes measurement by orchidometer; the deck’s Tanner description of genital stage II is “enlargement of the scrotum and testes”, so testicular growth is the **first** event of true male puberty.\n\n| | **Central (gonadotrophin-dependent)** | **Peripheral (gonadotrophin-independent)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanism | Early activation of the HPG axis | Sex steroid made **downstream** of the pituitary, or given exogenously |\n| LH / FSH | **Detectable / pubertal** | **Suppressed / undetectable** |\n| Testes in a boy | **Enlarged** (> 4 mL) | **Prepubertal** (small) — the androgen is not coming from them |\n| Sequence | Follows normal pubertal sequence | Out of sequence — virilisation without testicular growth |\n| Typical causes | Idiopathic (especially girls), CNS lesion (up to 75% of boys), prolonged untreated hypothyroidism | Adrenal tumour, CAH, testicular/ovarian tumour, McCune–Albright, **exogenous steroid** |\n| Responds to GnRH agonist | **Yes** | **No** |\n\nJohn has **pubic and axillary hair with 1.5 mL testes and undetectable gonadotrophins**. The androgen is therefore adrenal or exogenous, and among the options only an adrenal tumour supplies it. His bone age of 5 years against a chronological age of 3 confirms that the androgen exposure is real and sustained, because androgens accelerate skeletal maturation — the reason untreated precocity costs adult height.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Brain tumor** — would cause **central** precocity with **raised** gonadotrophins and **enlarged** testes. `14) Puberty.txt` warns that with brain tumours “The only sign is precocious puberty” and that “structural central nervous system (CNS) abnormality can be demonstrated in up to 75% of boys with central precocious puberty” — so this is the right instinct for the wrong biochemistry. Undetectable gonadotrophins exclude it.\n- **Idiopathic precocious puberty** — a diagnosis of exclusion within the **central** category, and one that is far commoner in girls (“A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty has been reported in girls (90%)”). It, too, would give detectable gonadotrophins and large testes.\n- **Prader-Willi syndrome** — the opposite phenotype: neonatal hypotonia and poor feeding, then hyperphagia and obesity, with **hypogonadism** and delayed or absent puberty. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` lists it under syndromic obesity.\n- **Testicular tumor** — would produce androgen from the gonad itself and give **asymmetrical testicular enlargement**, usually a palpable unilateral mass. Symmetrically small testes exclude it.\n\n**The investigation that follows.** Adrenal androgens and a 17-hydroxyprogesterone to exclude congenital adrenal hyperplasia, then adrenal imaging. `14) Puberty.txt` notes that atypical premature adrenarche — with marked growth acceleration, phallic enlargement, cystic acne or a bone age more than 2 SD advanced — requires an “ACTH STIMULATION TEST to exclude CAH”. John’s advanced bone age and true virilisation put him firmly in that atypical group rather than in benign premature adrenarche.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Virilisation with small testes means the androgen is not testicular. Look at the adrenal, at the medicine cabinet, and at 17-hydroxyprogesterone.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Virilisation with prepubertal testes and suppressed gonadotrophins is peripheral precocity — an adrenal or exogenous androgen source.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.72'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-23', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Chelsea, a 7-year-old girl, is brought by her mother to the paediatric clinic. Her mother is concerned about early puberty. Chelsea has developed breasts and recently some pubic hair. On examination, she has breast development stage 3 and pubic hair stage 2. She has not started her periods but has had a growth spurt. A pelvic ultrasound shows multicystic ovaries and an enlarging uterus. What is the most likely cause of her early pubertal development?',
  options:['Brain tumor','Congenital adrenal hyperplasia','Idiopathic precocious puberty','Ovarian tumor','Turner syndrome'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> True central precocious puberty is suggested by breast development, growth spurt, and estrogen-sensitive organ changes like multicystic ovaries and enlarged uterus. In girls, this is most commonly idiopathic.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — read the ultrasound, then read the sequence.** Two findings establish that this is **central** precocity, and one epidemiological fact then names it.\n\n**1. The pelvic ultrasound is the giveaway.** `14) Puberty.txt` sets out exactly what to look for: “Ultrasound examination of the ovaries and uterus helps us to assess the progress of puberty. Uterus: Infantile: tubular. Pubertal: pear-shaped. Endometrial lining identification.” **Multicystic ovaries** mean multiple follicles are being recruited — the signature of pulsatile gonadotrophin drive — and an **enlarging uterus** means sustained oestrogen exposure. An ovary driven by FSH is a central process by definition.\n\n**2. The sequence is normal, just early.** Breast development first, then pubic hair, then a growth spurt, with menarche still to come. `14) Puberty.txt` states that central precocious puberty “follows the sequence observed in normal puberty”. Peripheral causes characteristically break the sequence — virilisation without gonadal enlargement, or rapid isolated development.\n\n**3. In girls, central precocity is usually idiopathic.** The same deck: central precocious puberty “occurs 5-10 folds more frequently in girls than in boys because ovaries are very sensitive to gonadotrophin secretion”, and “A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty has been reported in girls (90%)”, against a structural CNS abnormality demonstrable “in up to 75% of boys”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Brain tumor** — the 10% that must still be excluded, and the deck insists on it: “MRI brain with contrast for pituitary gland in gonadotropin-dependent PP.” It would be the answer if there were headache, papilloedema, a visual field defect, diabetes insipidus, gelastic seizures, hyperthermia or unnatural crying or laughing. Chelsea has none, and in a girl of 7 the prior probability is against it — but *most likely* is not *certain*, and she still gets the scan.\n- **Congenital adrenal hyperplasia** — an adrenal androgen excess, so it virilises: pubic hair, acne, clitoromegaly, accelerated growth and bone age, **without** breast development and **without** ovarian follicular activity. Breast stage 3 is oestrogen, not androgen.\n- **Ovarian tumor** — an oestrogen-secreting tumour would suppress gonadotrophins and typically present with a **unilateral adnexal mass**, not bilateral multicystic ovaries, and often with rapid isolated breast development out of sequence.\n- **Turner syndrome** — the opposite problem. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` describes it as “a chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism”: gonadal dysgenesis gives **absent or delayed** puberty with **raised** gonadotrophins, plus short stature, neck webbing, widely spaced nipples and coarctation.\n\n**Management, and why it matters here.** Chelsea is young and progressing with a growth spurt already under way, so she is in exactly the group that benefits from a **GnRH agonist**: continuous receptor stimulation downregulates the axis, halting progression and — the point of the treatment — postponing epiphyseal fusion, since “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Multicystic ovaries with an enlarging uterus and a normal pubertal sequence mean central precocity — idiopathic in about 90% of girls, but still image the brain.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.74'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-24', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Sophia, an 18-month-old girl, is brought to outpatients by her mother, who is concerned as Sophia has developed breasts. She is otherwise well and growing normally. On examination, she has breast development stage 3 (BIII), but no pubic or axillary hair. Her bone age is 20 months. What is the most likely cause of her early pubertal development?',
  options:['Brain tumor','Congenital adrenal hyperplasia','Idiopathic precocious puberty','Premature thelarche','Premature puberty'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Sophia has isolated breast development without pubic or axillary hair and with normal growth and bone age. This is characteristic of premature thelarche, a benign and self-limiting condition in infants and toddlers. There are no signs to suggest true precocious puberty or hormonal pathology.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the word *isolated* is doing all the work.** `14) Puberty.txt` groups premature thelarche among the **“Benign incomplete variants”** of premature sexual development. The defining feature of an incomplete variant is that **one** pubertal sign appears **alone**, without the systemic consequences of true sex-steroid exposure. Three checks confirm it in Sophia:\n\n1. **No androgen signs** — no pubic or axillary hair, so the adrenal axis is quiet.\n2. **Normal growth** — no growth acceleration. True precocity produces a growth spurt.\n3. **Bone age 20 months at a chronological age of 18 months** — that is normal. Sustained oestrogen or androgen exposure **advances** bone age, and its absence is the strongest single piece of evidence that no meaningful hormone excess exists.\n\n**The three benign incomplete variants, side by side.**\n\n| | **Premature thelarche** | **Premature adrenarche / pubarche** | **Premature menarche** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sign | Isolated **breast** development | Isolated **sexual hair** (± body odour, mild acne) | Isolated cyclical **vaginal bleeding** |\n| Typical age | Infants and toddlers (often < 2 y) | Before 8 y in girls, 9 y in boys | Any, uncommon |\n| Growth & bone age | **Normal** | Normal or “slight increase of growth rate and bone age” | Normal |\n| Course | **Self-limiting**, often regresses | **Self-limited** | Self-limited |\n| Caveat | Reassess if it progresses or growth accelerates | Girls have “higher risk for polycystic ovarian syndrome later on” | Exclude local causes and abuse |\n\nThe adrenarche column is quoted from `14) Puberty.txt`, which defines premature adrenarche as “the appearance of sexual hair before the age of 8 yr in girls or 9 yr in boys without other evidence of maturation or growth acceleration”, notes it is “more frequent in girls than in boys”, and attributes it to “sensitivity to androgen production by adrenal gland (6-8 yrs)”.\n\n**A relevant neonatal aside from the same deck.** “High maternal level of prolactin can cause neoborn babies to be born with breast buds and even to lactate (self-limited).” Transplacental maternal hormones make breast tissue in a newborn an expected finding, and Sophia’s presentation at 18 months is the toddler version of the same benign phenomenon.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Brain tumor** — would drive the **whole** axis, giving a growth spurt, an advanced bone age and usually pubic hair, plus neurological or neuroendocrine features.\n- **Congenital adrenal hyperplasia** — an androgen excess: pubic hair, clitoromegaly, accelerated growth and a markedly advanced bone age. Sophia has none of these, and no breast development would be expected from adrenal androgen.\n- **Idiopathic precocious puberty** and **Premature puberty** — both name **true, complete** precocious puberty, which requires progression through the pubertal sequence with growth acceleration and bone-age advance. A normal bone age refutes both. (Note that the bank offers two labels for essentially the same wrong idea; neither is the answer, and recognising that they are the same answer twice is itself a clue.)\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Isolated breast tissue + normal growth + normal bone age = observe and re-measure in a few months. What would change the diagnosis is *progression*: a rising growth velocity, an advancing bone age, or pubic hair appearing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Isolated breast development with normal growth and a normal bone age is premature thelarche — a benign incomplete variant, not precocious puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.76'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-25', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Tom, a 7-year-old boy, is referred by his GP due to concerns about his growth. He is adopted, with no details available about his biological father; his biological mother was of average height. He is a happy, playful boy with no dysmorphic features. His height is 110 cm (just below the 0.4th centile), and his weight is also on the 0.4th centile. Physical examination is normal. What is the most likely cause of his short stature?',
  options:['Achondroplasia','Constitutional delay of growth and puberty','Familial short stature','Growth hormone deficiency','Vitamin D deficiency'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Proportionate short stature with normal development and growth tracking suggests a familial pattern, especially in the absence of concerning features. Adopted children without parental data make this harder to confirm but still likely.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-8`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**Expansion — this is a harder version of the familial short stature question, and the difficulty is deliberate.** The bank prints a near-identical stem elsewhere in this section (a 7-year-old boy, 110 cm, just below the 3rd centile, short father) with the same five options and the same key. **This vignette is not that one**: Tom is **adopted**, so the mid-parental height — normally the single most useful number in this consultation — is unavailable, and he sits at the **0.4th** centile rather than the 3rd, which is considerably shorter. The examiner is asking whether you can still reach the same conclusion when the easiest evidence has been removed.\n\n**What still points to a normal variant:**\n\n1. **Height and weight are on the same centile.** Proportionate smallness. `13) Short stature.txt`: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine.” Neither pattern is present.\n2. **He is well.** Happy, playful, normal examination — no chronic disease, no dysmorphism, no skeletal abnormality among the things the lecture lists for examination.\n3. **He is *tracking*, not falling.** The vignette gives no fall in centile. Growth failure is a change in slope; a low but parallel line is a variant.\n\n**What is genuinely missing, and should be said.** Without parental heights there is no mid-parental height, and `13) Short stature.txt` uses that value twice — in the history (“Mid-parental height calculation”) and in the definition of short stature (“Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”). A biological mother “of average height” is a weak surrogate. In practice this child warrants documented **serial** measurements to establish the height velocity, since velocity is the one thing that does not depend on knowing the parents.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Achondroplasia** — disproportionate, with short limbs, macrocephaly and frontal bossing. `13) Short stature.txt` gives it as the example of “Disproportionate short stature”. Tom is proportionate and has no dysmorphic features.\n- **Constitutional delay of growth and puberty** — needs a **delayed bone age** and **delayed puberty**, and characteristically presents in adolescence. At 7, with nothing pointing to delayed maturation, it is not the best answer — though it is the option that cannot be fully excluded without an X-ray, and a bone age is the reasonable next investigation.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — the lecture’s trigger list for suspecting it is “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year).” The 0.4th centile is roughly −2.6 SD, so he is short but not severely so by that threshold, and crucially his weight is equally affected and his velocity is not stated to be poor.\n- **Vitamin D deficiency** — rickets gives bone signs and a poor velocity, not a thriving child on a low tracking centile.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** When parental data are missing, growth **velocity** and a **bone age** replace them. `13) Short stature.txt` closes with “Most short children are normal variants” — but the way you prove it is a second measurement six months later, not a single reassuring visit.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Proportionate, well, tracking a low centile = familial short stature; where parental heights are unavailable, growth velocity and bone age carry the diagnosis.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.78'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-26', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'May, a 14-year-old girl, is referred by her school nurse because she is below the 0.4th centile for height. Her mother is 167 cm tall, and her father is 175 cm tall. She was born at term, birth weight 3.2 kg. She has always been the shortest in her class. On examination, she appears in early puberty. Her blood pressure is 135/65 mmHg. Which investigation is most likely to lead to a correct diagnosis?',
  options:['Coeliac screen','Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)','Full blood count','MRI scan of the brain','Karyotype'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Turner syndrome needs to be excluded as she has always been short. The elevated blood pressure may be because of an undetected coarctation of the aorta which is associated with the syndrome\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — three separate clues all point at one karyotype.**\n\n**1. Severe short stature against tall parents.** With a mother of 167 cm and a father of 175 cm, May’s mid-parental height is well above average, yet she is below the 0.4th centile. `13) Short stature.txt` defines short stature partly as “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”; a child this discordant with her family is not a familial variant.\n\n**2. The blood pressure.** 135/65 in a 14-year-old is hypertensive, and the wide pulse pressure with a high systolic is exactly what an unrepaired coarctation of the aorta produces in the arm. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists “Congenital heart defects (coarctation of the aorta)” among the clinical features of Turner syndrome. This is the clue that converts a plausible diagnosis into the likely one.\n\n**3. She has always been short, and puberty is only just beginning at 14.** Turner short stature dates from early childhood and does not fluctuate.\n\n**The lecture is categorical about the test.** `13) Short stature.txt` places **“Karyotyping (All girls)”** in its investigation ladder for short stature — not as an option to consider, but as a standing instruction. Turner syndrome is easy to miss because, as the same lecture’s Case 3 shows, **mosaic** Turner may have “No, or minimal manifestations”, since “Only a small percentage of cells have a chromosomal abnormality 45,XO” while the rest are normal 46,XX.\n\n**What Turner looks like when it does show itself.** `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt`: “a chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism and certain physical abnormalities”, incidence 1:2500 live-born girls (“More than 95% of 45X0 fetuses abort spontaneously”), with lymphoedema of the hands and feet in the neonate, spoon-shaped nails, short stature treated with growth hormone therapy, neck webbing, a wide carrying angle, widely spaced nipples, and coarctation. Cytogenetically about half are 45,X0, the remainder having a structural X abnormality or mosaicism such as 46XX/45X0 — and “The presence of Y chromosome sequence may increase the risk of gonadoblastoma, so gonadectomy is indicated.”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** The bank offers this same five-option menu three times in this section with three different keys, so the menu itself carries no information — the vignette must decide.\n\n- **Coeliac screen** — the answer when the **weight** line falls faster than the height line, or with gut symptoms, or in a high-risk group such as Down syndrome or type 1 diabetes. `13) Short stature.txt` puts the coeliac screen in Step Two beside TSH.\n- **TSH** — the answer when height falls while **weight is preserved or rising**, with lethargy, constipation and declining school performance (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`). May is not described that way, and hypothyroidism does not cause hypertension with a wide pulse pressure.\n- **Full blood count** — a Step One systemic screen, appropriate for suspected chronic disease or anaemia, but it cannot diagnose the condition the blood pressure is pointing at.\n- **MRI brain** — the answer when a pituitary or hypothalamic lesion is suspected: severe growth failure with a poor velocity, headache, visual field loss or papilloedema. Nothing here suggests it.\n\n**Filing note.** Kept in growth and puberty because the question is a short-stature work-up; the secondary chapter is **genetics**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Karyotype every short girl — Turner syndrome may show only short stature, and a raised blood pressure suggests the associated coarctation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.80'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-27', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Eleanor, a 7-year-old girl, is referred for short stature. She is on the 5th centile for height and 25th centile for weight. Nine months ago, her height and weight were on the 15th centile. Her mother notes that she has become lethargic, her school performance has worsened, and she refuses to participate in sports. Which investigation is most likely to lead to a correct diagnosis?',
  options:['Coeliac screen','Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)','Full blood count','MRI scan of the brain','Karyotype'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Falling height with preserved or increased weight, lethargy, and cognitive decline are classic for hypothyroidism. TSH testing will help confirm the diagnosis.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-26`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**Expansion — do the arithmetic on the two centile lines before anything else.** Nine months ago Eleanor was on the 15th centile for **both** height and weight. Now her height has fallen to the 5th while her weight has **risen** to the 25th. The two lines have moved in **opposite directions**, and `13) Short stature.txt` prints the interpretation as a rule:\n\n- “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease”\n- “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”\n\nThe same lecture makes the point again from the other direction under obesity: nutritional obesity gives a child who is “Obese + Tall”; endocrine causes give “Obese + Short/poor height velocity”. A child getting shorter relative to her peers while getting heavier has a hormone problem.\n\n**Then read the symptoms, which name the hormone.** Lethargy, declining school performance and refusal to exercise are the classic constitutional features of hypothyroidism. `52) hypothyrodism.txt` lists lethargy and poor feeding, constipation, cold extremities, dry thick skin, coarse facies and a hoarse cry among its features, describes the affected child as “Short with infantile proportions”, and opens by stating the lecture’s emphasis on “linear growth and brain” — the two things Eleanor has lost.\n\n**Why TSH is the right single test.** In acquired primary hypothyroidism the thyroid fails and the pituitary shouts: TSH rises before free T4 falls, so TSH is the most sensitive single screen. `13) Short stature.txt` places “TSH, fT4” together with the coeliac screen in **Step Two** of the investigation ladder — after the Step One systemic screen and, importantly, *before* any growth hormone testing, since “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”. The same deck lists hypothyroidism first among “Endocrinal causes of Short stature”, beside Cushing syndrome, GH deficiency and IGF-1 deficiency.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** This is the same five-option menu the bank uses twice more in this section with different keys, so the menu is uninformative and the vignette must decide.\n\n- **Coeliac screen** — the answer when the **weight** line leads the fall, or with diarrhoea, abdominal distension or anaemia. Eleanor’s weight is rising, which argues against malabsorption.\n- **Full blood count** — Step One screening for chronic disease and anaemia; it would not explain a rising weight and does not diagnose thyroid disease.\n- **MRI brain** — reserved for suspected hypothalamic–pituitary pathology: very severe growth failure, headache, visual field defects, papilloedema. `13) Short stature.txt` lists it under “Considering potential causes of GHD”, alongside fundoscopy and visual field assessment looking for bitemporal hemianopia from a craniopharyngioma. Nothing in Eleanor’s story requires it yet.\n- **Karyotype** — mandatory in short girls per the lecture (“Karyotyping (All girls)”), and it would eventually be done, but Turner syndrome does not produce lethargy, weight gain and cognitive decline over nine months. The acute change of pattern is what makes TSH the higher-yield first test.\n\n**Filing note.** Secondary chapter **endocrine**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Falling height with rising weight plus lethargy and poor school performance is acquired hypothyroidism — check TSH.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.82'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-28', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Jake, a 5-year-old boy with Down syndrome, presents for a routine check-up. He has dropped one centile in height and two centile lines in weight over the past 6 months. His mother reports increased irritability and poor appetite. Which investigation is most likely to lead to a correct diagnosis?',
  options:['Coeliac screen','Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)','Full blood count','MRI scan of the brain','Karyotype'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children with Down syndrome are at increased risk for coeliac disease, which can present with growth faltering, irritability, and poor appetite. A coeliac screen is warranted.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-26`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**Expansion — the direction of the weight line is the whole question.** This section contains **two** Down syndrome growth vignettes, they carry **different keys**, and the only thing that separates them is which centile falls faster:\n\n| | **This boy (Jake)** | **The other Down syndrome vignette** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Height | Down **one** centile | Down **two** centiles (75th → 25th) |\n| Weight | Down **two** centiles | **Up** one centile (50th → 75th) |\n| Pattern | **Weight affected > Height** | **Height affected, weight ↑** |\n| Rule (`13) Short stature.txt`) | → **Systemic disease** | → **Endocrine** |\n| Symptom | Irritability, poor appetite | Constipation |\n| Answer | **Coeliac screen** | **Thyroid function tests** |\n\nThe lecture states the rule in two lines — “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine” — and this pair of questions is the cleanest possible demonstration of it. If you answer both by reflex (“Down syndrome, therefore thyroid”), you get one of them wrong.\n\n**Why coeliac disease in particular.** Down syndrome carries a raised risk of several autoimmune conditions, coeliac disease among them, which is why coeliac serology sits in periodic health surveillance for these children *(the surveillance schedule itself is not stated in the cached decks — not taken from the course material)*. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` documents the syndrome’s broad medical burden — congenital heart defects in 40%, duodenal atresia, Hirschsprung disease, conductive hearing loss in 75%, visual impairment, hypothyroidism-prone endocrine risk, atlantoaxial instability, obstructive sleep apnoea in 50–75% and an increased risk of leukaemia — establishing the principle that a Down syndrome child with a new symptom needs an active search, not reassurance.\n\nClinically, coeliac disease in a young child presents as **weight faltering with irritability and anorexia**, often with abdominal distension, loose or foul stools and iron deficiency. That is Jake’s story almost word for word. The screen is IgA tissue transglutaminase antibodies with a total IgA (to detect IgA deficiency, which causes false negatives), and diagnosis is confirmed on biopsy — **while the child is still eating gluten** *(the serology and biopsy detail is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; `13) Short stature.txt` names the “Celiac screen” as a Step Two investigation without specifying the assay)*.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **TSH** — the right answer for the *other* Down syndrome vignette, where weight rises as height falls. Here weight is falling faster, which is the systemic pattern.\n- **Full blood count** — will very likely be abnormal (iron deficiency anaemia is common in coeliac disease) but it is non-specific and does not make the diagnosis.\n- **MRI brain** — no neurological or visual features, and no severe isolated height failure.\n- **Karyotype** — already known. He has a diagnosis of Down syndrome; repeating the karyotype adds nothing. This option is only correct in a short **girl** with no diagnosis.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Plot both lines. Weight falling faster than height sends you to the gut and to systemic disease; height falling while weight holds or rises sends you to the endocrine system.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Weight falling faster than height is the systemic pattern — in a Down syndrome child with irritability and anorexia, screen for coeliac disease.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.84'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-29', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 12-year-old girl is referred to the paediatric endocrinology clinic for evaluation of delayed puberty. She is diagnosed with constitutional delay of growth. What investigation is most likely to confirm this diagnosis?',
  options:['MRI brain','Thyroid function tests','Growth hormone level','Bone age','Karyotype'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Constitutional delay is confirmed by a bone age that is delayed relative to chronological age. It helps differentiate from pathological causes of delayed puberty.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — what a bone age actually is, and why it settles this.** `13) Short stature.txt` gives the method in three lines: “Most use: Greulich and Pyle”, “X-ray left wrist and hand”, and then the interpretation that answers this question:\n\n- “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine.”\n- “B. Normal → Familial.”\n\nThe radiograph compares the appearance and fusion of the ossification centres in the hand and wrist against a standard atlas. Skeletal maturation is driven by sex steroids and thyroid hormone, so it is a biological clock rather than a calendar one. In constitutional delay the whole clock is set back: the child is short **and immature**, and because the epiphyses are still open she has more growing time than her age implies — which is precisely why her final height will be normal.\n\n**Where bone age sits in the ladder.** Step Two of `13) Short stature.txt`, alongside TSH and fT4, the coeliac screen, IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, and karyotyping in all girls — after the Step One systemic bloods and **before** Step Three GH provocation testing.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **MRI brain** — reserved for suspected hypothalamic–pituitary disease. The lecture places it under “Considering potential causes of GHD” together with fundoscopy for papilloedema and visual field assessment for a bitemporal hemianopia, hunting a craniopharyngioma. A normal, well girl with delayed puberty does not need a scan first.\n- **Thyroid function tests** — genuinely part of the work-up, and `14) Puberty.txt` lists “Acquired hypothyroidism” among the causes of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, so they must be done. But a normal TSH **excludes** an alternative; it does not **confirm** constitutional delay. The stem asks which test confirms the diagnosis.\n- **Growth hormone level** — actively misleading, and the lecture says so twice: “GH testing: No use of basal GH level !” because “GH secreted in pulses”, and confirmation requires “2 GH provocation tests (sequential or on separate days)” with glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine. A single random GH tells you nothing whatever.\n- **Karyotype** — mandatory in short girls per the same lecture (“Karyotyping (All girls)”), and it is the test that would confirm **Turner syndrome**, the main pathological alternative here. But Turner is hypergonadotropic hypogonadism with a **normal or advanced** bone age relative to the degree of pubertal failure and with characteristic stigmata; it is excluded rather than confirmed by this stem, which has already given the diagnosis as constitutional delay.\n\n**A note on the definition, because it should be checked first.** `14) Puberty.txt`: delayed puberty is “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by 13 yrs of age in females or by 14 yrs of age in males.” At 12 this girl has not formally reached that threshold — so the referral is early, and a bone age plus reassurance is the entire appropriate response.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A bone age (left wrist and hand, Greulich and Pyle) delayed relative to chronological age is what confirms constitutional delay.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.86'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-30', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 15-year-old girl is referred to the paediatric endocrinology clinic for delayed puberty. Her height is on the 0.4th centile. She has an ejection systolic murmur and widely spaced nipples. Gonadotrophin levels are raised. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Turner syndrome','Klinefelter syndrome','McCune-Albright syndrome','Hypothyroidism','Silver-Russell syndrome'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Short stature, delayed puberty, heart murmur, and raised gonadotropins point to Turner syndrome. It is caused by gonadal dysgenesis and confirmed with karyotyping.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — “raised gonadotrophins” localises the lesion before you name the syndrome.** Delayed puberty splits into two biochemical halves, and `14) Puberty.txt` gives both:\n\n| | **Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism** | **Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism** |\n|---|---|---|\n| LH / FSH | **Low or normal** | **Raised** |\n| Lesion | Hypothalamus or pituitary — the drive is missing | **The gonad itself** has failed; no feedback, so the pituitary shouts |\n| Causes (per the deck) | Systemic disorders / any chronic illness; acquired hypothyroidism; hypothalamic-pituitary disorder, isolated or part of panhypopituitarism from tumour or radiation; **Kallmann syndrome, associated with absent sense of smell** | Gonadal dysgenesis — **Turner syndrome** in girls, Klinefelter in boys; chemotherapy or radiotherapy; surgical or torsion loss |\n\nRaised gonadotrophins therefore place this girl in the right-hand column before any physical sign is considered. Add **severe short stature** and **widely spaced nipples**, and the diagnosis is made.\n\n**The murmur is not incidental.** `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists “Congenital heart defects (coarctation of the aorta)” as a feature of Turner syndrome; a bicuspid aortic valve is the other common lesion and is a classic source of an ejection systolic murmur *(the bicuspid valve is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. Either finding mandates cardiac assessment, because aortic complications are the main threat to life in Turner syndrome.\n\n**The full Turner picture from the genetics deck.** “A chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism and certain physical abnormalities”; incidence 1:2500 live-born girls, with more than 95% of 45,X0 fetuses aborting spontaneously; increasingly detected prenatally when “fetal edema of the neck, hands or feet or a cystic hygroma may be identified”. Clinical features: lymphoedema of the hands and feet in the neonate, spoon-shaped nails, **short stature (growth hormone therapy)**, neck webbing, a wide carrying angle, widely spaced nipples, and coarctation. Cytogenetics: about 50% are 45,X0, the rest having a short-arm deletion, an isochromosome, another structural abnormality, or mosaicism such as 46XX/45X0 — and where Y-chromosome sequence is present, gonadectomy is indicated because of gonadoblastoma risk.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Klinefelter syndrome** — 47,XXY, a **male** condition. It is also hypergonadotropic, and boys are typically **tall** with small firm testes and gynaecomastia. It cannot apply to a girl.\n- **McCune-Albright syndrome** — causes **peripheral precocious puberty**, not delayed puberty, with café-au-lait macules with irregular “coast of Maine” borders and polyostotic fibrous dysplasia *(the triad detail is not taken from the course material)*. Gonadotrophins would be **suppressed**.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — a cause of short stature and delayed puberty, but it is **hypogonadotropic** (`14) Puberty.txt` lists “Acquired hypothyroidism” under that heading), and it produces no murmur or widely spaced nipples.\n- **Silver-Russell syndrome** — severe intrauterine growth restriction with postnatal short stature, a relatively large head, a triangular face and body asymmetry; `13) Short stature.txt` names “Russel-Silver syndrome” among the syndromic causes of short stature. It does not cause gonadal failure with raised gonadotrophins.\n\n**Filing note.** Secondary chapter **genetics**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Raised gonadotrophins mean primary gonadal failure — with short stature, widely spaced nipples and a murmur, that is Turner syndrome.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.88'
},
{
  id:'pedep-gp-31', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 5-year-old boy is referred to a paediatric specialist for tall stature and headaches. On examination, he has testicular enlargement and bilateral papilloedema. What investigation is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?',
  options:['CT or MRI brain scan','Karyotype','ACTH level','17-Hydroxyprogesterone','Bone age'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Precocious puberty and neurological symptoms like headache and papilledema suggest a central brain lesion (e.g., hypothalamic hamartoma). Brain imaging is the key investigation.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — two findings make this an emergency, and both point the same way.**\n\n**1. Testicular enlargement means the puberty is CENTRAL.** In a boy, the testes are the switch that separates the two kinds of precocity. Enlarged testes mean the gonad is being **driven** by pituitary gonadotrophins, so the lesion lies at or above the pituitary. `14) Puberty.txt` defines central precocious puberty as “a result of the early activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis”, and includes a slide on “Testicular examination in precocious puberty” precisely because this single sign redirects the whole work-up. Had the testes been **small** with virilisation, the androgen would be adrenal or exogenous and the answer would be 17-hydroxyprogesterone or an adrenal study.\n\n**2. Papilloedema and headache mean raised intracranial pressure.** Together with tall stature for age — the growth acceleration of early sex-steroid exposure — this is a structural intracranial lesion until proved otherwise.\n\n**The epidemiology makes the scan compulsory in a boy.** `14) Puberty.txt` is explicit: central precocious puberty “occurs 5-10 folds more frequently in girls than in boys”; “A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty has been reported in girls (90%)”, whereas “structural central nervous system (CNS) abnormality can be demonstrated in up to **75% of boys** with central precocious puberty.” The same deck warns about presentation: with brain tumours “The only sign is precocious puberty”, and lists the accompanying features to hunt for — neurological signs; neuroendocrine features such as diabetes insipidus, hyperthermia, “unnatural crying or laughing (gelastic seizures)”, obesity and cachexia; and visual manifestations such as visual field defect, proptosis and decreased visual acuity. Under investigations it names “MRI brain with contrast for pituitary gland in gonadotropin-dependent PP.”\n\nThe gelastic-seizure line is worth remembering here, because **hypothalamic hamartoma** — the lesion named in the bank’s own box — is the classic cause of central precocity with gelastic seizures.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Karyotype** — the test for Turner syndrome in a short girl, or Klinefelter in a tall boy with small firm testes and delayed puberty. It has no role in a boy with an obviously acquired, progressive process and raised intracranial pressure.\n- **ACTH level** — points at adrenal disease, which causes **peripheral** precocity with **small** testes. Testicular enlargement rules that pathway out.\n- **17-Hydroxyprogesterone** — the screening test for congenital adrenal hyperplasia, again peripheral virilisation with prepubertal testes. `14) Puberty.txt` reserves adrenal testing (“ACTH STIMULATION TEST to exclude CAH”) for **atypical premature adrenarche** — marked growth acceleration, clitoral or phallic enlargement, cystic acne, or a bone age more than 2 SD advanced.\n- **Bone age** — will certainly be advanced and is part of the assessment, but it only quantifies how much sex-steroid exposure has occurred. It cannot find a tumour, and it would delay the scan in a child with papilloedema.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Central precocious puberty in a **boy** = image the brain. Add headache and papilloedema and the imaging is urgent.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Testicular enlargement means central precocity; in a boy — and especially with headache and papilloedema — that demands brain imaging.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.90'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-32', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'There are many causes of short stature in children. Which of the following does not cause short stature?',
  options:['Turner syndrome','Constitutional delay of growth','Rickets','Thyrotoxicosis','Cystic fibrosis'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Thyrotoxicosis typically causes growth acceleration, not short stature. The others are recognized causes of growth failure.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — thyroid hormone works in both directions, and that is the trap.** Too little thyroid hormone slows linear growth and skeletal maturation; too much **accelerates** both. `52) hypothyrodism.txt` sets hypothyroidism against thyrotoxicosis and lists “Rapid growth in height” among the features of the hyperthyroid child, alongside lid retraction. A thyrotoxic child is therefore typically **tall for age with an advanced bone age** — and the sting in the tail is that, because sex steroids and thyroid hormone drive epiphyseal fusion, prolonged untreated thyrotoxicosis can still shorten *adult* height even while the child looks tall *(the final-height consequence is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. The question asks about short stature in childhood, and there thyrotoxicosis is the odd one out.\n\n**Why each of the other four does cause short stature.**\n\n- **Turner syndrome** — one of the two commonest genetic causes of short stature in girls. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists “Short stature. (Growth hormone therapy)” among its clinical features, and `13) Short stature.txt` names Turner first among “Syndromes causing Short stature”, alongside Russell–Silver, Down and Noonan. It is also the reason that lecture instructs “Karyotyping (All girls)”.\n- **Constitutional delay of growth** — causes short stature **during childhood and adolescence**, with a delayed bone age and delayed puberty, even though final adult height is normal. Note the distinction the examiner may exploit: it causes short stature *now*, not permanently.\n- **Rickets** — vitamin D deficiency impairs mineralisation of the growth plate, producing bowing, widened wrists, a rachitic rosary and stunted linear growth. The syllabus devotes two decks to it (`17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` and `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt`).\n- **Cystic fibrosis** — the model of growth failure from chronic disease: fat malabsorption from pancreatic insufficiency, a high energy cost from chronic suppurative lung disease, and recurrent infection. It produces the systemic pattern of `13) Short stature.txt` — “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease.”\n\n**The framework the lecture wants you to carry into any short-stature question.**\n\n| Category | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Normal variants** — commonest | Familial short stature; constitutional delay of growth and puberty |\n| **Systemic / chronic disease** | Coeliac disease, cystic fibrosis, chronic renal failure, chronic anaemia, inflammatory bowel disease, PEM |\n| **Endocrine** | GH deficiency, IGF-1 deficiency, **hypo**thyroidism, Cushing syndrome |\n| **Syndromic / genetic** | Turner, Down, Noonan, Russell–Silver |\n| **Skeletal (disproportionate)** | Achondroplasia and other dysplasias, rickets |\n| **Prenatal** | SGA without catch-up — “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth” |\n\n(Categories and named entities from `13) Short stature.txt`.)\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Any question containing a thyroid word should be answered by asking *which direction*: hypothyroidism shortens and delays, thyrotoxicosis lengthens and advances.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Hypothyroidism stunts growth and delays bone age; thyrotoxicosis accelerates both, so it is not a cause of childhood short stature.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.92'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-33', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 5-year-old boy is referred by the school nurse because his height and weight are both on the 99th centile at school entry. He appears healthy, with tall parents, no birthmarks, and normal blood pressure. What is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Thyroid function tests','9 a.m. cortisol level','Calculate mid-parental height','Dietary assessment','Genetic studies for Prader-Willi syndrome'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A child growing along the 99th centile with tall parents and no red flags likely has familial tall stature. Calculating mid-parental height is appropriate to assess genetic potential.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — tall stature is assessed exactly like short stature, in reverse.** The same instrument answers both: plot the child, plot the parents, and see whether the two agree. `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Mid-parental height calculation” as a routine part of the growth history and defines abnormality partly by discordance from it (“Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”). A tall child of tall parents is **concordant** and needs no investigation; a tall child of average parents is **discordant** and does.\n\n**The three details in this stem that are all negatives, and each excludes something.**\n\n| Detail given | What it excludes |\n|---|---|\n| **Tall parents** | Pathological tall stature — the child is growing to plan |\n| **“No birthmarks”** | **McCune–Albright syndrome** (café-au-lait macules) and **neurofibromatosis type 1** — both associated with endocrine and growth abnormality *(not taken from the course material)* |\n| **Normal blood pressure** | Glucocorticoid or catecholamine excess |\n| **Height AND weight both on the 99th** | Proportionate — no obesity-with-short-stature pattern, no gigantism-with-normal-weight pattern |\n\nThe “no birthmarks” clause is quietly load-bearing and is easy to read past; the examiner put it there.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Thyroid function tests** — appropriate for suspected **thyrotoxicosis**, which `52) hypothyrodism.txt` associates with “Rapid growth in height” and lid retraction. This boy has none of the accompanying features (tachycardia, weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance, goitre), and he is heavy as well as tall, which is against it.\n- **9 a.m. cortisol level** — measures for Cushing syndrome, which `13) Short stature.txt` lists among the **endocrinal causes of short stature**. Cushing makes children **fat and short**, not tall and proportionate, and would be accompanied by hypertension, striae and a moon face. The stem’s normal blood pressure argues against it.\n- **Dietary assessment** — the move for **nutritional** obesity. But `13) Short stature.txt` gives the discriminator: “A. Nutritional Obesity: Obese + Tall. B. Endocrinal causes: Obese + Short/poor height velocity.” This boy is tall *and* heavy in proportion, i.e. large rather than obese; without a BMI above the 95th centile — `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` gives obese >95th, overweight 85th–95th, healthy 5th–85th — there is nothing to assess.\n- **Genetic studies for Prader-Willi syndrome** — Prader–Willi causes **obesity with short stature**, hypotonia and poor feeding in infancy followed by hyperphagia, hypogonadism and learning difficulty. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` lists it under syndromic obesity. It is the opposite phenotype to this child.\n\n**When tall stature *would* need investigating.** A tall child **discordant** with the parents, with a growth velocity that is accelerating, or with any of: disproportionate long limbs and an arm span exceeding height (Marfan syndrome), a tall boy with small firm testes and learning difficulties (Klinefelter, 47,XXY), café-au-lait macules with precocious puberty (McCune–Albright), or coarse features with acral enlargement (a GH-secreting pituitary lesion — gigantism before epiphyseal fusion) *(this list is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the cached decks address short stature and precocious puberty rather than tall stature as a topic)*.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Do the arithmetic before ordering any test. Most tall children are tall for the same reason most short children are short — their parents.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Assess tall stature by comparing the child with the mid-parental height; concordance with tall parents in a well child needs no investigation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.94'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-34', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 14-month-old baby girl is brought to the clinic due to concerns about her size. She is on the 2nd centile for weight, height, and head circumference. She was born on the 2nd centile. She is taking cow’s milk, fully weaned, with normal stools and unremarkable examination. Her parents are short and are concerned she may also be small as she grows. What is the most likely cause of her size?',
  options:['Constitutionally small','Cow’s milk protein enteropathy','Hypothyroidism','Growth hormone deficiency','Skeletal dysplasia'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Consistent growth along low centiles since birth in a healthy child with short parents suggests constitutional smallness rather than pathological causes.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — four features, and every one of them says “normal small child”.**\n\n1. **She was born on the 2nd centile and is still on the 2nd centile.** No centile crossing. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` sets the threshold for concern at a weight falling “across two centile spaces”; nothing has fallen at all. `13) Short stature.txt` likewise defines abnormality by “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines”, not by a low position.\n2. **All three parameters are on the same centile** — weight, height **and head circumference**. She is a small child, uniformly scaled. Pathology almost always disturbs the proportions: malnutrition takes weight first, endocrine disease takes height while sparing weight, and head circumference is preserved longest of all.\n3. **Her parents are short.** Her size is concordant with her genetic potential, which is the definition of constitutional (familial) smallness.\n4. **She is well.** Normal stools, unremarkable examination, fully weaned and feeding. None of the red flags in `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — is present.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Cow’s milk protein enteropathy** — would give gastrointestinal symptoms: loose, mucoid or blood-streaked stools, vomiting, abdominal distension, often eczema or a family history of atopy, and characteristically **weight falling faster than height**. Her stools are normal and she has not crossed centiles.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — produces the endocrine pattern, with **height falling while weight is preserved or rises**, plus constipation, lethargy, coarse facies, a large tongue, dry skin, cold extremities and an umbilical hernia (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`, which also describes the affected child as “Short with infantile proportions”). Congenital hypothyroidism is detected on newborn screening; the same deck notes that “Before neonatal screening, less than 5 %” were picked up early. Nothing here fits.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — needs falling centiles and a poor velocity. `13) Short stature.txt` sets the bar for even suspecting it: “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year).” None is met, and GH-dependent growth is barely established at 14 months in any case.\n- **Skeletal dysplasia** — would give **disproportionate** short stature. `13) Short stature.txt` gives achondroplasia its own slide under “Disproportionate short stature”, and requires that “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)” be measured in every short child. This girl is proportionate on all three parameters and her examination is unremarkable.\n\n**What to do, since the parents are asking.** Plot her again in three to six months on the WHO chart (`13) Short stature.txt`: “Recommended for 0-24 months”). If she stays on the 2nd centile she is a small, healthy child and needs nothing. If she falls off it, the work-up begins — and the lecture’s own summary is the message for the parents: “Most short children are normal variants.”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A child born on a low centile who is still on it, proportionate, well, and with short parents is constitutionally small — reassure and re-plot.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.96'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-35', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal',
  stem:'Which of the following is the most important cause of failure to establish breathing and the requirement of resuscitation at birth?',
  options:['Congenital lung anomalies','Birth trauma','Birth asphyxia','Retained lung fluid'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Birth asphyxia is the leading cause of neonatal respiratory failure and need for resuscitation. Congenital anomalies or retained fluid are less common causes.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Filing note.** This is a **perinatal** question printed inside the Growth and Puberty section of the endpoint file. It is filed under `perinatal` because it concerns the transition at delivery and resuscitation in the delivery room; the secondary chapter is **neonatal**.\n\n**Expansion — why asphyxia dominates the list.** The first breath has to do three things at once: clear lung liquid, establish a functional residual capacity, and drop pulmonary vascular resistance so that blood diverts from the ductus into the lungs. Anything that impairs the drive to breathe interrupts all three, and the commonest such insult is **intrapartum hypoxia–ischaemia**. Hypoxia first causes rapid breathing, then primary apnoea (which responds to stimulation), then gasping, then **terminal apnoea**, which responds only to positive-pressure ventilation *(the primary/terminal apnoea sequence is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. That is why the single most effective manoeuvre in newborn resuscitation is inflation of the lungs, not chest compressions or drugs.\n\n**The syllabus treats asphyxia as a major topic in its own right.** `12) HIE.txt` is devoted to hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy, and `58) cerebral palsy.txt` and `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` both list birth asphyxia among their causes — the recurrence across three decks is itself a statement of weight.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Congenital lung anomalies** — congenital diaphragmatic hernia, pulmonary hypoplasia, congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation. Each is a genuine cause of failure to establish breathing, and each is **rare**. The question asks for the most important cause, meaning the commonest and most consequential.\n- **Birth trauma** — a real contributor, and the material covers it (`10) new Seizures . Birth injuries , Hpoglycemia 2.txt`). Severe trauma such as cervical spinal injury or a subgaleal haemorrhage can compromise a newborn, but it accounts for a small fraction of resuscitations compared with asphyxia.\n- **Retained lung fluid** — transient tachypnoea of the newborn, commonest after elective caesarean section. It is very common but usually **mild and self-limiting**: these babies are tachypnoeic and may need oxygen, but they breathe. It is a cause of respiratory distress, not of failure to breathe at all.\n\n**The distinction the examiner is drawing.** *Respiratory distress* (the baby is breathing, badly) and *failure to establish respiration* (the baby is not breathing) are different problems with different differentials. `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` handles the first; this question is about the second, and there the answer is asphyxia.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Anticipate. Risk factors for intrapartum asphyxia — fetal distress, meconium-stained liquor, abruption, cord prolapse, prolonged or obstructed labour — should bring a trained resuscitator to the delivery before the baby is born, because the intervention that works is effective lung inflation in the first minute.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Birth asphyxia is the leading reason a newborn fails to establish breathing and needs resuscitation; effective lung inflation is the key intervention.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.98'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-36', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the most important determinant of growth in the infant phase?',
  options:['Nutrition','Genetic factors','Hormonal influence','Physical activity'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Infant growth relies primarily on adequate nutrient intake. Even with normal genes and hormones, poor nutrition can slow growth significantly.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ A near-twin, held separately.** The endpoint file asks this same fact elsewhere in the section over a **different, five-option list** (Maternal nutrition / Infant nutrition / Size at birth / Vitamins / Parental size), and again over a third list. The option sets are **replaced**, not merely trimmed, so by the standard folding test these are separate questions rather than reprints, and each is held. The underlying fact is one fact — but the discriminations being tested differ: the five-option version tests *infant* nutrition against *maternal* nutrition, while this four-option version tests nutrition against the other three broad determinants.\n\n**Expansion — the four determinants, ranked by phase.**\n\n| Determinant | When it dominates | Why not in infancy |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nutrition** | **Birth to ~2 years** | — it *is* the answer |\n| Genetic factors | Childhood onwards | Sets the destination centile, not the early rate; this is why infants normally cross centiles as they migrate from their birth size toward their genetic size |\n| Hormonal influence | Childhood (GH/IGF-1) and puberty (sex steroids) | GH-dependent growth is barely established in the first months; a GH-deficient newborn is of normal length |\n| Physical activity | Never a primary determinant of linear growth | Affects muscle and bone density, not height |\n\nThe endpoint file’s own summary slide assigns the infantile phase **15% of final height** (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7), and `13) Short stature.txt` lists the influences on growth as genetics, systemic health, hormones (GH/IGF-1, thyroid hormone, insulin, sex steroids, glucocorticoids) and “Nutrition, Sleep, Exercise” — a set that must then be sequenced by age, which is what this question tests.\n\n**Why this matters clinically rather than as trivia.** Because nutrition is the infant driver, **growth faltering clusters in infancy**, and the first response is a feeding history rather than a hormone assay. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` calls faltering growth a “Description, not a diagnosis”, present when weight falls “across two centile spaces”, and gives management as “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with “Investigations should be guided by history and examination” — not a reflex panel. It also lists the red flags that shift the suspicion off pure intake: dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight **despite adequate caloric intake**, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, and developmental delay.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Genetic factors* set the target, not the trajectory, in the first two years. *Hormonal influence* is the childhood and pubertal answer — offering it for infancy is a phase error, and the same bank tests that error directly with a question keying growth hormone for the childhood phase. *Physical activity* has no meaningful role in infant linear growth and is a filler option.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Infant not growing → look at what is going into the baby, and how much of it stays in.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Nutrition is the dominant determinant of growth in infancy; genes and hormones take over from the childhood phase onward.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.100'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-37', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Laila, an 11-month-old girl, is being monitored by her health provider. Her birthweight was 2.4 kg (2nd centile) and she has remained on the 2nd centile, now weighing 7.0 kg. Her mother is on the 5th centile and her father is on the 40th centile for height. She is well, has a good appetite and has never needed to visit her doctor. She has no abnormal signs on examination and her development is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Cow milk protein allergy','Stunting','Marasmus','Normal','Vitamin D deficiency'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Laila’s growth follows her familial pattern (parents’ heights), and she is healthy and developing normally, indicating constitutional smallness rather than pathology.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note.** The stem is printed with a double space and a space before the question mark (“What is  the most likely diagnosis ?”); the spacing has been tidied and nothing else altered.\n\n**Expansion — “Normal” is the hardest answer to choose, so justify it explicitly.** Five things have to be true, and all five are given:\n\n1. **She has never crossed a centile.** Born on the 2nd, still on the 2nd. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines faltering growth as present when weight falls “across two centile spaces” — Laila has fallen across none. Position on a chart is not a diagnosis; **movement** is.\n2. **Her size is concordant with her family.** Her mother is on the 5th centile. A small child of a small mother is growing to plan; `13) Short stature.txt` requires the mid-parental height precisely so this judgement can be made.\n3. **She is well and has a good appetite.** No feeding difficulty, no illness, never needed a doctor.\n4. **Examination is normal.** None of the red flags in `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, chronic respiratory signs, developmental delay.\n5. **Development is normal.** A malnourished infant loses developmental ground; `16) P.E.M.txt` lists “Retarded development” in the underweight child and “Permanent delay in intellectual developement” among the consequences of PEM.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Cow milk protein allergy** — needs symptoms: loose, mucoid or blood-streaked stools, vomiting, poor feeding, eczema, or weight falling faster than height. Laila has a good appetite, no signs, and a stable centile.\n- **Stunting** — low **height-for-age** from chronic undernutrition. Laila is small, but she is small **as expected for her family** and has not decelerated; stunting describes a child whose linear growth has been suppressed below her potential, which is a different claim.\n- **Marasmus** — a severe form of PEM. `16) P.E.M.txt` defines it by the Wellcome classification as weight **below 60%** of the 50th centile without oedema, occurring “during first 3 years of life (Usually 1st year)”, with loss of subcutaneous fat, muscle wasting of the limbs and abdomen, and psychic changes — anxious, irritable, excessively crying, little sleeping. At 7.0 kg on the 2nd centile Laila is nowhere near that, and she is thriving in every other respect.\n- **Vitamin D deficiency** — rickets gives bone signs (widened wrists, rachitic rosary, delayed fontanelle closure, bowing once weight-bearing), often with irritability, and a decelerating growth line. Her examination is normal.\n\n**The teaching point.** A low centile is a **position**; growth faltering is a **direction**. Two measurements six months apart carry more information than any single number — which is exactly what `13) Short stature.txt` means by “A single measurement is less important than growth trend.” The correct management of Laila is to keep plotting her, and to say clearly to her parents that she is normal.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A well infant tracking her own low centile, concordant with a small parent, is normal — faltering growth is a fall, not a low position.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.102'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-38', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'You are examining the growth chart of an individual who presented as a teenager complaining of short stature and delayed puberty. He has had X-rays for bone age assessment done twice at the age of 5 and at the age of 11 years and both were somewhat delayed. Finally, he ended with a height that is compatible with his mid-parental height. Which type of short stature did he suffer from?',
  options:['Genetic short stature','Growth hormone deficiency','Chromosomal disorder','Constitutional short stature','Nutritional short stature'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Delayed bone age and puberty with eventual normal adult height is characteristic of constitutional growth delay.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this question is answered by the *ending*, not the beginning.** The stem is written retrospectively on purpose: it gives you the whole natural history, and the diagnostic weight sits in the last sentence. **He reached a final height compatible with his mid-parental height.** That single outcome excludes every pathological option, because pathology costs adult height. Combined with a **delayed bone age on two separate occasions** and **delayed puberty**, only constitutional delay of growth and puberty fits.\n\n| Feature in the stem | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| Presented **as a teenager** | CDGP declares itself when peers begin their pubertal spurt — not in infancy |\n| **Delayed puberty** | Sets the diagnosis in the delayed-maturation family, not the familial one |\n| Bone age delayed at **5 and again at 11** | Consistently immature skeleton — `13) Short stature.txt`: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine” |\n| **Final height = mid-parental height** | Nothing was lost. Growth was postponed, not destroyed |\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Genetic short stature** (familial short stature) — would show a **normal** bone age (“B. Normal → Familial”) and **normally timed** puberty, and the final height, while normal *for the family*, would be reached on schedule rather than late. The two bone ages exclude it.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — the “Endocrine” half of the delayed-bone-age rule, and the reason a delayed bone age alone is not diagnostic. But GH deficiency untreated does **not** end at mid-parental height: `13) Short stature.txt` describes low IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, a growth velocity under 4 cm a year, severe short stature more than 3 SD below the mean, and treatment with daily subcutaneous recombinant human GH — the whole point of which is that final height is otherwise lost.\n- **Chromosomal disorder** — Turner syndrome in a girl or Down syndrome in either sex both produce **permanently** reduced adult height (`6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists short stature under both), and Turner needs growth hormone therapy for that reason. Reaching mid-parental height excludes them.\n- **Nutritional short stature** — PEM causes growth failure with weight affected more than height (`16) P.E.M.txt`), and severe or prolonged malnutrition leaves permanent deficits, including “Permanent delay in intellectual developement”. Catch-up after treatment can be substantial, but the stem gives no nutritional history and gives delayed puberty and a delayed bone age instead.\n\n**Management, which the examiner often asks next.** `13) Short stature.txt` answers it in its Case 2: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” The treatment is offered for the psychological burden of being the smallest boy in the year, not to increase final height — which is normal either way.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Delayed bone age + delayed puberty + normal final height = constitutional delay. Delayed bone age + a final height *below* the mid-parental range = something pathological was missed.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay is recognised retrospectively by a persistently delayed bone age, late puberty, and a final height matching mid-parental height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.104'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-39', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 2-year-old boy presents to the office with his father due to concerns over the appearance of his son’s genitals. He reports that over the last 2 months he has noticed his penis has gotten longer, frequent erections, and what appears to be a few dark strands of hair. On examination, you confirm that the phallus is long for stated age, pubic hair that is curly, and testicles that are less than 2.5 cm. His father denies any past medical history for his son and even states that he had imaging of his head during an ER visit for a fall 2 weeks prior which was normal. When you question the father about his onset of puberty, he claims to have noticed changes at 14 years old. He denies any other family history of illness. The child lives with his mother and father. Medications in the home include maternal intake of daily multivitamins and paternal gel for decreased libido. Which of the following statements is likely an explanation for this child’s presentation?',
  options:['Noncentral precocious puberty hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis early activation','Noncentral precocious puberty from exogenous source of sex hormone','Central precocious puberty hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis early activation','Central precocious puberty from exogenous source of sex hormone','Incomplete precocious puberty based on history and early thelarche'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This 2-year-old boy shows signs of virilization (penile growth, pubic hair) but prepubertal testicular size (<2.5 cm), which suggests peripheral (gonadotropin-independent) precocious puberty, rather than central (which would have testicular enlargement). The history of paternal testosterone gel at home raises concern for exogenous androgen exposure, which is a common cause of noncentral precocious puberty in young children. Central activation of the HPG axis is unlikely given the very young age and small testes.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Note on the printed options.** Options A and C are internally contradictory as written: “noncentral precocious puberty **hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis early activation**” describes two mutually exclusive things, since noncentral (peripheral) disease is by definition **not** driven by the HPG axis. They are deliberate nonsense pairings, and recognising that A cannot exist is itself a route to the answer. The options are reproduced exactly as printed.\n\n**Expansion — the answer is a 2×2 grid, and only one cell is possible.**\n\n| | **HPG axis activation** | **Exogenous / peripheral source** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Central** | The real entity — enlarged testes, detectable LH/FSH | Impossible by definition |\n| **Noncentral (peripheral)** | Impossible by definition | **The real entity — small testes, suppressed LH/FSH** ✔ |\n\n**The clinical evidence.** *Testicles less than 2.5 cm* is prepubertal. In a boy, true central puberty **begins** with testicular enlargement — `14) Puberty.txt` describes genital stage II as “enlargement of the scrotum and testes and thinning and redness of the scrotal skin”, and includes a slide on testicular examination in precocious puberty for exactly this reason. Virilisation (penile growth, erections, curly pubic hair) **without** testicular growth therefore means the androgen is arriving from somewhere other than the testes.\n\n**The source is named in the last line of the stem.** “Paternal gel for decreased libido” is a transdermal testosterone preparation. Skin-to-skin contact transfers it, and a toddler carried against a treated shoulder or chest receives a proportionally enormous dose. This is a **reversible** cause: the virilisation stops and partially regresses once the exposure ends *(the reversibility and the transfer mechanism are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; `14) Puberty.txt` classifies precocity into central and peripheral without discussing transdermal androgen transfer)*.\n\n**The distractor details the examiner planted.** *“Imaging of his head... was normal”* pre-empts the central answer. *“He had changes at 14 years old”* gives a father whose own puberty was, if anything, late, weakening any familial argument. Both are there to close off the alternatives before you reach the medication list.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *A* — impossible construction (see above); noncentral disease is gonadotrophin-independent. *C* — central activation would give **enlarged testes**, and the head imaging was normal; the deck notes that up to 75% of boys with central precocity have a demonstrable structural CNS abnormality, making a normal scan additional evidence against it. *D* — impossible construction; an exogenous hormone suppresses the axis rather than activating it. *E* — “thelarche” is **breast** development, which this boy does not have; the option is describing a girl’s benign incomplete variant and does not apply.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In any virilised toddler, examine the testes and then take a **household medication history**. Small testes plus an androgen in the home is the diagnosis, and the treatment is removing the exposure.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Virilisation with prepubertal testes is peripheral precocity — in a toddler, look for exogenous androgen such as a parent’s testosterone gel.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.106, box on p.107'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-40', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the most likely cause of faltering growth in a 9-month-old infant?',
  options:['Small atrial septal defect (ASD)','Persistent diarrhea','Physiological gastroesophageal reflux','Frequent minor upper respiratory infections'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Chronic or frequent diarrhea leads to poor nutrient absorption and weight/height faltering; minor infections or reflux are less impactful.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — faltering growth is an energy-balance problem, and there are only four ways to fail.** Every cause fits into one of these boxes, and the strategy for answering any faltering-growth question is to ask which box the option belongs in:\n\n| Mechanism | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Not enough in** | Inadequate intake, poor feeding technique, insufficient breast milk, neglect, poverty, an aversive feeding relationship |\n| **Not enough absorbed** | **Persistent diarrhoea**, coeliac disease, cow’s milk protein enteropathy, cystic fibrosis, chronic infection or parasitosis |\n| **Too much lost** | Recurrent vomiting, severe reflux, protein-losing states |\n| **Too much used** | Congenital heart disease with a significant shunt, chronic lung disease, chronic infection, hyperthyroidism |\n\n**Persistent diarrhoea sits in the second box and is by far the highest-yield answer in this population**, particularly in a setting where infectious diarrhoea is common; the syllabus gives it a full deck (`23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt`). It causes malabsorption of the nutrients that *are* taken in, is often accompanied by anorexia during illness, and recurs, so the child never gets a period of catch-up. The endpoint file’s own summary slide states the single commonest cause overall as “Most common cause of faltering growth: Inadequate calorie intake” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.27) — but of the four options offered here, only persistent diarrhoea produces a comparable sustained negative energy balance.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong — note that each is qualified to make it trivial.**\n\n- **Small atrial septal defect** — the word “small” is doing the work. A **large** left-to-right shunt causes faltering growth by raising energy expenditure and causing breathlessness on feeding, and the syllabus covers this in `33)Congenital acyanotic heart disease_.txt`. A **small** ASD is haemodynamically insignificant, often asymptomatic, and frequently closes spontaneously.\n- **Physiological gastroesophageal reflux** — again the qualifier decides it. Physiological reflux (“happy posseting”) occurs in a large proportion of infants and by definition does **not** affect growth. Pathological reflux with volume loss, feed refusal or aspiration does.\n- **Frequent minor upper respiratory infections** — common in infancy, especially once a child enters day care, and they cause brief dips in intake with prompt recovery. They are **minor** and self-limiting, so they do not produce a sustained deficit.\n\n**How this is assessed in practice.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` reminds you that faltering growth is a “Description, not a diagnosis”, present when weight falls “across two centile spaces”; that you must “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart”; that “Investigations should be guided by history and examination”; and that “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake”. Its red flags — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — are what separate a behavioural feeding problem from organic disease.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Read the adjectives. “Small”, “physiological” and “minor” are all instructions to discount the option.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Faltering growth is an energy-balance failure; persistent diarrhoea causes sustained malabsorption, whereas minor or physiological conditions do not.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.109'
},
{
  id:'pedep-gp-41', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'During which phase of growth is faltering most commonly seen?',
  options:['Infantile','Childhood','Pubertal','Fetal'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Infancy is a high-risk period for growth faltering due to dependence on nutrition and vulnerability to illness.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — three properties of infancy converge to make it the vulnerable phase.**\n\n1. **Growth is fastest postnatally, so the nutritional demand is highest.** A baby roughly triples birth weight and grows about 25 cm in the first year; the endpoint file’s own summary slide assigns the infantile phase 15% of final height (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7). A high growth rate means a small shortfall in intake shows up quickly on the chart.\n2. **The driver of growth in this phase is nutrition itself** — not hormones, not genes. Anything that reduces intake or absorption translates directly into lost growth, which is not true of the childhood phase, where GH keeps a well-nourished child growing through minor illnesses.\n3. **The infant is entirely dependent on someone else to feed her**, and the feeding method changes twice in the first year — breast or formula, then complementary feeding from 6 months (`15.2) Infant Feeding.txt`: pureed, mashed and semisolid at 6 months, finger foods at 8 months, family foods at 12 months). Each transition is an opportunity for intake to fall. Infection rates also rise as maternal antibody wanes and weaning foods introduce enteric pathogens.\n\n**What faltering growth actually means.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` is emphatic that it is a “Description, not a diagnosis”, and gives the threshold: “present if an infant’s weight falls across two centile spaces.” Management is “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with the reminder that “Investigations should be guided by history and examination” and that “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake”. Its red-flag list — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — is what separates the common behavioural picture from organic disease. The endpoint file’s own summary slide names the commonest cause: “Most common cause of faltering growth: Inadequate calorie intake” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.27).\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Childhood** — growth is slowest and steadiest here, GH-driven, and the child eats independently. Growth failure in this phase is *less common* but *more sinister*: it is where endocrine disease and chronic illness declare themselves, and a falling centile after age 5 should always be investigated.\n- **Pubertal** — the growth spurt is a period of high demand, and undernutrition in adolescence can delay puberty and blunt the spurt, but the adolescent controls her own intake and has reserves an infant does not.\n- **Fetal** — growth restriction in utero certainly happens (IUGR/SGA), but it is not called “faltering growth”. That term is reserved for postnatal weight crossing centiles. `13) Short stature.txt` handles the fetal equivalent under small for gestational age, noting that “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”.\n\n**A related question in this same section asks the same fact over a five-option list** that adds “Fetal phase” and “Prepubertal phase”; the answer is the same, and both are held because the option sets differ.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Faltering growth is an infant diagnosis. In an older child, a falling centile is not “faltering growth” — it is a symptom that needs a cause.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Faltering growth clusters in infancy, where growth is fast, nutrition-dependent and entirely reliant on the carer.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.111'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-42', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which is the fastest period of growth throughout life?',
  options:['Infancy','Childhood','Puberty','Adolescence'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Growth velocity is highest during infancy, surpassing fetal, childhood, and pubertal phases.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ DEFECT — the printed box contradicts the bank’s own answer elsewhere, and the bank is wrong here.** The key (**Infancy**) is defensible **because *Fetal* is not among the four options**, and among what is offered infancy genuinely has the highest velocity. But the box’s further claim that infancy *surpasses* the fetal period is wrong on the endpoint file’s own evidence:\n\n- Twice in this same section the file asks “What is the fastest period of growth in a normal human?” over a five-option list that **does** include *Fetal*, and keys **Fetal** both times.\n- Its own summary slide states: “Fetal phase (30% of final height, **Fastest period of Growth**)” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7).\n\nThe key is left exactly as the bank prints it, per the rule that the printed key is what the entry carries, and the discrepancy is recorded here so the learner is not confused when the other printing keys *Fetal*. **In an examination: answer *fetal* whenever fetal is offered, and *infancy* only when it is not.**\n\n**Expansion — the velocity curve.** Growth velocity is at its maximum before birth and declines almost continuously afterwards, with a single interruption at puberty:\n\n| Phase | Velocity | Share of final height | Driver |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fetal** | **Highest of life** (peak linear velocity in the second trimester) | **30%** | Placenta, maternal nutrition, uterine environment |\n| **Infancy** (0–2 y) | Highest **postnatal** velocity, decelerating steeply | **15%** | **Nutrition** |\n| **Childhood** (2 y – puberty) | Slowest and steadiest | ~40% | **GH / IGF-1**, thyroid permissive |\n| **Puberty** | A **second peak**, lower than fetal | **15%** | **Sex steroids** |\n\nThe pubertal figures come from `14) Puberty.txt`: the spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, oestrogen contributing “more than androgens”, adding “15% to final adult height”, after which “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. The same deck notes that “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Childhood* is the **slowest** phase — steady, GH-driven, roughly 5–7 cm a year *(the centimetre figure is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. *Puberty* is the second peak, real but smaller than infancy and far smaller than fetal life. *Adolescence* overlaps puberty and is, if anything, the tail of it — by late adolescence the epiphyses are fusing and growth is ending, so it is the weakest option on the list.\n\n**A useful sanity check on the numbers.** A term newborn is about 50 cm and a 1-year-old about 75 cm — roughly 25 cm in the first year. A 5-year-old grows 5–7 cm a year. A boy at peak height velocity manages 9–10 cm a year. Nothing postnatal comes close to the fetal rate, in which a whole 50 cm human is built in 40 weeks *(these velocity figures are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Fetal life has the highest growth velocity, infancy the highest postnatal velocity — answer fetal whenever it is offered.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.113'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-43', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which factor mainly determines the size of a newborn at birth?',
  options:['Maternal size','Paternal size','Growth hormone','Nutrition after birth'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Maternal genetics and uterine environment primarily influence birth size, more than paternal genetics or postnatal nutrition.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — birth size is a statement about the *uterus*, not about the baby’s genes.** The fetus grows inside a maternal environment that constrains it, supplies it, and sets a ceiling on it. Three separate maternal factors combine:\n\n1. **Uterine capacity and maternal size** — a small mother constrains fetal growth regardless of the father’s height. This is the biological reason a first baby of a small mother is not born large even when the father is tall.\n2. **Placental function** — the delivery system for every nutrient and every molecule of oxygen. Placental insufficiency is the mechanism of most asymmetrical growth restriction.\n3. **Maternal nutrition and health** — `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists the causes of small for gestational age in two columns: **asymmetrical IUGR** (“Head spare”, onset after the 18th week of gestation, chronic hypertension, severe malnutrition, genetic mutation) and **symmetrical IUGR** (“Proportionate to the rest of the body”, TORCH infections, chromosomal anomalies, anaemia, substance abuse including fetal alcohol syndrome). Almost every entry on that list is a maternal or placental factor.\n\n**And birth size does not predict adult size.** This is the key clinical corollary, and it is why the question matters. Because birth size reflects the intrauterine environment while later size reflects genetic potential, infants **normally cross centiles** in the first months — upward if they were constrained in utero, downward if the environment was generous. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` therefore sets the threshold for concern at a weight falling “across two centile spaces” rather than at any single crossing.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Paternal size** — contributes to the child’s **genetic** height potential, which becomes visible during the childhood phase, and it enters the mid-parental height calculation that `13) Short stature.txt` requires. But it has little influence on birth size, because the father contributes no uterus.\n- **Growth hormone** — a striking negative that is worth remembering: **fetal growth is essentially GH-independent**. A baby with congenital GH deficiency is born of normal length; the growth failure only becomes apparent through the first year or two. Fetal growth is driven far more by insulin and IGF-2 *(the specific fetal growth factors are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. The insulin point is directly illustrated in the syllabus by the infant of a diabetic mother: `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` gives the mechanism as “Maternal Hyperglycemia, Fetal Hyperglycemia & Hyperinsulinemia”, producing **macrosomia** — fetal insulin acting as a growth factor.\n- **Nutrition after birth** — by definition cannot determine size **at** birth. It is the determinant of growth *during* the infantile phase, which is a different question the bank asks separately.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A small newborn prompts a question about the pregnancy, not about the family’s height. A small 2-year-old prompts a question about the parents.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Birth size is set by maternal size, placental function and maternal health — not by paternal genes or growth hormone.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.115'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-44', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which hormone is least related to growth in early life?',
  options:['Growth hormone','Insulin','Thyroid hormone','IGF-1'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Early infancy growth is largely driven by nutrition, insulin, and thyroid hormones, while GH plays a minor role initially.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this is counter-intuitive and correct, and the counter-intuition is the point.** “Growth hormone” is named for what it does in **childhood**, not in fetal life or early infancy. GH-dependent growth becomes established from roughly the second half of the first year and dominates from about 2 years until puberty. Before that, the growth of a well-fed infant is driven by nutrition, with insulin, thyroid hormone and IGF-1 as the effectors.\n\n| Hormone | Role in **fetal life and early infancy** | Role later |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Growth hormone** | **Minimal** — a GH-deficient newborn is of normal length | **The** childhood driver, via IGF-1 |\n| **Insulin** | **Major fetal growth factor** — drives tissue accretion in response to substrate | Metabolic; permissive for growth |\n| **Thyroid hormone** | **Essential** — for skeletal maturation and, critically, brain development | Permissive throughout; deficiency causes growth failure |\n| **IGF-1** | Active from fetal life, but in infancy it is largely **nutrition-driven rather than GH-driven** | GH-driven and the main mediator of childhood growth |\n\n**Two lines of evidence from the syllabus support each column.**\n\n*For insulin as the fetal growth factor*, `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` gives the infant of a diabetic mother: the aetiology is “Maternal Hyperglycemia, Fetal Hyperglycemia & Hyperinsulinemia”, and the consequence heading the problem list is **macrosomia**. High fetal insulin makes a big baby — a natural experiment proving insulin’s growth role before birth.\n\n*For thyroid hormone*, `52) hypothyrodism.txt` opens by stating its emphasis on “linear growth and brain” development, describes the untreated child as “Short with infantile proportions” with delayed bone age, and devotes several slides to neonatal screening — “Analyzed in central lab. For TSH” — because the damage of untreated congenital hypothyroidism in the first weeks is irreversible. `13) Short stature.txt` lists thyroid hormone among the hormonal influences on growth, and puts “TSH, fT4” in **Step Two** of investigation, ahead of any GH testing.\n\n*For GH’s late arrival*, `13) Short stature.txt` sets a demanding threshold before GH is even investigated — “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)” — and warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”. It also lists the neonatal clues that GH deficiency *is* present, and note that none of them is short length: “Neonate: Hypoglycemia, Prolonged jaundice, microphallus”. GH deficiency in a newborn shows itself metabolically, not by size.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Insulin*, *thyroid hormone* and *IGF-1* are all directly involved in fetal and early infant growth, as above. Only GH is the outlier — and the question is asked in the negative (“least related”), so the outlier is the answer.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A newborn of normal size does not exclude GH deficiency, and a GH assay in a small infant is the wrong first test. Think nutrition, thyroid, and the systemic screen first.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Fetal and early infant growth is driven by nutrition, insulin, thyroid hormone and IGF-1; growth hormone only takes over in the childhood phase.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.117'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-45', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Low birth weight in infants may predispose to which later condition?',
  options:['Childhood obesity','Tall stature','Early puberty','Reduced IQ'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The key as printed is D, reduced IQ, and it is defensible — but note that A is also a recognised consequence, so read the list before answering.**\n\n**Why reduced IQ follows low birth weight.** Low birth weight arises by two routes, and both threaten the brain. A **preterm** infant is born before brain growth is complete and then faces the complications `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists as the frequent prematurity-related problems: “Periventricular-Intraventricular Hemorrhage (IVH)” and “Periventricular Leukomalacia (PVL)”, of which it warns “Both are usually silent in presentation”, plus hypoglycaemia from low glycogen stores, hypothermia, sepsis, and the consequences of prolonged ventilation. A **growth-restricted** term infant has been chronically undersupplied in utero; `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` divides SGA into asymmetrical (“Head spare”, onset after the 18th week, chronic hypertension, severe malnutrition) and symmetrical (“Proportionate to the rest of the body”, TORCH, chromosomal anomalies, anaemia, fetal alcohol syndrome) — and the symmetrical group, in which the head has **not** been spared, carries the worse neurodevelopmental outlook. The association between low birth weight and reduced cognitive scores is graded: the lower the birth weight and the earlier the gestation, the larger the average deficit *(the graded dose-response is not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**⚠️ DEFECT — option A is also correct, and arguably more famous.** Low birth weight is the classic antecedent in the **developmental origins of health and disease** (Barker) hypothesis: a fetus undersupplied in utero adapts its metabolism to scarcity, and when it later meets an abundant food supply it gains fat disproportionately, giving central obesity, insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes and hypertension in adult life. Rapid postnatal catch-up growth is the step that converts the risk into disease *(this entire paragraph is not taken from the course material — none of the cached Pediatrics decks covers the Barker hypothesis or metabolic programming)*. **Option C, early puberty, is likewise a recognised association** in girls born small who show rapid catch-up. The bank keys D and D is left as printed, per the rule that the printed key stands; the alternatives are recorded here so a learner who chose A knows why.\n\n**Why option B is unambiguously wrong.** Low birth weight predisposes to **short**, not tall, stature. `13) Short stature.txt` states it directly under small for gestational age: “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth” — that is, one in ten SGA infants never regains the centile they should have occupied, and SGA without catch-up is a standing cause of short stature in the lecture’s own list.\n\n**The clinical consequence — surveillance, not prediction.** A low birth weight infant needs: growth plotted at **corrected** age (`13) Short stature.txt`: “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age”); developmental surveillance, since `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` requires that skills also be judged at corrected age and gives the worked example of a 9-month-old born at 28 weeks whose skills resemble a 6-month-old’s; hearing and vision checks, given retinopathy of prematurity; and attention to feeding, because catch-up growth needs calories. What must be avoided is the opposite error — assuming a poor outcome. Most low birth weight children develop normally, and the purpose of follow-up is early intervention, which `1) Normal development.txt` places in the “first 2- 3 ys of age (critical period)”.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Low birth weight is a marker of risk across three domains at once — cognitive, growth, and later metabolic — and the answer to a single-best-answer question depends on which of them is offered.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Low birth weight predisposes to reduced IQ, to short stature where catch-up fails, and — via metabolic programming — to later obesity.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.119'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-46', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which type of scale provides the most accurate measurement of infant weight?',
  options:['Electronic scale','Spring balance','Bathroom scale','Manual balance'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Electronic scales are precise for small weight changes, unlike spring, bathroom, or manual balances.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the measurement is the diagnosis, so the instrument matters.** Everything in this chapter rests on serial weights: `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines faltering growth as a weight falling “across two centile spaces” and instructs you to “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart”; `13) Short stature.txt` states that “A single measurement is less important than growth trend” and that “Serial measurements show pattern of growth”. A trend can only be trusted if each point on it is trustworthy — and in an infant the changes being tracked are tens of grams, so a scale that reads to the nearest 100 g cannot detect what you are looking for.\n\n| Instrument | Typical resolution | Why it fails for an infant |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Electronic (digital) infant scale** | **To the gram or 5–10 g**, with a tare function and often a damping/averaging mode for a moving baby | — it is the answer |\n| Spring balance | Coarse; the spring **stretches and drifts** with age and load | Loses calibration silently; poor reproducibility |\n| Bathroom scale | 100–500 g, designed for an adult’s weight range | Resolution far too coarse; a baby cannot stand still on it |\n| Manual (beam) balance | Good when correctly calibrated and read | Slow, operator-dependent reading, and hard to use with a squirming infant |\n\n*(The resolution figures are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the cached decks require accurate serial measurement without specifying instruments.)*\n\n**The technique matters as much as the device.** Weigh the infant **naked or in a clean nappy of known weight**, on the same scale, at a similar time relative to feeds, with the scale zeroed (tared) immediately beforehand and standing on a hard level surface. Using a different scale at each visit introduces a step change in the growth line that can be mistaken for faltering or for catch-up. The same discipline applies to length: `13) Short stature.txt` devotes two consecutive slides to “How to measure Height?” and “How to accurately measure Height?”, and the corresponding rule is that under about 3 years the child is measured **recumbent** with two observers, and standing thereafter — never interchangeably, since the two methods differ by about a centimetre.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Spring balance* — the archetype of a drifting instrument; springs fatigue, so the error is systematic, not random, and it grows over exactly the months you are trying to compare. *Bathroom scale* — the wrong resolution and the wrong weight range, and unusable for a baby who cannot stand. *Manual balance* — genuinely accurate when calibrated and read properly, which is why it is the strongest distractor, but it is slower, needs a still subject, and its reading is observer-dependent.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Before you diagnose faltering growth, check the plot: same scale, tared, naked baby, correct chart, correct (or corrected) age. A surprising proportion of apparent growth failure is a measurement artefact.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Serial infant weights need a tared electronic scale reading to the gram; coarse or drifting instruments manufacture false growth trends.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.121'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-47', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'How should height be measured in children below 3 years of age?',
  options:['Recumbent length (lying down)','Standing height','Sitting height','Arm span'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children under 3 cannot reliably stand straight; recumbent measurement is standard.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — why the method changes at about 2–3 years, and why it must not be mixed.** A toddler cannot stand still, straight, with heels down, buttocks and shoulders against a stadiometer, looking ahead in the Frankfurt plane. **Recumbent length** removes all of that: the child lies on a length board, one observer holds the head against the fixed headboard with the eyes vertical, and a second observer straightens the legs and brings the footboard to the soles. It needs two people, which is precisely why it is abandoned as soon as the child can cooperate.\n\nThe practical consequence is the part examiners test: **recumbent length reads about 0.5–1 cm greater than standing height in the same child**, because the spine is not compressed by gravity and the joints are fully extended. Switching methods therefore produces an artefactual **drop** in the plotted line that can be mistaken for growth failure *(the magnitude of the difference is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. This is also why the growth charts themselves change: `13) Short stature.txt` gives the WHO chart as “Recommended for 0-24 months” and the CDC chart as “Recommended for 2-19 years”, and the WHO chart is built on **length** while the CDC chart is built on **height**. Use the chart that matches the method.\n\n**The lecture treats measurement as a skill in its own right**, devoting consecutive slides to “How to measure Height?” and “How to accurately measure Height?”, and building every definition on top of it: short stature is “Height >2 SDs below the mean for age and sex (<3rd percentile)”, “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”, and “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”. A velocity is the difference between two measurements, so a 1 cm method error in either one can double or erase an apparent year’s growth. Hence “A single measurement is less important than growth trend” — but only if the trend is measured the same way each time.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Standing height** — the correct method from about 2–3 years onward, once the child can cooperate. Attempting it in a 1-year-old produces an unreliable, usually underestimated, number.\n- **Sitting height** — a real and useful measurement, but for a different purpose: it is part of assessing **body proportions**. `13) Short stature.txt` includes a slide on “Sitting height” and lists “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)” under examination, because proportion is what separates a skeletal dysplasia such as achondroplasia from proportionate short stature. It is an adjunct, never the primary measure of stature.\n- **Arm span** — likewise a proportion measurement, used to detect disproportionate limb length (long limbs in Marfan syndrome, short limbs in achondroplasia). It approximates height in an adult but is not a substitute for measuring it, least of all in a toddler.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Under 3, lie the child down and use a length board and two people. Over 3, stand the child up. Record which method was used, and never compare one with the other.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Measure recumbent length under about 3 years and standing height thereafter — and never mix the two, since length reads ~1 cm greater.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.123'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-48', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What does head circumference mainly reflect?',
  options:['Brain growth','Skull bone growth','Body weight','Muscle mass'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Brain growth drives skull expansion in infancy; weight or muscle mass has minimal impact.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the skull is a passive container, and the brain inflates it.** The infant calvarium is a set of separate bones joined by open sutures and fontanelles. It expands **because the brain expands**, not the other way round, so occipitofrontal circumference (OFC) is a non-invasive proxy for brain growth. The endpoint file’s own summary slide states it directly: “Head circumference (OFC) ... Reflects brain growth” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.8).\n\nThe converse gives the two disorders that flank the normal range:\n\n| | **Microcephaly** | **Macrocephaly** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Definition | OFC well below the expected range for age and sex | OFC well above it |\n| Mechanism | The brain has failed to grow, or has been injured | The brain, CSF or blood inside the skull has increased |\n| Typical causes | Congenital infection (TORCH), perinatal asphyxia and HIE, severe prenatal insults, genetic and metabolic syndromes | **Raised intracranial pressure — hydrocephalus, subdural haematoma, brain tumour**; benign familial macrocephaly; some metabolic and storage disorders |\n\nThe macrocephaly causes are from the endpoint file’s own summary slide (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.20: “Macrocephaly ... Causes: ... Raised ICP (hydrocephalus, subdural hematoma, brain tumor)”). `1) Normal development.txt` includes both **MICROCEPHALY** and **SEVERE HYDROCEPHALUS** among the conditions recognisable from the face in a child with intellectual disability, and lists “TOTAL CRANIOSTENOSIS” beside them — the third mechanism, in which the skull itself restricts the brain rather than following it.\n\n**Why the crossing of centiles is what matters, not the absolute value.** A big-headed baby with big-headed parents whose OFC tracks the 97th centile is normal (benign familial macrocephaly). A baby whose OFC **crosses upward** through centiles while the anterior fontanelle bulges, the sutures separate and the sun-setting sign appears has progressive hydrocephalus and needs urgent imaging. Equally, an OFC that **falls away** from its centile in the first year signals acquired brain injury or a failure of brain growth. Plot it at every visit alongside weight and length — a triad of measurements, as `16) P.E.M.txt` requires under anthropometric data (“Head, chest & abdominal circumf.”).\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Skull bone growth** — the tempting answer, and precisely backwards. The bones grow *at the sutures, in response to* brain expansion. The proof is craniosynostosis: fuse a suture and the skull deforms rather than restricting the brain, because the brain still has to go somewhere; and in total craniosynostosis, where the brain genuinely is restricted, the result is raised intracranial pressure, not a small brain.\n- **Body weight** — head circumference is remarkably **independent** of nutritional state. In malnutrition the head is spared longest, which is exactly why the asymmetrical growth-restricted fetus in `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` is described as “Head spare”. An infant whose head circumference is falling with her weight has a far more serious problem than one whose weight alone is falling.\n- **Muscle mass** — no relationship; muscle bulk is assessed by mid-arm circumference and by inspection, and `16) P.E.M.txt` uses “Mid-arm circumference (11.5 cm)” for exactly that.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Measure the head at every infant visit and plot it. A head circumference crossing centiles in either direction is a neurological finding.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Head circumference reflects brain growth, is spared in malnutrition, and must be plotted serially — crossing centiles in either direction is neurological.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.125'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-49', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which index is used to assess underweight and obesity?',
  options:['Body Mass Index (BMI)','Mid-arm circumference','Waist/hip ratio','Growth velocity'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> BMI accounts for height and weight and is widely used to classify underweight, overweight, and obesity.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — BMI, and the two things that make the paediatric version different.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` states the definition and the method:\n\n- “Obesity is defined as an excessive accumulation of body fat.”\n- “Body mass index (BMI) is the most reliable and most commonly used clinical indicator of obesity.”\n- “BMI is calculated by dividing the weight in Kg by the height in meters squared (kg/m2).”\n- “In children BMI should be plotted on appropriate charts.”\n- “BMI in children is **age- and sex-specific**.”\n\nThat last pair of lines is the examinable difference. An adult uses fixed cut-offs; a child cannot, because body composition changes continuously with age and differs between the sexes. So a child’s BMI is converted to a **centile for age and sex**:\n\n| BMI centile | Category (`15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt`) |\n|---|---|\n| **> 95th** | **OBESE** |\n| **85th – 95th** | **Over weight** |\n| **5th – 85th** | **Healthy Wt** |\n\n**Why it matters that BMI is plotted rather than read.** The same deck gives the management framework once a child is classified — “Eat Healthy, More active, ↓sedentary activity”, with “Successful management requires sustained changes in lifestyle”; drug treatment (orlistat, “a lipase inhibitor which reduces the absorption of dietary fat and thus produces steatorrhoea”) only “in children over the age of 12 years who have physical co-morbidities or severe psychological problems”; and bariatric surgery only in children who have “almost achieved maturity”, with a BMI above 40, or 35–40 with complications such as type 2 diabetes or hypertension, after all else has failed. It also names the causes: idiopathic obesity, and endogenous obesity from endocrine causes (hypothyroidism, hypercortisolism, acquired hypothalamic syndrome), syndromic obesity such as Prader–Willi, and gene defects such as leptin deficiency.\n\n**The one-line triage that goes with BMI.** `13) Short stature.txt`: “A. Nutritional Obesity: Obese + Tall. B. Endocrinal causes: Obese + Short/poor height velocity.” A fat, tall child is over-eating; a fat, short child with a poor height velocity has an endocrine disorder. Always plot the **height** alongside the BMI.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Mid-arm circumference** — a rapid screen for **acute severe malnutrition**, and `16) P.E.M.txt` gives it under anthropometric data with the 11.5 cm cut-off. It detects wasting; it says nothing about obesity.\n- **Waist/hip ratio** — an adult measure of **central fat distribution** and cardiovascular risk. It is not used to classify underweight, and paediatric norms are not part of the cached material.\n- **Growth velocity** — cm per year, a measure of the **rate of linear growth**. `13) Short stature.txt` uses it to define short stature (“Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”) and to trigger GH investigation (“Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)”). It is about height over time, not about body composition.\n\n**Filing note.** Secondary chapter **nutrition**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'BMI (kg/m²) classifies underweight and obesity, and in children must be plotted as an age- and sex-specific centile: >95th obese, 85th–95th overweight.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.127'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-50', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is constitutional growth delay considered as?',
  options:['A variation of normal growth','Growth hormone deficiency','Malnutrition','Endocrine disorder'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> It is a benign pattern with delayed growth and puberty but normal adult height.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the whole chapter is summarised in this one answer.** `13) Short stature.txt` closes with a summary slide whose first statement is: **“Most short children are normal variants.”** There are two such variants, and constitutional delay of growth and puberty is one of them:\n\n| | **Familial short stature** | **Constitutional delay (CDGP)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nature | Normal variant | **Normal variant** |\n| Parents | Short | Normal height; often a late-developing parent |\n| Bone age | Normal | **Delayed** |\n| Puberty | Normal timing | **Delayed** |\n| Growth velocity | Normal | Normal, then a late catch-up |\n| **Final adult height** | Short, as predicted | **NORMAL** |\n| Sex | Either | Boys |\n| Investigation needed | None | Bone age, and enough screening to exclude disease |\n\n**Why it is a variant and not a disease.** Nothing is missing and nothing is failing. The child’s biological clock simply runs behind the calendar. Because the epiphyses stay open longer, the growing period is extended, and the height that looked lost is recovered — which is why the defining feature is a **normal final adult height**. `13) Short stature.txt` bears this out in Case 2: a 13-year-old boy with no puberty yet, a father who was a “late bloomer”, Tanner stage 1, a bone age delayed 2 years and **normal labs**, answered as constitutional delay of growth and puberty and managed with “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” Treatment, where given, is for the psychological burden — not to buy height.\n\n**The one caution, and it is important.** “Normal variant” is a conclusion, not a starting assumption. A delayed bone age is shared by CDGP **and** by endocrine disease: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ **Endocrine**.” So a child is only labelled constitutionally delayed once the screening that `13) Short stature.txt` sets out has been passed — Step One (CBC, ESR, renal functions, electrolytes, LFT), Step Two (TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, bone age, karyotyping in all girls) — and, in a girl, only once Turner syndrome has been excluded. The lecture’s Case 3 exists to make that point: a 7-year-old girl with a bone age delayed 5 years turned out to have **mosaic Turner syndrome**, which may show “No, or minimal manifestations”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — a genuine disease with low IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, a growth velocity under 4 cm a year, severe short stature, confirmation by two GH provocation tests, and treatment with daily subcutaneous recombinant human GH. Untreated, final height is **lost**; in CDGP it is not.\n- **Malnutrition** — PEM causes growth failure with weight affected more than height (`16) P.E.M.txt`), with clinical signs — loss of subcutaneous fat, muscle wasting, oedema in kwashiorkor — and normal labs would not be expected.\n- **Endocrine disorder** — the closest wrong answer, since endocrine disease shares the delayed bone age. But in CDGP the endocrine axes are **normal**; the timing of their activation is simply late.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Constitutional delay is a diagnosis of normality that must nevertheless be earned by excluding disease — and once earned, its management is explanation and reassurance.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay of growth and puberty is a normal variant with a delayed bone age, late puberty and a normal final adult height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.129'
},
{
  id:'pedep-gp-51', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which condition presents with delayed puberty and short stature in teenage years?',
  options:['Constitutional growth delay','Familial short stature','Turner syndrome','Hypothyroidism'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Teens with delayed puberty and proportionate short stature usually have constitutional delay, often with family history.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — three words in the stem select the answer: *delayed puberty*, *short stature*, *teenage*.** Only one condition needs all three to be present at once, and it is constitutional delay of growth and puberty.\n\n| Option | Delayed puberty? | Short in the teens? | Presents in the teens? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Constitutional growth delay** | **Yes — defining** | **Yes** | **Yes — that is when it declares itself** |\n| Familial short stature | **No** — puberty on time | Yes | Usually noticed much earlier |\n| Turner syndrome | Yes (girls only) | Yes | Often much earlier, and with stigmata |\n| Hypothyroidism | Yes | Yes | Any age, with systemic symptoms |\n\n**Why the timing is diagnostic.** Constitutionally delayed children grow along a low-normal line without attracting attention. They become conspicuous at 12–14, when their classmates enter the pubertal growth spurt and pull away — `14) Puberty.txt` records that “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys”, so a boy who is himself two years behind his own cohort can appear four years behind the girls in his class. The definition to apply first is in the same deck: delayed puberty is “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by 13 yrs of age in females or by 14 yrs of age in males.”\n\n**The confirmatory test and the management.** A bone age of the left wrist and hand read against Greulich and Pyle: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial” (`13) Short stature.txt`). The lecture’s Case 2 gives the management for exactly this patient: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Familial short stature** — the short child of short parents, with a **normal** bone age and **normally timed** puberty. It fails on the delayed-puberty requirement, which is the single most discriminating item in the stem.\n- **Turner syndrome** — a real cause of short stature with delayed puberty, but only in **girls**, and it is **hypergonadotropic** (raised LH and FSH from gonadal dysgenesis) rather than a delay in activation. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` describes it as “a chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism”, with neck webbing, widely spaced nipples, a wide carrying angle, spoon-shaped nails and coarctation of the aorta. It also usually causes short stature from early childhood, not from the teens. Because mosaic Turner can be almost silent, `13) Short stature.txt` still requires “Karyotyping (All girls)”.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — causes short stature and can delay puberty (`14) Puberty.txt` lists “Acquired hypothyroidism” under hypogonadotropic hypogonadism), but it brings the systemic picture with it: lethargy, constipation, cold intolerance, dry skin, declining school performance, and the characteristic growth pattern of **height falling while weight is preserved or rises** (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`; `13) Short stature.txt`: “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”). A plain delayed teenager without those features is not hypothyroid — but TSH and fT4 are still checked, in Step Two, before the label of constitutional delay is applied.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In the teenage clinic, delayed puberty plus short stature plus a normal, well child plus a family history of late development equals constitutional delay — confirmed by a bone age, and treated mostly with an explanation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Short stature with delayed puberty declaring itself in the teenage years is constitutional delay of growth and puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.131'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-52', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Growth during childhood is usually:',
  options:['Slower than in infancy','Faster than infancy','Equal to puberty','Independent of nutrition'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Childhood growth is steadier and slower compared to rapid infant growth.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the shape of the growth-velocity curve is the single most useful picture in this chapter.** Velocity is maximal before birth and falls steeply through infancy, reaches its lowest sustained value in mid-childhood, then rises to a second, smaller peak at puberty before falling to zero at epiphyseal fusion.\n\n| Phase | Velocity | Share of final height | Driver |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fetal | **Highest of life** | **30%** | Placenta, maternal nutrition |\n| **Infancy** (0–2 y) | Very fast, decelerating (~25 cm in year one) | **15%** | **Nutrition** |\n| **Childhood** (2 y – puberty) | **Slowest and steadiest** (~5–7 cm/year) | ~40% | **GH / IGF-1** |\n| Puberty | Second peak (~8–10 cm/year at its maximum) | **15%** | Sex steroids |\n\nThe 30% and 15% figures are from the endpoint file’s own summary slide (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7) and the pubertal 15% from `14) Puberty.txt` (“Add 15% to final adult height”); *the centimetre-per-year velocities are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material*.\n\n**Note that the childhood phase contributes the largest share of final height despite the slowest velocity** — simply because it lasts the longest, about eight to ten years. Slow does not mean unimportant, and a sustained small deficit over that period costs a great deal of height. This is why `13) Short stature.txt` makes growth **velocity** rather than height a criterion in its own right: short stature is defined partly as “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”, and GH investigation is triggered by a “Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Faster than infancy** — the reverse. An infant grows roughly four to five times faster than a school-age child.\n- **Equal to puberty** — no: puberty is a distinct acceleration. `14) Puberty.txt` describes the pubertal growth spurt as a separate event, “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, with oestrogen contributing “more than androgens”, adding 15% to adult height, and terminated by the same steroids causing “fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. If childhood velocity equalled pubertal velocity there would be no spurt to see, and Tanner staging against height velocity would be meaningless.\n- **Independent of nutrition** — dangerously wrong. Growth in childhood is *less immediately* nutrition-limited than in infancy, because GH dominates and a well child has reserves — but chronic undernutrition stunts, and `16) P.E.M.txt` lists growth failure as the first clinical feature of protein–energy malnutrition, with weight typically affected more than height (`13) Short stature.txt`: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease”). Adequate nutrition is a **precondition** for GH to act, not an irrelevance.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Because childhood velocity is low and steady, deviation from it is easy to see and always meaningful. A school-age child crossing centiles downward is abnormal, and this is the phase in which endocrine disease and chronic illness are most often unmasked.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Childhood growth is the slowest, steadiest phase — but it lasts longest and supplies the largest share of final height, so deviation always matters.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.133'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-53', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'In constitutional growth delay, the final adult height is usually:',
  options:['Normal','Less than expected','Below 150 cm','Above average'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Although delayed during adolescence, final adult height is typically within the normal range.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this is the defining fact of the condition, and the reason it is not a disease.** In constitutional delay of growth and puberty the biological clock is set back but nothing is broken. Because sex steroids are what close the growth plates — `14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth” — a child whose puberty is late keeps his epiphyses open for longer and therefore keeps growing after his peers have stopped. The height that appeared to be lost during adolescence is recovered afterwards.\n\n**Two consequences follow, and both are examinable.**\n\n1. **Reassurance is the treatment.** `13) Short stature.txt`, Case 2: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” The testosterone is offered for the psychological burden of being smallest and least developed in the year group; it does not increase, and must not be allowed to reduce, final height.\n2. **The mirror image is precocious puberty**, in which early sex-steroid exposure closes the plates early and produces a child who is **tall for age but short as an adult**. That is precisely the loss GnRH agonist therapy is given to prevent.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Less than expected** — this is the outcome of **pathological** short stature: untreated growth hormone deficiency, chronic disease, Turner syndrome, untreated hypothyroidism, severe malnutrition. `13) Short stature.txt` describes GH deficiency treated with “recombinant human GH... Daily subcutaneous injection”, and monitored by “Increase in height and change in height velocity” — treatment exists precisely because final height would otherwise be less than expected. If a child labelled “constitutional delay” ends up shorter than his mid-parental height, the label was wrong.\n- **Below 150 cm** — a specific pathological figure; untreated Turner syndrome is the classic example, which is why `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` notes growth hormone therapy against “Short stature” in its Turner list. It has nothing to do with a normal variant.\n- **Above average** — no mechanism. The extended growing period recovers the child’s **own** genetic potential; it does not exceed it. Final height in CDGP is expected to fall within the mid-parental range, which is exactly the retrospective test the bank applies elsewhere in this section.\n\n**The one caveat worth stating to a family.** “Normal” means normal *for that family* — a constitutionally delayed boy whose parents are both short will still end up a short adult, because the mid-parental height is the target. What CDGP guarantees is that nothing has been **lost**, not that the outcome will be tall.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** If the bone age is delayed, puberty is late, the labs are normal and the family history fits, tell the family plainly: he will be later than his friends and he will get there. The prognosis is the diagnosis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay postpones growth without losing it — final adult height is normal and within the mid-parental range.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.135'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-54', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'The infantile growth phase is largely dependent on:',
  options:['Nutrition','Genetics','Hormones','Puberty'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Early postnatal growth is primarily nutrition-dependent.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ A third option-set variant of one fact.** The endpoint file asks the driver of infantile growth three times in this section over three **different** option lists — a five-option version separating *infant* from *maternal* nutrition, a four-option version testing nutrition against genetic, hormonal and activity factors, and this one. Because the option sets are replaced rather than trimmed, each is held as a separate question by the standard folding test. The fact is one fact; the discriminations differ.\n\n**Expansion — the four phases and their drivers.**\n\n| Phase | Age | Driver | Share of final height |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fetal | to birth | Placenta, maternal nutrition, uterine environment | **30%** |\n| **Infantile** | **birth to ~2 y** | **NUTRITION** | **15%** |\n| Childhood | ~2 y to puberty | **GH / IGF-1**, thyroid permissive | ~40% |\n| Pubertal | puberty to fusion | **Sex steroids** | **15%** |\n\n(Fetal and infantile percentages from the endpoint file’s own summary slide, `Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7; the pubertal figure from `14) Puberty.txt`.)\n\n**Why nutrition and not hormones, when hormones are obviously present.** Hormones are present but are not **rate-limiting** in infancy. A newborn with congenital growth hormone deficiency has a normal birth length, and GH-dependent growth only becomes established through the first year or two — which is why `13) Short stature.txt` lists the neonatal clues of GH deficiency as “Hypoglycemia, Prolonged jaundice, microphallus” rather than short length. Thyroid hormone is genuinely essential in this period, both for skeletal maturation and for the brain, which is why newborn screening exists (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`: “Analyzed in central lab. For TSH”) — but it is **permissive**: its absence causes disaster, its presence does not set the rate. What sets the rate is calorie and protein supply.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Genetics** — sets the destination, not the early speed. This is exactly why infants normally **cross centiles** in the first months, migrating from the size the uterus produced toward the size their genes specify. It becomes the visible determinant during the childhood phase.\n- **Hormones** — the childhood and pubertal answer. Offering it for infancy is a phase error, and the bank tests the correct pairing directly elsewhere by keying growth hormone for the childhood phase.\n- **Puberty** — not a determinant of infant growth at all; it is a later phase. (Note the neonatal “mini-puberty” illustrated in `14) Puberty.txt`, a transient postnatal surge of gonadotrophins and sex steroids — real, but it does not drive somatic growth.)\n\n**The clinical translation.** Because infancy is nutrition-driven, growth faltering clusters there, and the first response is a feeding history. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` calls faltering growth a “Description, not a diagnosis”, present when weight falls “across two centile spaces”, managed by “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with investigations “guided by history and examination”. The red flag that redirects you away from simple intake is telling: “Failure to gain weight **despite adequate caloric intake**.”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The infantile growth phase is nutrition-dependent; hormones are permissive and genes set the destination rather than the rate.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.137'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-55', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'The infantile growth phase accounts for approximately how much of eventual height?',
  options:['40%','30%','20%','15%'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The key is D, 15%, and the endpoint file states it on its own summary slide:** “Infantile phase (**15% of final height**)” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7). The same slide gives the neighbouring figure that generates every distractor on this list: “Fetal phase (**30% of final height**, Fastest period of Growth)”.\n\n**The whole ledger of final height, phase by phase.**\n\n| Phase | Timing | Share of final height | Driver | Velocity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fetal** | conception → birth | **30%** | Placenta, maternal nutrition, uterine environment | **Fastest of life** |\n| **Infantile** | birth → ~2 years | **15%** | **Nutrition** | Fastest postnatal, steeply decelerating |\n| **Childhood** | ~2 years → puberty | ~40% | **GH / IGF-1**, thyroid permissive | Slowest and steadiest |\n| **Pubertal** | puberty → epiphyseal fusion | **15%** | **Sex steroids** | Second peak |\n\nThe pubertal 15% is stated in `14) Puberty.txt`: the growth spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, with oestrogen contributing “more than androgens”, and it “Add[s] 15% to final adult height”, after which “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. The childhood share is the remainder — the largest single block, because although its velocity is the lowest, it lasts eight to ten years *(the arithmetic of the remainder is inference, not a printed figure — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why 15% is a smaller number than it feels like it should be.** In absolute terms infancy is spectacular: a baby grows roughly 25 cm in the first year and about 12 cm in the second, so around half of birth length is added in two years. But *final adult height* is the denominator, and 37 cm of a 170 cm adult is close to a fifth — much of which is offset because the fetal phase has already banked 50 cm, or 30%. The apparent mismatch between the drama of infant growth and its modest share of adult height is exactly why the examiner asks it *(the centimetre figures are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **40%** — closest to the **childhood** share, which is the largest of the four. Choosing it for infancy confuses the phase that grows fastest with the phase that grows longest.\n- **30%** — the **fetal** share, printed on the same slide. This is the trap answer, and it catches anyone who remembers a number without remembering which phase it belongs to.\n- **20%** — no phase carries this value; it is a plausible-looking filler placed between the two real figures.\n\n**Why the shares matter clinically.** They tell you what can still be recovered. An insult in fetal life has already cost part of a fixed 30%, and `13) Short stature.txt` notes that “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”. An insult in infancy threatens 15% but is the most reversible, because the driver — nutrition — is the one thing a clinician can directly supply. An insult in puberty is the least recoverable, because sex steroids simultaneously drive the spurt and close the plates, so time lost there cannot be regained.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Learn the four percentages as a set — 30 / 15 / ~40 / 15 — because the bank asks for each of them in turn and the distractors are always the other three.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The infantile phase contributes about 15% of final height, against 30% fetal and a further 15% at puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.139'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-56', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the most common cause of faltering growth in infants?',
  options:['Inadequate weight gain','Genetic short stature','Growth hormone deficiency','Endocrine disorder'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Insufficient nutrition is the leading cause, rather than endocrine or genetic disorders.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the file states this on its own summary slide.** “Most common cause of faltering growth: **Inadequate calorie intake**” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.27). The keyed option, “inadequate weight gain”, is that idea expressed as the observation rather than the mechanism, and the point is the same: faltering growth is overwhelmingly a **nutritional** problem, and overwhelmingly a problem of what goes in.\n\n**Why intake dominates.** Infancy is the nutrition-driven phase — the endpoint file’s summary slide gives it 15% of final height, and the driver is what reaches the gut rather than any hormone. So anything that reduces intake shows up on the weight line almost immediately: poor feeding technique or attachment, insufficient breast milk, over-dilute formula, an aversive or frustrating feeding relationship, illness-related anorexia, neglect, or poverty. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` accordingly gives management as “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, notes that “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake”, and insists that “Investigations should be guided by history and examination” — not fired off as a panel.\n\n**The framing that prevents over-investigation.** The same deck opens by calling faltering growth a **“Description, not a diagnosis”**, “present if an infant’s weight falls across two centile spaces”, and closes by repeating that it “is not a disease, but rather a description of a relatively common growth pattern”. Before anything else, “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart” — and the first question is always whether the plot is even correct (right chart, right age, corrected age in a preterm infant, same scale).\n\n**What moves you off the nutritional answer.** The red-flag list from `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt`:\n\n- Dysmorphic features\n- **Failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake**\n- Recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea, or dehydration\n- Organomegaly or lymphadenopathy\n- Signs suggestive of chronic respiratory disease\n- Developmental delay\n\nThe second of these is the pivot: if the calories are genuinely going in and the weight is still not rising, the problem is malabsorption, loss, or increased expenditure, and the differential opens up — coeliac disease, cow’s milk protein enteropathy, cystic fibrosis, chronic infection, a significant cardiac shunt.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Genetic short stature** — familial short stature is a **normal variant** that tracks a low centile without crossing it. It affects **height** in line with the parents, not weight, and it does not produce faltering.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — rare, and it is a childhood-phase disorder. `13) Short stature.txt` requires severe short stature more than 3 SD below the mean, height affected **more** than weight, and a growth velocity under 4 cm a year before it is even investigated, and warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”. Faltering growth in infancy is weight-led, which is the opposite pattern.\n- **Endocrine disorder** — the general form of the same error. `13) Short stature.txt` gives the discriminator in two lines: “Weight affected > Height → **Systemic disease**; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → **Endocrine**.” Faltering growth means weight is leading, so it points away from the endocrine column.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Weigh, plot, and take a feeding history. Most faltering infants need a feeding plan and a follow-up appointment, not a blood test.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Faltering growth in infancy is usually inadequate calorie intake — investigate only when a red flag or failure to gain despite adequate intake appears.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.141'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-57', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 7-year-old girl presents to your office with her mother noting pubic hair growth over the last month. The girl also has started to develop breast buds. Growth and development are normal. Maternal history is remarkable for pubarche at age 11 years. Past medical history and family history are otherwise unremarkable. Your physical examination reveals genitalia and breast Tanner stage 3. Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely to be found?',
  options:['Decreased estradiol, luteinizing hormone (LH) detectable, normal bone age for age','Decreased estradiol, LH undetectable, normal bone age for age','Elevated estradiol, LH detectable, advanced bone age for age','Elevated estradiol, LH detectable, normal bone age for age','Elevated estradiol, LH undetectable, advanced bone age for age'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Central precocious puberty shows activation of the HPG axis with elevated LH and estradiol, and bone age is typically advanced relative to chronological age.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the option list is a truth table, and each column must be decided separately.** Every option combines three variables. Work out each one from the clinical picture and only one row survives.\n\n**Column 1 — oestradiol: ELEVATED.** She has breast Tanner stage 3. Breast tissue is an oestrogen end-organ; there is no other way to grow it. Options A and B, which offer *decreased* oestradiol, are refuted by the physical examination alone.\n\n**Column 2 — LH: DETECTABLE.** This is the column that separates central from peripheral precocity, and it is the heart of the question.\n\n| | **Central (gonadotrophin-dependent)** | **Peripheral (gonadotrophin-independent)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanism | “Early activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis” (`14) Puberty.txt`) | Sex steroid from an adrenal or gonadal source, or exogenous |\n| **LH / FSH** | **Detectable / pubertal** | **Suppressed — undetectable** (negative feedback) |\n| Sequence | Follows the normal pubertal sequence | Out of sequence |\n| Ovaries on US | **Multicystic**, uterus enlarging and pear-shaped | Ovaries quiet, or a unilateral mass |\n| GnRH agonist | **Effective** | Ineffective |\n\nHer development has followed the **normal sequence** — breast buds with pubic hair, developing together, both reaching Tanner 3 — and `14) Puberty.txt` states that central precocious puberty “follows the sequence observed in normal puberty”. That, plus the sex and age, makes central disease overwhelmingly likely: the same deck records that central precocity “occurs 5-10 folds more frequently in girls than in boys because ovaries are very sensitive to gonadotrophin secretion”, with “A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty... in girls (90%)”. Option E, with **undetectable** LH, describes peripheral disease and is eliminated.\n\n**Column 3 — bone age: ADVANCED.** Sex steroids accelerate skeletal maturation as well as growth. Any child with sustained, genuine sex-steroid exposure — Tanner 3 in both breast and pubic hair — will have a bone age ahead of her chronological age. This is not incidental: it is the mechanism by which untreated precocious puberty **costs adult height**, because `14) Puberty.txt` records that “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. Option D, offering elevated oestradiol and detectable LH with a **normal** bone age, is internally inconsistent and is eliminated.\n\n**Only option C survives: elevated oestradiol, detectable LH, advanced bone age.**\n\n**The distractor detail in the stem.** “Maternal history is remarkable for pubarche at age 11 years” invites you to reach for a familial or benign-variant answer. It does not help: 11 is a normal age for pubarche, and it does not make 7 normal. Compare the true benign variant, premature adrenarche, which `14) Puberty.txt` defines as “the appearance of sexual hair before the age of 8 yr in girls or 9 yr in boys **without other evidence of maturation or growth acceleration**” — this girl has breast Tanner 3, so she fails that definition outright.\n\n**What happens next.** `14) Puberty.txt` lists the investigations: pelvic ultrasound (“Infantile: tubular; Pubertal: pear-shaped; Endometrial lining identification”) and “MRI brain with contrast for pituitary gland in gonadotropin-dependent PP” — because although 90% of girls are idiopathic, the 10% that are not include hypothalamic and pituitary lesions. Treatment where progression is rapid is a GnRH agonist, to protect final height.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Central precocious puberty gives elevated oestradiol, detectable LH and an advanced bone age; suppressed LH would mean a peripheral source.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.143'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-58', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Parents bring their 6-month-old son to see you. He is symmetrically less than the fifth percentile for height, weight, and head circumference on routine growth curves. He was born at 30 weeks of gestation and weighed 1000 g. He was a planned pregnancy, and his mother’s prenatal course was uneventful until an automobile accident initiated the labor. He was ventilated for 3 days in the intensive care unit (ICU), but otherwise did well without ongoing problems. He was discharged at 8 weeks of life. Which of the following is the mostly likely explanation for his small size?',
  options:['Chromosomal abnormality','Protein-calorie malnutrition','Normal ex-premie infant growth','Intestinal malabsorption','Congenital hypothyroidism'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Preterm infants often track below standard growth curves but follow their own percentile pattern; this symmetric low growth is typical for a healthy ex-premature infant without additional pathology.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note.** The stem prints “the mostly likely explanation”; this has been tidied to “the most likely explanation” and nothing else altered.\n\n**Expansion — do the correction before you do the diagnosis.** He was born at 30 weeks, so he is **10 weeks preterm**. At a chronological age of 6 months (about 26 weeks) his **corrected age is roughly 4 months**. Plotted at corrected age on a standard chart he is far less small than he appears, and the vignette explicitly says his growth is **symmetrical** — height, weight and head circumference all together — which is the signature of a small, well-scaled child rather than a sick one.\n\n`13) Short stature.txt` gives the rule: correction for gestational age is “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age.” `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` gives the developmental counterpart with a worked example: “The anticipated developmental skills of a 9-month-old baby (chronological age) born 3 months early at 28 weeks’ gestation are more like those of a 6-month-old baby (corrected age)”, and “Correction is not required after age of 2 years.”\n\n**Everything else in the history is reassuring, and each detail was placed deliberately.** The cause of the prematurity was **mechanical** — a road traffic accident initiating labour — not a maternal illness, an infection or a placental problem, so there is no chronic intrauterine insult. The pregnancy was otherwise uneventful. He needed only **3 days** of ventilation and “otherwise did well without ongoing problems”, so he escaped the complications that would predict poor growth: `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists chronic lung disease (“Need for oxygen >36 weeks postmenstrual age”), necrotising enterocolitis, intraventricular haemorrhage and periventricular leukomalacia among the frequent hazards of prematurity. He was discharged at 8 weeks, on schedule for his gestation.\n\n**Note also that 1000 g at 30 weeks is appropriate**, not growth-restricted — it is **extremely low birth weight** by the classification in `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` (“LBW - < 2500 grams; VLBW - < 1500 grams; ELBW - < 1000 grams”), which is a statement about *prematurity*, not about being small for dates.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Chromosomal abnormality** — would usually bring dysmorphic features, congenital anomalies or developmental concerns; `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Dysmorphic features” as a specific thing to examine for, and names Turner, Russell–Silver, Down and Noonan among syndromic causes. None is described, and a chromosomal cause does not appear from nowhere in a baby whose prematurity has a mechanical explanation.\n- **Protein-calorie malnutrition** — would give **asymmetrical** failure: weight falling first and furthest, with head circumference spared longest. `16) P.E.M.txt` describes marasmus as weight below 60% of the 50th centile with loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle wasting. Symmetrical smallness across all three parameters is not this.\n- **Intestinal malabsorption** — again weight-led, and would come with stool changes, vomiting or abdominal signs. The history is explicitly free of ongoing problems.\n- **Congenital hypothyroidism** — detected by newborn screening, and by 6 months an untreated case would show coarse facies, a large tongue, an umbilical hernia, constipation, lethargy, a hoarse cry and a wide anterior fontanelle (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`). It also produces the endocrine growth pattern — height affected with weight preserved — not symmetrical smallness with a small head.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In any small ex-preterm infant, correct the age and re-plot **before** reaching for a diagnosis. Then keep plotting: it is the trend at corrected age, not one point, that tells you whether catch-up is happening.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Plot ex-preterm infants at corrected age; symmetrical smallness in a well ex-premature baby with an uncomplicated course is normal.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.145'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-59', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 2-year-old boy had been slightly less than the 50th percentile for weight, height, and head circumference, but in the last 6 months he has fallen to slightly less than the 25th percentile for weight. The pregnancy was normal, his development is as expected, and the family reports no psychosocial problems. The mother says that he is now a finicky eater (wants only macaroni and cheese at all meals), but she insists that he eat a variety of foods. The meals are marked by much frustration for everyone. His examination is normal. Which of the following is the best next step in his care?',
  options:['Sweat chloride testing','Ophthalmologic examination for retinal hemorrhages','Reassurance and counseling for family about normal childhood development','Testing of stool for parasites','Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Mild faltering due to picky eating in an otherwise healthy child is common; supportive counseling and monitoring are preferred over unnecessary tests.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — measure the fall before you react to it.** He has moved from “slightly less than the 50th” to “slightly less than the 25th” centile for weight. That is a fall of **less than one centile space**, and `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` sets the threshold for faltering growth at a weight falling “**across two centile spaces**”. By the syllabus’s own definition this child does not yet meet the criterion — and his height and head circumference have not moved at all.\n\n**Then check the red flags, and note that all of them are absent.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` lists: dysmorphic features; failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake; recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration; organomegaly or lymphadenopathy; signs suggestive of chronic respiratory disease; developmental delay. He has none. His development is “as expected”, his examination is normal, and the pregnancy was normal.\n\n**What he does have is a classic, age-appropriate feeding conflict.** Food neophobia and fixed food preferences peak in the second and third years, and the frustration described — a mother who “insists that he eat a variety of foods”, with meals “marked by much frustration for everyone” — is the mechanism by which a normal phase becomes a self-sustaining problem. Pressure at mealtimes reduces intake. `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` addresses precisely this under **active responsive feeding**: it “Allows the child to guide feeding”, “Balances helping with encouraging self-feeding”, “Minimizes distractions during meals and feedings”, “Offers different food combinations, tastes, and textures”, and adds “Children are slow eaters; Do not hurry the infant while eating..... Be patient” and “Do not be frustrated from mealtime mess. children learn out of mess”. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` makes the same point in one line: “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake.”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Sweat chloride testing** — the test for cystic fibrosis, which would present with weight-led failure **plus** a chest or gut story: recurrent respiratory infections, chronic cough, bulky offensive stools, and usually a much steeper fall. “Signs suggestive of chronic respiratory disease” is a red flag he does not have.\n- **Ophthalmologic examination for retinal hemorrhages** — a test for **abusive head trauma**. The vignette explicitly states “no psychosocial problems” and a normal examination and development. This option exists to see whether an unsupported suspicion of maltreatment can be planted; neglect and abuse are genuine causes of faltering growth, but nothing here points to them.\n- **Testing of stool for parasites** — would be reasonable with diarrhoea, abdominal pain, anaemia or an exposure history. `16) P.E.M.txt` includes “Stools analysis for ova & parasites” in the work-up of established malnutrition — but that is malnutrition, not a well toddler who has dipped one centile band.\n- **MRI of the brain** — reserved for neurological signs, a falling head circumference, developmental regression, or severe isolated growth failure suggesting hypothalamic–pituitary disease. He has a normal head circumference and normal development.\n\n**What “reassurance and counselling” actually means here.** Not dismissal. It means: explain that toddler food refusal is developmental and self-limiting; stop the mealtime battle, because pressure lowers intake; offer a variety without insisting; keep milk within limits so it does not displace solids; **and re-weigh in a defined interval**. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` requires that you “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart” — follow-up is part of the plan, not an alternative to it. If the weight then crosses a second centile space, or a red flag appears, the work-up begins.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A well toddler who dips less than two centile spaces with normal height, head circumference and development needs feeding advice and follow-up, not tests.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.147, box on p.148'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-60', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'An 8-year-old boy has short stature. He has no significant past medical history and his physical examination is normal. The growth curve shows growth parallel to a growth line at the fifth percentile. His father is 5 ft 4 in (163 cm), and his mother is 5 ft 1 in (155 cm). Radiograph of the left wrist shows a bone age equal to the chronological age. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Reassure the parents that the child has normal prepubertal development.','Order thyroid function tests.','Order chromosomal analysis.','Refer to pediatric endocrinology for evaluation.','Obtain further radiographic imaging.'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Proportionate short stature with growth tracking along a percentile line and normal bone age usually indicates familial short stature. No further testing is required unless growth pattern changes.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — every element needed for the diagnosis has already been supplied, which is why the answer is to stop.**\n\n1. **The growth curve runs *parallel* to the 5th centile.** Parallel means a normal growth velocity. `13) Short stature.txt` defines pathological short stature partly by the opposite — “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” and “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”. Neither applies.\n2. **The bone age equals the chronological age.** This is the decisive investigation and it has already been done. The lecture’s rule is unambiguous: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. **B. Normal → Familial.**”\n3. **Both parents are short** — 163 cm and 155 cm. His height is concordant with his mid-parental height, which is what familial short stature means.\n4. **History and examination are normal**, so there is no systemic disease, no dysmorphism and no skeletal abnormality among the things the lecture requires you to look for.\n\nTogether these give familial short stature — and `13) Short stature.txt` closes with exactly this message: “**Most short children are normal variants.**”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Order thyroid function tests** — TSH and fT4 sit in **Step Two** of the investigation ladder and would be entirely correct in a child whose **height was falling while weight was preserved or rising**, with lethargy or constipation (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`; `13) Short stature.txt`: “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”). Hypothyroidism also **delays** bone age — and this boy’s is normal, which is evidence against it.\n- **Order chromosomal analysis** — the lecture does say “Karyotyping (**All girls**)”, and Turner syndrome is the reason. This patient is a **boy**, and there is no dysmorphism or other feature to suggest a chromosomal disorder.\n- **Refer to pediatric endocrinology for evaluation** — the tempting “safe” answer, and it is wrong for a specific reason: the diagnosis is already made, and referral converts a normal variant into a medical problem in the family’s mind while consuming a clinic slot. `13) Short stature.txt` explicitly discourages escalation, warning “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”, and sets a high bar before growth hormone deficiency is even suspected: “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year).” The 5th centile is about −1.6 SD, his height is not discordant with his mid-parental height, and his velocity is normal — he meets none of the four.\n- **Obtain further radiographic imaging** — a skeletal survey is for **disproportionate** short stature, where a dysplasia such as achondroplasia is suspected; `13) Short stature.txt` requires that “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)” be checked, and his examination is normal. Repeating or extending the imaging adds radiation and no information.\n\n**What “reassure” must include, so that it is safe.** Explain the diagnosis and the expected adult height from the mid-parental calculation; state plainly that he is healthy; **and arrange to re-measure**, because the one thing that would overturn the diagnosis is a change in the slope. A normal variant is defined by continuing to track, so the follow-up measurement is what makes the reassurance evidence rather than opinion.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Growth parallel to a low centile, short parents and a bone age equal to chronological age is familial short stature — reassure and re-measure, do not refer.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.150, box on p.151'
},
{
  id:'pedep-gp-61', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 16-year-old boy complains that he is the shortest boy in his class. He has a normal past medical history, and although always a bit small for age, he has really noticed that he has fallen behind his peers in the last 2 years. He is Tanner stage 3 and is at the 5th percentile for height. His father began puberty at the age of 16 and completed his growth at the age of 19; he is now 6 ft 2 in (188 cm) tall. His mother began her pubertal development at the age of 10 and had her first menstrual period at the age of 13; her height is 5 ft 4 in (163 cm). Which of the following is the single most appropriate first intervention?',
  options:['Chromosomal analysis','Liver function studies','Measurement of bone age','Measurement of somatomedin C','Pediatric endocrinology referral'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Bone age assessment helps differentiate constitutional delay of growth and puberty from pathological causes and is the first step in evaluating delayed puberty.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the father’s history is the loudest clue in the vignette.** “His father began puberty at the age of 16 and completed his growth at the age of 19; he is now 6 ft 2 in (188 cm).” That is a textbook description of constitutional delay in the previous generation, followed by an entirely normal — indeed tall — adult height. `13) Short stature.txt` builds its Case 2 on the same feature, a father who was a “late bloomer”, and answers it as constitutional delay of growth and puberty.\n\nThe boy’s own story fits: always a bit small, but only **falling behind in the last 2 years** — which is when his classmates began their pubertal spurts and he did not. He is Tanner stage 3, so he *is* in puberty, simply later than his peers. With a father of 188 cm and a mother of 163 cm his mid-parental height is well above average, so the 5th centile is markedly discordant — the discordance that would be worrying if it were not explained by delay.\n\n**Why bone age is the first test.** It is cheap, quick, single-view, and it answers the only question that matters at this stage. `13) Short stature.txt`: “Most use: Greulich and Pyle; X-ray left wrist and hand. A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.” A delayed bone age in this boy confirms that he has growing time in hand and that his adult height will approach his mid-parental target. A bone age **equal** to his chronological age would be far more concerning, because it would mean the height is not going to be recovered.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Chromosomal analysis** — the lecture instructs “Karyotyping (All girls)”, for Turner syndrome. In a boy the analogous condition would be Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY), which characteristically produces a **tall** boy with small firm testes, not a short one. Nothing here calls for it.\n- **Liver function studies** — part of the **Step One** systemic screen (“CBC, ESR, Renal functions, Electrolytes, LFT”), appropriate where chronic disease is suspected. His past medical history is normal and he has no systemic features, and a screen ordered without a question in mind mostly generates incidental findings.\n- **Measurement of somatomedin C** — “somatomedin C” is the older name for **IGF-1**. It is a legitimate and useful test, sitting in **Step Two** alongside IGFBP-3, and it is the right test when growth hormone deficiency is genuinely suspected. But GH deficiency requires a poor growth velocity and severe short stature — `13) Short stature.txt` gives the threshold as height more than 3 SD below the mean, height affected more than weight, and velocity under 4 cm a year — and this boy is at the 5th centile and progressing through puberty. The lecture also warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n- **Pediatric endocrinology referral** — reasonable eventually, but not the “single most appropriate **first** intervention”. The bone age can be obtained in primary care, and in most cases it converts the consultation into reassurance without a referral at all.\n\n**What follows a delayed bone age.** `13) Short stature.txt`, Case 2: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” At 16 and already Tanner 3, this boy is progressing on his own; the main intervention is an explanation and a predicted adult height, which for an adolescent worried about being smallest in the class is the treatment he actually needs.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A bone age of the left wrist and hand is the first test in delayed puberty — a delayed bone age with a late-developing parent means constitutional delay.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.153'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-62', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'You are seeing 10-year-old girl for evaluation of short stature. Which of the following are important to the workup?',
  options:['History and physical including examination of growth chart','Parental height, growth pattern, and timing of puberty','Birth weight and length','Assessment for underlying systemic disease','All of the above'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A complete evaluation includes history, growth chart review, parental heights, birth history, and assessment for systemic disease to identify potential causes.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this is `13) Short stature.txt`’s own history-and-examination slide, broken into four options.** The lecture lists under **History**: “Birth size: SGA”, “Growth pattern”, “Nutrition”, “Chronic illness, Drugs”, “Family history of growth and puberty”, and “Mid-parental height calculation”. Under **Examination**: “Accurate height measurement”, “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)”, “Dysmorphic features”, “Skeletal abnormalities”, “Puberty staging”, and “Thyroid signs”. Every option on this list is one of those items, so “All of the above” is not a lazy answer here — it is the syllabus.\n\n**What each element actually decides:**\n\n| Element | The question it answers |\n|---|---|\n| **Growth chart** | Is she **tracking** or **crossing**? Crossing is pathological; tracking is usually a variant. “A single measurement is less important than growth trend.” |\n| **Parental height** | Is she short **for her family**? Mid-parental height turns “short” into “short *relative to potential*”, and short stature is defined partly as “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height” |\n| **Timing of parental puberty** | A late-developing parent supports **constitutional delay**, the second normal variant |\n| **Birth weight and length** | Was she **SGA**? “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”, so an SGA birth explains lifelong short stature without any postnatal disease |\n| **Systemic disease assessment** | Coeliac disease, chronic renal failure, chronic anaemia, inflammatory bowel disease, cystic fibrosis — all cause the pattern “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease” |\n\n**And the item the option list does not name but the vignette makes mandatory: she is a girl.** `13) Short stature.txt` places “Karyotyping (**All girls**)” in its investigation ladder, because Turner syndrome is the commonest missed cause of short stature in a girl and mosaic Turner may show “No, or minimal manifestations” — the lecture’s Case 3 is a 7-year-old girl with a bone age delayed 5 years and no major complaints who proved to have mosaic Turner syndrome.\n\n**Why an “All of the above” option is still worth reasoning through rather than guessing.** The examiner is testing whether you know that short stature is diagnosed by **history and examination**, with investigation used only to confirm. The investigation ladder is deliberately staged for that reason: Step One (CBC, ESR, renal functions, electrolytes, LFT), Step Two (TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, bone age, karyotyping in all girls), Step Three (GH provocation testing) — with the instruction “Do not jump to GH tests early !!” written across it. Each of the four options in this question is a step that must be completed **before** the ladder is climbed at all; skipping them is how a normal variant ends up in an endocrine clinic and how a coeliac child ends up with a GH test.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Plot her, measure her parents, ask about their puberty, ask about her birth, and examine her for chronic disease and dysmorphism. In most children that sequence gives the diagnosis; in a girl, add a karyotype regardless.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Short stature is worked up by history and examination first — growth chart, parental heights and puberty timing, birth size, and a systemic disease screen.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.155'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-63', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 10-year-old girl is 4 ft 10 in (147 cm) tall. Her father is 5 ft 10 in (178 cm) tall, and her mother is 5 ft 5 in (165 cm) tall. Over the past 3 years she has dropped from the 25th percentile for height to the 5th percentile. Her weight has remained stable. Radiographs show bone age is delayed. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her management?',
  options:['Send patient to the emergency department for CT evaluation of the brain.','Obtain growth hormone level.','Obtain IGF-1 level.','Start growth hormone injections.','Provide reassurance to parents'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This child has evidence of possible growth hormone deficiency. Obtaining an IGF-1 level would be the most appropriate initial choice. Growth hormone deficiency is not an emergent condition but the earlier the diagnosis is made the sooner appropriate treatment can be initiated. Obtaining a growth hormone level would not be diagnostically helpful because its secretion is pulsatile. Referral to a pediatric endocrinologist would be warranted if initial screening tests confirm the suspected growth hormone deficiency.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the box is unusually complete for this bank, and the lecture backs every sentence of it.**\n\n**Why this girl is suspected of GH deficiency.** She has dropped from the 25th to the 5th centile over 3 years while her **weight has remained stable** — the classic endocrine pattern of `13) Short stature.txt`: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; **Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine**.” Her parents are of average and above-average height, so she is discordant from her mid-parental height. Her bone age is delayed, consistent with the lecture’s rule that a delayed bone age points to “Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ **Endocrine**”.\n\n**Why a random GH level is useless — the single most testable fact here.** `13) Short stature.txt` prints it as a heading: “**GH testing: No use of basal GH level !**”, with the reason underneath: “**GH secreted in pulses**”. A random sample is as likely to catch a trough as a peak, so a low value proves nothing and a normal value excludes nothing. Confirmation requires provocation: “GH provocation test confirms diagnosis... GH levels fail to rise after stimulation... **2 GH provocation tests (sequential or on separate days) are required**”, using glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine.\n\n**Why IGF-1 is the right screening test.** IGF-1 is the mediator of GH action and, unlike GH, circulates at a **stable** concentration bound to its binding proteins, so a single sample is interpretable. `13) Short stature.txt` places “IGF-1, IGFBP-3” in **Step Two** of the ladder — before Step Three GH testing — and states that in GH deficiency you find “Low levels of IGF-1 and IGFBP-3” together with a “Delayed bone age”. (Note the caveat that makes clinical sense of it: IGF-1 is also low in undernutrition and chronic illness, which is why Step One systemic bloods and the coeliac screen come first.)\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Emergency CT of the brain** — imaging is part of the pathway, but *after* biochemical confirmation and as an MRI, not an emergency CT. `13) Short stature.txt` places it under “Considering potential causes of GHD”: “MRI brain (pituitary view), Fundus: papilledema, Visual field assessment: Visual field loss – bitemporal hemianopia (optic chiasma)”, hunting a craniopharyngioma (“4% – a developmental tumor arising from squamous remnant of Rathke’s pouch... Not truly malignant, but locally invasive”). Slow growth over 3 years is not an emergency; headache with papilloedema would be.\n- **Obtain growth hormone level** — refuted above, and refuted by the lecture in bold.\n- **Start growth hormone injections** — treating before diagnosing. Treatment is “recombinant human GH... Daily subcutaneous injection”, monitored by “Increase in height and change in height velocity”, and it carries real risks: `13) Short stature.txt` lists pseudotumor cerebri, slipped capital femoral epiphysis, worsening of scoliosis, insulin resistance and pancreatitis.\n- **Provide reassurance to parents** — the correct answer for a child **tracking** a low centile with a normal bone age and short parents. This girl is **crossing** centiles with tall parents. Reassurance here would be a missed diagnosis, and the lecture’s point that “Most short children are normal variants” explicitly does not extend to a child whose growth line is falling.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Crossing centiles + stable weight + delayed bone age = screen the GH axis with IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, not with a GH level, and not with a prescription.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Screen the GH axis with IGF-1 and IGFBP-3 — a basal GH level is worthless because GH is secreted in pulses.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.157'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-64', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 5-year-old girl has bilateral breast development that was first noticed 6 months ago. She takes no medications, and no source of exogenous estrogen is present in the home. Family history is unremarkable. Physical examination reveals a girl who is at the 50th percentile for height and weight, with normal blood pressure, normal skin without oiliness, Tanner stage II breasts, soft abdomen without palpable masses, no body odor, no pubic/axillary hair, and mild estrogenization of the vagina. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for the child’s breast development?',
  options:['Adrenal tumor','Central precocious puberty','Congenital adrenal hyperplasia','Premature adrenarche','Premature thelarche'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Isolated, mild breast development in young girls without pubic/axillary hair or accelerated growth is benign premature thelarche.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the examination in this stem is written as a list of *negatives*, and each one closes a door.**\n\n| Finding | What it excludes |\n|---|---|\n| **50th centile for height and weight** — no growth acceleration | Central precocious puberty; any significant sex-steroid excess |\n| **No pubic or axillary hair, no body odour** | Every **androgen**-driven cause: adrenal tumour, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, premature adrenarche |\n| **Normal skin without oiliness** | Androgen excess again (acne, seborrhoea) |\n| **Soft abdomen, no palpable mass** | An ovarian or adrenal tumour |\n| **Normal blood pressure** | Congenital adrenal hyperplasia of the hypertensive variants; adrenal pathology |\n| **No exogenous oestrogen in the home** | Peripheral precocity from an exogenous source |\n| **Tanner II only, mild vaginal oestrogenisation** | Progressive, complete precocious puberty |\n\nWhat remains is **isolated, mild, non-progressive breast tissue in a young girl** — premature thelarche, which `14) Puberty.txt` classifies among the **“Benign incomplete variants”** of premature sexual development.\n\n**The defining feature of an incomplete variant** is that one pubertal sign appears **alone**, without growth acceleration or bone-age advance, because the hormone exposure is minimal or transient. The same deck records the neonatal analogue: “High maternal level of prolactin can cause neoborn babies to be born with breast buds and even to lactate (self-limited)” — breast tissue can be produced by small amounts of oestrogen without any pubertal process.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Adrenal tumor** — an androgen (or occasionally oestrogen) secreting mass. Androgen excess would give pubic and axillary hair, body odour, oily skin and rapid growth, none of which is present; an abdominal mass is explicitly absent.\n- **Central precocious puberty** — would drive the whole axis: progression through the pubertal sequence, a **growth spurt**, an **advanced bone age**, and on ultrasound the changes `14) Puberty.txt` describes — multicystic ovaries and a uterus moving from “Infantile: tubular” to “Pubertal: pear-shaped” with an identifiable endometrial lining. She is on the 50th centile for height with no acceleration, which is strong evidence against it.\n- **Congenital adrenal hyperplasia** — adrenal androgen excess, virilising: pubic hair, clitoromegaly, accelerated growth, markedly advanced bone age, and in the salt-wasting form a neonatal crisis. `14) Puberty.txt` reserves the “ACTH STIMULATION TEST to exclude CAH” for **atypical premature adrenarche**, defined by “marked growth acceleration, clitoral (girls) or phallic (boys) enlargement, cystic acne, advanced bone age (>2 SD above the mean for age)”. She has none of these.\n- **Premature adrenarche** — the *other* benign incomplete variant, and the closest wrong answer. But it is the **hair** variant, not the breast one: `14) Puberty.txt` defines it as “the appearance of sexual hair before the age of 8 yr in girls or 9 yr in boys without other evidence of maturation or growth acceleration”, notes it is “more frequent in girls than in boys”, attributes it to “sensitivity to androgen production by adrenal gland (6-8 yrs)”, allows “a slight increase of growth rate and bone age”, calls it “Self-limited”, and adds the long-term caveat that “Girls have higher risk for polycystic ovarian syndrome later on”. This girl has **no** pubic or axillary hair, so adrenarche is excluded by definition.\n\n**Management.** Observation with re-measurement of height and re-examination in a few months, and a bone age if there is any doubt. What would change the diagnosis is **progression** — growth acceleration, an advancing bone age, or the appearance of pubic hair — at which point she is investigated as central precocious puberty.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Isolated breast development with normal growth, no androgen signs and no mass is premature thelarche — a benign incomplete variant to observe.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.159'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-65', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which is a characteristic of constitutional growth delay?',
  options:['Adequate bone age','More common in girls','Anal height is affected','A delayed onset of puberty','Presentation is in infancy'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children with constitutional growth delay have delayed bone age and puberty, but eventually reach normal adult height. It is more common in boys.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint, and ⚠️ the same printed typo.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section, identically — including option C, which reads **“Anal height is affected”** on both pages. Printing it twice confirms it is the source’s own error rather than a scan artefact; it plainly means **“Final height is affected”**, and the option is reproduced here exactly as printed, since a printed typo survives verbatim in this bank. Both printings are staged and held as separate entries, paired by their shared menu — see `pedep-gp-3` for the anchored option table.\n\n**Expansion — one option is true and the other four are all lifted from the *familial short stature* column or are simple inversions.**\n\n| Option | Verdict | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Adequate (normal) bone age | **False** | `13) Short stature.txt`: “A. **Delayed** → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → **Familial**.” A normal bone age argues against CDGP |\n| More common in girls | **False** | It is commoner in **boys** — hence the classic vignette of the shortest, least-developed boy in the class |\n| Final height is affected | **False** | The defining reassurance of CDGP: growth is **postponed, not lost**. Final height matches the mid-parental range |\n| **A delayed onset of puberty** | **TRUE** | The condition’s name says so — constitutional delay of growth **and puberty** |\n| Presentation is in infancy | **False** | It declares itself in **late childhood and adolescence**, when peers begin their pubertal spurt |\n\n**Why puberty is the discriminating feature rather than height.** Plenty of things make a child short. Only a small number make a child short **and** sexually immature **and** skeletally immature at the same time, with everything else normal. `14) Puberty.txt` gives the threshold at which the delay becomes formal: “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by 13 yrs of age in females or by 14 yrs of age in males”, and lists constitutional delay as its own category alongside hypogonadotropic and hypergonadotropic hypogonadism.\n\n**The mechanism, in one sentence.** The hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal axis activates late; because sex steroids are what close the growth plates — `14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth” — a late start keeps the epiphyses open longer, and the apparent height deficit is recovered.\n\n**The clinical picture, from the lecture’s own Case 2.** A 13-year-old boy: “Timing of puberty: No puberty yet; Father ‘late bloomer’; Tanner stage 1; Bone age delayed 2 years; Normal labs” → “Constitutional delay of growth and puberty”, managed by “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.”\n\n**The one safeguard.** A delayed bone age is shared with endocrine disease, so the label is only applied after the screening that `13) Short stature.txt` sets out — Step One systemic bloods, Step Two TSH/fT4, coeliac screen, IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, and karyotyping in **all girls**, since mosaic Turner syndrome may show “No, or minimal manifestations”.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Delayed puberty is the defining feature of constitutional delay; the bone age is delayed, it is commoner in boys, and final height is normal.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.161'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-66', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the first sign of normal puberty in girls?',
  options:['Height acceleration','Pubic hair changes','Menarche','Palpable breast bud','Clitoris growth'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Thelarche is the first sign of puberty in girls, typically around age 9–11. Menarche and pubic hair changes occur later.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the sequence of female puberty, in order, and why the order is examined.**\n\n| Order | Event | Name | Hormonal driver |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1** | **Breast bud** (Tanner B2) | **Thelarche** | Ovarian **oestrogen** |\n| 2 | Pubic and then axillary hair | Pubarche / adrenarche | **Adrenal androgens** |\n| 3 | **Peak height velocity** — the growth spurt | — | Gonadal steroids, “Estrogen more than androgens” |\n| 4 | **First menstrual period** | **Menarche** | Established oestrogen cycling |\n| 5 | Regular ovulatory cycles, completion of Tanner V | — | — |\n\nThe hormonal detail is from `14) Puberty.txt`, which states that the pubertal growth spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, with “Estrogen more than androgens”, adding “15% to final adult height”.\n\n**Two facts make this sequence worth learning rather than memorising as a list.**\n\n1. **Thelarche and pubarche have different sources.** Breast development is **ovarian oestrogen**; pubic hair is **adrenal androgen**. They usually run together, but they are separate axes — which is exactly why the two benign incomplete variants exist as separate entities. `14) Puberty.txt` groups them under “Benign incomplete variants”: premature **thelarche** is isolated breast development, while premature **adrenarche/pubarche** is “the appearance of sexual hair before the age of 8 yr in girls or 9 yr in boys without other evidence of maturation or growth acceleration”.\n2. **The growth spurt comes *before* menarche in girls, and this differs from boys.** A girl who has started her periods has already passed her peak height velocity and has limited growth remaining, because oestrogen has begun closing the epiphyses — `14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth.” In boys the spurt comes comparatively later in the sequence, which is part of why they end taller; the same deck notes that “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Height acceleration** — real, but it is event number three, after breast budding and pubic hair, and it is much harder to detect clinically than a breast bud unless serial heights are being plotted.\n- **Pubic hair changes** — event number two, and driven by a different axis. Pubic hair *before* breast development, especially before age 8, suggests an androgen problem rather than normal puberty.\n- **Menarche** — the **last** major event, not the first. The bank pairs this question with another in the same section asking for the *last* sign, keyed menarche, so read the stem carefully.\n- **Clitoris growth** — not part of normal female puberty at all. Clitoromegaly is a **virilising** sign and should raise congenital adrenal hyperplasia, an androgen-secreting tumour, or exogenous androgen. `14) Puberty.txt` lists “clitoral (girls) or phallic (boys) enlargement” among the features of **atypical** premature adrenarche that mandate an “ACTH STIMULATION TEST to exclude CAH”.\n\n**The clinical threshold.** Breast budding before **8 years** in a girl is precocious and needs assessment; absence of any pubertal feature by **13 years** is delayed puberty (`14) Puberty.txt`). Between those two ages, watch and reassure.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Female puberty begins with the breast bud (thelarche), then pubic hair, then the growth spurt, with menarche last.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.163'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-67', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'During which period of human growth does faltering growth typically occur?',
  options:['Fetal phase','Infant phase','Childhood phase','Prepubertal phase','Pubertal phase'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Faltering growth is most commonly seen in infancy when nutrition and feeding problems are most likely.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ A four-option version of this same question also appears in this section** (Infantile / Childhood / Pubertal / Fetal), with the same key. Because this printing **replaces** the option set — adding a prepubertal phase and rewording the others — the two are held as separate questions by the standard folding test.\n\n**Expansion — “faltering growth” is a term with a specific meaning, and that meaning is confined to infancy.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines it in two lines: it is a “**Description, not a diagnosis**”, and it is “present if an **infant’s weight** falls across two centile spaces.” The definition names the infant, and it names weight.\n\n**Three reasons infancy is the vulnerable phase:**\n\n1. **Growth is fastest postnatally and the nutritional demand is greatest** — the endpoint file’s own summary slide assigns the infantile phase 15% of final height (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7), packed into two years.\n2. **The driver of growth in this phase is nutrition itself.** Any shortfall in intake or absorption translates straight onto the weight line, with no hormonal buffer.\n3. **The infant depends entirely on a carer**, and the feeding method changes twice in the first year — `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` gives complementary feeding “after 6 months”, then “6m Pureed, mashed and semisolid; 8m Finger foods; 12m Family foods”. Each transition is a chance for intake to fall, and weaning coincides with rising exposure to enteric infection as maternal antibody wanes.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Fetal phase** — growth restriction certainly occurs in utero, but it is called **IUGR / SGA**, not faltering growth. `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` classifies it as asymmetrical (“Head spare”, onset after the 18th week, chronic hypertension, severe malnutrition) or symmetrical (“Proportionate to the rest of the body”, TORCH, chromosomal anomalies, anaemia, substance abuse), and `13) Short stature.txt` adds the outcome: “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”.\n- **Childhood phase** — growth is at its slowest and steadiest here and is GH-driven, and the child feeds herself. Growth failure in this phase is less common but **more significant**: it is where endocrine disease and chronic illness declare themselves, and a falling centile after school entry always warrants investigation.\n- **Prepubertal phase** — the tail of childhood; the same reasoning applies, and there is no particular vulnerability.\n- **Pubertal phase** — demand is high during the spurt and severe undernutrition can delay puberty and blunt it, but the adolescent controls her own intake and has reserves that an infant does not.\n\n**What to do with a faltering infant.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt`: “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart”; “Investigations should be guided by history and examination”; “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”; and “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake”. Its red flags — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — are what shift the diagnosis away from simple intake.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Faltering growth is by definition an infant phenomenon — a weight falling across two centile spaces, usually from inadequate intake.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.165'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-68', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What would be the shape of the head if craniosynostosis affects the lambdoid suture?',
  options:['Wide and broad','Long and narrow','Prominent forehead','Flattening of the skull'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Fusion of the lambdoid suture causes a flat occiput and asymmetric skull shape (posterior plagiocephaly).\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section with the same four options and the same key, and a third time with a fifth option added (“Plagiocephalic”), which creates a defect because that added option is a synonym for the key. The printings are staged and held as separate entries, paired by the shared menu — see `pedep-gp-4` for the anchored option table.\n\n**Expansion — one principle generates the whole table.** A fused suture blocks growth **across** it, so the skull grows **along** it instead. Apply that to each suture:\n\n| Suture fused | Growth blocked | Resulting shape | Name |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Sagittal** | Side-to-side widening | Long and narrow | **Scaphocephaly** |\n| **Coronal**, unilateral | Forward growth on that side | Flattened forehead, asymmetry | **Anterior plagiocephaly** |\n| **Coronal**, bilateral | Forward growth altogether | Short, wide, tall skull with prominent forehead | **Brachycephaly** |\n| **Lambdoid** | Backward growth | **Flat occiput** | **Posterior plagiocephaly** |\n| **Metopic** | Forehead widening | Triangular, keeled forehead | **Trigonocephaly** |\n\n*(The metopic and bilateral-coronal rows are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the endpoint file’s other printing of this question prints a box covering the sagittal, coronal and lambdoid rows only. `1) Normal development.txt` illustrates “TOTAL CRANIOSTENOSIS” among the conditions recognisable from the face, without a suture table.)*\n\nThe lambdoid suture separates the occipital bone from the parietals at the **back** of the skull, so its fusion produces a posterior deficit — flattening of the occiput.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Wide and broad* — bilateral coronal fusion. *Long and narrow* — sagittal fusion, the commonest single-suture synostosis and therefore the most attractive wrong answer. *Prominent forehead* — brachycephaly or metopic fusion; either way, a **frontal** finding for a **posterior** suture.\n\n**The distinction that matters far more in practice than the eponyms.** True lambdoid synostosis is **rare**. The overwhelming majority of flat occiputs in infancy are **positional (deformational) plagiocephaly**, caused by supine sleeping, and are benign: they need repositioning and supervised tummy time, not surgery. The two are separated by looking down on the head from above *(this comparison is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*:\n\n| | **Positional plagiocephaly** | **Lambdoid synostosis** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Head shape from above | **Parallelogram** | **Trapezoid** |\n| Ipsilateral ear | Displaced **forward** | Displaced **backward and down** |\n| Ipsilateral forehead | **Bossed forward** | Flat or normal |\n| Suture | Palpably normal | **Ridge** over the suture |\n| Management | Repositioning, tummy time; resolves | Surgical referral |\n\n**Why craniosynostosis matters at all.** Beyond the cosmetic deformity, multi-suture fusion restricts the growing brain and raises intracranial pressure, producing headache, papilloedema and developmental consequences — which is why it appears in `1) Normal development.txt` among causes of intellectual disability recognisable from the face.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Measure and plot the head circumference, look at the head from above, palpate the sutures for a ridge, and check the fontanelle. Shape plus ridge plus a crossing OFC centile is a surgical referral; shape alone in a supine-sleeping baby is not.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Lambdoid synostosis flattens the occiput; most flat occiputs, however, are benign positional plagiocephaly, distinguished by ear position and the absence of a ridge.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.167'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-69', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'How is a very low birth weight infant defined?',
  options:['Birth weight less than 2500 g','Birth weight less than 1500 g','Birth weight less than 1750 g','Birth weight less than 1000 g','Birth weight less than 500 g'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> VLBW is defined as a birth weight less than 1500 g. ELBW (extremely low birth weight) is <1000 g.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section with the same five options and the same key. This printing gives option E complete as “less than 500 g”; the other prints it without the unit. Held as one question in the app.\n\n**Expansion — the definitions, verbatim from the syllabus.** `7) Intro to neonatology.txt`, under Classification by birth weight:\n\n- “LBW - < 2500 grams”\n- “VLBW - < 1500 grams”\n- “ELBW - < 1000 grams”\n\n**Three independent axes classify a newborn, and the examiner mixes them.**\n\n| Axis | Categories | Independent of |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Birth weight** | LBW < 2500 g · VLBW < 1500 g · ELBW < 1000 g | gestation, and appropriateness for gestation |\n| **Gestational age** | Preterm < 37 wk · Term 37–42 wk · Post-term > 42 wk | weight |\n| **Weight for gestational age** | SGA · AGA · LGA | absolute weight |\n\nA 1200 g baby at 30 weeks is **VLBW, preterm and appropriate for gestational age** — small because early. A 2100 g baby at 40 weeks is **LBW, term and SGA** — small because growth-restricted. The two have entirely different problems: the first faces the complications of immaturity, the second the consequences of chronic intrauterine undersupply. `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` gives the SGA causes in two columns — asymmetrical (“Head spare”, onset after the 18th week, chronic hypertension, severe malnutrition, genetic mutation) and symmetrical (“Proportionate to the rest of the body”, TORCH, chromosomal anomalies, anaemia, fetal alcohol syndrome) — and lists the SGA problems as respiratory distress including meconium aspiration, hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, congenital infection, polycythaemia and hyperbilirubinaemia, and NEC.\n\n**Why the weight bands exist: they are risk strata.** The lower the band, the longer the list from `7) Intro to neonatology.txt`: respiratory distress syndrome type I (“Lung is the last organ to mature into a functional state”), congenital pneumonia, pneumothorax, chronic lung disease (“Need for oxygen >36 weeks postmenstrual age”), apnoea of prematurity (“Cessation of breathing for 20sec leads to bradycardia and desaturation”, “Incidence inversely related to GA”), patent ductus arteriosus (“markedly more common in preterm infants than at term”), infection (“IgG only crosses placenta in 3rd trimester”), intraventricular haemorrhage and periventricular leukomalacia (“Both are usually silent in presentation”), necrotising enterocolitis, hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, retinopathy of prematurity, anaemia and hyperbilirubinaemia.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *< 2500 g* is plain LBW, the umbrella category. *< 1750 g* is not a recognised class — a manufactured distractor between the two real numbers. *< 1000 g* is ELBW. *< 500 g* is below the usual threshold of viability and defines nothing.\n\n**Filing note.** Kept in growth and puberty as a growth-measurement definition printed here by the bank; the secondary chapter is **neonatal**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'VLBW is birth weight under 1500 g — a weight band independent of gestational age and of whether the baby is SGA, AGA or LGA.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.169'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-70', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal',
  stem:'Which problem is commonly associated with large for gestational age infants?',
  options:['Hyperglycemia','Hypercalcemia','Anemia','Birth asphyxia','Heart failure'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> LGA infants, often of diabetic mothers, are prone to shoulder dystocia, birth trauma, and birth asphyxia.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Filing note.** This is a **perinatal / neonatal** question printed inside the Growth and Puberty section. It is filed under `perinatal` because it concerns delivery and the transition at birth; the secondary chapter is **neonatal**.\n\n**⚠️ Note the deliberate inversion in option A.** The infant of a diabetic mother is **HYPOglycaemic**, not hyperglycaemic — and it is the single most tested fact about these babies. The mechanism is in `7) Intro to neonatology.txt`: the aetiology of the IDM picture is “Maternal Hyperglycemia, Fetal Hyperglycemia & Hyperinsulinemia.” Maternal glucose crosses the placenta; maternal insulin does not. The fetus therefore makes its **own** insulin in large amounts, and insulin is a growth factor — hence macrosomia. At delivery the cord is cut, the maternal glucose supply stops, but the baby’s hyperinsulinaemia persists for hours to days, and the blood glucose falls. Option A states the maternal problem and attributes it to the baby.\n\n**Why birth asphyxia is the answer.** A macrosomic fetus is mechanically difficult to deliver. Shoulder dystocia, a prolonged second stage, instrumental delivery and birth trauma all follow from size, and each can interrupt gas exchange during delivery. The syllabus treats birth asphyxia as a major topic in its own right (`12) HIE.txt`) and lists it among the causes in `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` and `58) cerebral palsy.txt`.\n\n**The full IDM problem list, verbatim from `7) Intro to neonatology.txt`:**\n\n1. Fetal demise\n2. **Macrosomia**\n3. Congenital malformations\n4. RDS\n5. Hyperbilirubinaemia\n6. **Hypoglycaemia**\n7. Hypocalcaemia\n8. Polycythaemia\n9. Renal vein thrombosis\n10. Cardiomyopathy\n11. Congenital malformation: sacral hypoplasia and small left colon syndrome\n\nThe deck adds a slide on “HYPERTROPHIC CARDIOMYOPATHY IN IDM”, which is the one mechanism by which an LGA infant of a diabetic mother genuinely can develop cardiac compromise — septal hypertrophy from fetal hyperinsulinaemia, causing outflow obstruction, and usually resolving over weeks to months as the insulin drive falls away *(the natural history is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Hyperglycemia** — inverted, as above. Expect **hypo**glycaemia, and screen for it in the first hours.\n- **Hypercalcemia** — again inverted: the IDM list gives **hypocalcaemia**.\n- **Anemia** — the opposite. These infants are prone to **polycythaemia**, driven by chronic relative fetal hypoxia and raised erythropoietin, which in turn feeds the hyperbilirubinaemia on the same list.\n- **Heart failure** — hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is real, but it is usually asymptomatic or causes an outflow murmur; frank heart failure is uncommon, and it is not the problem “commonly associated” with LGA babies as a group.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** For an LGA baby: anticipate a difficult delivery with a skilled resuscitator present, then check the **blood glucose** early and repeatedly, and watch for jaundice, polycythaemia and a murmur.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'LGA infants risk shoulder dystocia and birth asphyxia at delivery, then hypoglycaemia — never hyperglycaemia — hypocalcaemia and polycythaemia afterwards.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.171'
},
{
  id:'pedep-gp-71', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 2-year-old girl is brought to the clinic for a routine check-up. Her mother states that she does not like to eat but prefers eating only a few spoons of pasta each day. What is the best method to check her nutritional status?',
  options:['Head circumference','Height','Weight','Weight-for-height','Skinfold thickness'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Weight-for-height assesses acute malnutrition and is the best parameter for nutritional status in toddlers.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-6`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section with the same five options and the same key. On the earlier printing the explanation box is laid over the last line and option E is truncated to “Skinfold thicknes”; **this printing shows it complete**, and it is the one from which the option text should be taken. Held as one question in the app.\n\n**Expansion — the three malnutrition indices, and which time window each reports.**\n\n| Index | Detects | Named condition | Window |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Weight-for-height** | Thinness for a given stature | **Wasting** | **Acute — weeks** |\n| Height-for-age | Shortness for age | **Stunting** | Chronic — months to years |\n| Weight-for-age | Lightness for age | **Underweight** | Mixed, non-specific |\n\nThe question asks about a toddler whose intake has been poor recently. That is an **acute** question, so the index must be weight-for-height.\n\n**Why the ratio, and not either number alone.** A 2-year-old on the 3rd centile for weight may be perfectly nourished if she is also on the 3rd centile for height — she is small, not starved. Weight-for-height removes body size from the equation and asks the only acute question: is there enough tissue on this frame? This is why `16) P.E.M.txt` lists the anthropometric panel as “Length / Height (use percentile charts), **Weight for age & weight for height**, Head, chest & abdominal circumf., Mid-arm circumference (11.5 cm)”, and grades protein–energy malnutrition on the Wellcome classification: 60–80% of the 50th centile weight without oedema is underweight, below 60% without oedema is **marasmus**, 60–80% with oedema is **kwashiorkor**, below 60% with oedema is **marasmic-kwashiorkor**.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Head circumference** — reflects **brain growth** (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.8: “Head circumference (OFC) ... Reflects brain growth”) and is the parameter **most preserved** in undernutrition — the reason the asymmetrically growth-restricted fetus is described as “Head spare” in `7) Intro to neonatology.txt`. It is a poor nutritional index precisely because it is protected.\n- **Height** — the **stunting** measure. Linear growth is slow to respond, so a few weeks of poor eating will not show on it. It answers a chronic question.\n- **Weight** — uninterpretable alone. It must be referenced to age (weight-for-age) or to stature (weight-for-height), and only the latter separates thin from small.\n- **Skinfold thickness** — a genuine measure of fat stores, and `16) P.E.M.txt` includes it (“Others: skin fold thickness, etc....”). But it needs calipers, training, and an experienced observer for reproducible readings, so it is a research or specialist tool rather than the best routine clinic method.\n\n**And the clinical judgement that follows.** A fussy toddler with a **normal weight-for-height** has a feeding-behaviour problem, not a nutritional one — and the management is behavioural. `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` describes active responsive feeding: it “Allows the child to guide feeding”, “Balances helping with encouraging self-feeding”, “Minimizes distractions during meals and feedings”, and reminds the clinician that “Children are slow eaters; Do not hurry the infant while eating..... Be patient.” `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` adds that “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake”, and sets the threshold for calling it faltering growth at a weight falling “across two centile spaces”.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Weight-for-height reports acute nutritional status; head circumference is spared in malnutrition and height reports chronicity instead.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.173'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-72', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 7-year-old boy is brought by his grandmother to the pediatrician with concerns about his growth. According to his grandmother’s words, his mother is ‘of average height’ and his father is ‘slightly shorter than his mother’. Physical examination reveals a happy and playful boy with no dysmorphic features. His height is 110 cm which is just below the 3rd centile. His weight is on the 3rd centile. He has a normal physical examination. What is the most likely cause for his short stature?',
  options:['Achondroplasia','Constitutional delay of growth and puberty','Familial short stature','Growth hormone deficiency','Vitamin D deficiency'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The child’s growth follows the 3rd centile, both height and weight proportionate, and parents are short consistent with familial short stature. Constitutional delay would show delayed puberty signs.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-8`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this vignette twice in this section with the same five options and the same key, the wording of the stem differing only slightly. This printing carries the fuller box — it adds the discrimination against constitutional delay — and is the version to keep. Held as one question in the app.\n\n**Expansion — this is the lecture’s own Case 1.** `13) Short stature.txt` prints it as: “Height 3rd centile; Normal growth velocity; Parents short; MPH= 165 cm; Normal proportions; Normal puberty stage; **Bone age = chronological age**” → “**Familial short stature**”, with the accompanying questions (“Is this pathological? Do we investigate?”) both answered no.\n\n**The four features that make it a normal variant.**\n\n1. **Height and weight on the same low centile** — proportionate. The lecture’s discriminator does not fire in either direction: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine.”\n2. **No dysmorphic features** — examination in short stature is a syndrome search, and `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Dysmorphic features” and “Skeletal abnormalities” explicitly, naming Turner, Russell–Silver, Down and Noonan.\n3. **Short parents** — his height is concordant with a low mid-parental height.\n4. **He is well** — happy, playful, normal examination.\n\n**Familial short stature against constitutional delay — the comparison the box raises and the exam always tests.**\n\n| | **Familial short stature** | **Constitutional delay (CDGP)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parents | **Short** | Normal height, often a late-developing parent |\n| Bone age | **= chronological age** | **Delayed** |\n| Puberty | **Normal timing** | **Delayed** |\n| Age at presentation | Often school entry | **Adolescence** |\n| Sex | Either | **Boys** |\n| Final height | **Short**, as predicted | **Normal** |\n\nAt 7 there are no pubertal signs to assess in either direction, which is why the parents’ heights and the bone age carry the diagnosis. `13) Short stature.txt` reduces the radiograph to one rule: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Achondroplasia* is **disproportionate** — short limbs, macrocephaly, frontal bossing — and gets its own slide under “Disproportionate short stature”; this boy is proportionate. *Constitutional delay* needs a delayed bone age and delayed puberty, and typically declares itself later. *Growth hormone deficiency* requires crossing centiles and a poor velocity: the lecture’s trigger list is “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)”, and none is met. *Vitamin D deficiency* would give bone signs — widened wrists, rachitic rosary, bowing — and a falling velocity, not a well child tracking a low centile.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Measure the parents, plot the child twice, and X-ray the wrist if there is doubt. `13) Short stature.txt` closes with the message for the family: “Most short children are normal variants.”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Proportionate short stature in a well child of short parents, with a bone age equal to chronological age, is familial short stature.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.175'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-73', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the most important factor affecting growth during the "infantile phase" in humans?',
  options:['Maternal nutrition','Infant nutrition','Size at birth','Vitamins','Parental size'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Growth in the infantile phase (first 2 years) is primarily dependent on nutrition. Later phases are influenced by hormones and genetics.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-9`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section with the same five options and the same key. This printing’s box adds the definition of the phase — “first 2 years” — which the other omits. Held as one question in the app.\n\n**Expansion — the boundary of the infantile phase is the part this box supplies, and it matters.** The phase runs from **birth to about 2 years**, which is exactly why so many other rules in this chapter change at the same point: `13) Short stature.txt` gives the WHO growth chart as “Recommended for 0-24 months” and the CDC chart from “2-19 years”; correction for prematurity is “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age”, and `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` confirms “Correction is not required after age of 2 years”; and length is measured recumbent until about the same age. Two years is the hinge of the whole topic.\n\n**The four phases and their drivers.**\n\n| Phase | Age | Driver | Share of final height |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fetal | to birth | Placenta, **maternal** nutrition, uterine environment | **30%** |\n| **Infantile** | **birth to ~2 y** | **INFANT nutrition** | **15%** |\n| Childhood | ~2 y to puberty | **GH / IGF-1**, thyroid permissive | ~40% |\n| Pubertal | puberty to fusion | **Sex steroids** | **15%** |\n\n(Fetal and infantile shares from the endpoint file’s own summary slide, `Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7; the pubertal share from `14) Puberty.txt`, which states the spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)” and “Add[s] 15% to final adult height”.)\n\n**The discrimination being tested is *infant* versus *maternal* nutrition.** Maternal nutrition governs the **fetal** phase, through the placenta — `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists “Severe malnutrition” among the maternal causes of asymmetrical intrauterine growth restriction. Once the cord is cut, the determinant becomes what reaches the baby’s own gut, in adequate volume, and is absorbed.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Maternal nutrition* — the fetal-phase driver, not the infantile one. *Size at birth* — sets the starting point and predicts whether catch-up will be needed (`13) Short stature.txt`: “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”), but does not drive the rate. *Vitamins* — matter only where a specific deficiency exists, such as vitamin D; they are not the main determinant of normal growth. *Parental size* — sets the genetic destination, visible from the childhood phase onward, and the reason infants normally cross centiles early as they migrate from birth size to genetic size.\n\n**The clinical translation.** Because infancy is nutrition-driven, faltering growth clusters here. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines it as a weight falling “across two centile spaces”, calls it a “Description, not a diagnosis”, and manages it with “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”. Its pivotal red flag is “Failure to gain weight **despite adequate caloric intake**” — the finding that moves the diagnosis from intake to malabsorption, loss or increased expenditure.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The infantile phase runs from birth to about 2 years and is driven by the infant’s own nutrition; maternal nutrition drives the fetal phase instead.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.177'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-74', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the main drive for growth in the childhood phase?',
  options:['Nutrition','Growth hormone','Testosterone','Thyroid hormones','Testosterone and estrogen'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> During the childhood phase, GH is the major driver of growth. In puberty, sex steroids add a growth spurt.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-10`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question **three times** in this section, always with the same five options and the same key, the stem varying only between “drive” and “driver”. Held once in the app.\n\n**Expansion — this box adds the pubertal half, so take the two phases together.** Childhood growth is GH-driven and steady; puberty then superimposes a spurt driven by sex steroids, which also ends growth by fusing the epiphyses. `14) Puberty.txt`: the pubertal growth spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, with “Estrogen more than androgens”, adding “15% to final adult height”, and “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. So the same hormones that create the spurt terminate growth — which is the mechanism behind two clinical facts elsewhere in this chapter: precocious puberty makes a tall child into a short adult, and constitutional delay preserves final height by keeping the plates open longer.\n\n**How the GH axis is investigated when it fails.** `13) Short stature.txt` sets a staged ladder and warns against skipping it (“Do not jump to GH tests early !!”):\n\n| Step | Tests |\n|---|---|\n| **One** | CBC, ESR, renal functions, electrolytes, LFT |\n| **Two** | TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, **IGF-1 and IGFBP-3**, **bone age**, **karyotyping (all girls)** |\n| **Three** | **GH provocation** — “No use of basal GH level ! GH secreted in pulses”; “2 GH provocation tests (sequential or on separate days) are required” with glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine |\n\nIn deficiency you find “Low levels of IGF-1 and IGFBP-3” and a “Delayed bone age”, and then look for a cause: “MRI brain (pituitary view), Fundus: papilledema, Visual field assessment” for bitemporal hemianopia from a craniopharyngioma (4%, “arising from squamous remnant of Rathke’s pouch”). Treatment is “recombinant human GH... Daily subcutaneous injection”, monitored by “Increase in height and change in height velocity” — “the single most important parameter in monitoring a child with GHD” — with side effects listed as pseudotumor cerebri, slipped capital femoral epiphysis, worsening of scoliosis, insulin resistance and pancreatitis.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Nutrition* is the **infantile** driver. *Testosterone* and *testosterone and estrogen* are the **pubertal** drivers — a phase error. *Thyroid hormones* are the strongest distractor: they are genuinely required for normal childhood growth, hypothyroidism is a classic cause of growth failure, and `52) hypothyrodism.txt` opens by stressing “linear growth and brain” — but thyroid hormone is **permissive** rather than the pacemaker. Its absence stops growth; its presence does not set the rate. GH does.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Match the phase to the hormone before answering any growth question: infancy → food; childhood → GH (with thyroid permissive); puberty → sex steroids.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Childhood growth is GH-driven; sex steroids then add the pubertal spurt and simultaneously close the epiphyses.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.179'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-75', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which is a character of "constitutional growth delay"?',
  options:['It is a pathological condition.','It occurs only in girls.','There is a lower final height.','There is a delayed onset of puberty.','It presents early in infancy.'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Constitutional delay is benign, with normal final height but delayed puberty.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Not a reprint, despite the near-identical stem.** The endpoint file asks a very similar question twice elsewhere in this section, but there the option set is different (“Adequate bone age / More common in girls / Final height is affected / A delayed onset of puberty / Presentation is in infancy”). Here the options have been **replaced** — only the key text survives, and the distractors have been rewritten as absolute statements. By the standard folding test a replaced option set makes a new question, so both are held. The discriminations differ too: this version tests *pathological vs variant* and *only in girls* rather than the bone age.\n\n**Expansion — take each option as a statement to be judged true or false.**\n\n| Option | Verdict | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| “It is a pathological condition.” | **False** | `13) Short stature.txt` summary: “**Most short children are normal variants.**” CDGP is one of the two variants, alongside familial short stature |\n| “It occurs only in girls.” | **False** | It is commoner in **boys** — and “only” makes an already wrong statement absolute |\n| “There is a lower final height.” | **False** | Growth is **postponed, not lost**. Final height falls within the mid-parental range |\n| **“There is a delayed onset of puberty.”** | **TRUE** | The condition is *constitutional delay of growth **and puberty*** |\n| “It presents early in infancy.” | **False** | It declares itself in **late childhood and adolescence**, when peers begin their spurt |\n\n**Note the examiner’s technique.** Three of the four wrong options are **absolutes** — “only”, “pathological”, “lower”. Absolute statements about a benign variant are almost always false, and spotting that pattern is a reliable route through this style of question even before the content is recalled.\n\n**Why the final height is preserved — the mechanism, in one line.** Sex steroids fuse the epiphyses (`14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”), so a child whose sex-steroid exposure begins late keeps his growth plates open longer and grows for longer. The lecture’s Case 2 shows the whole picture: a 13-year-old boy with no puberty, a father who was a “late bloomer”, Tanner stage 1, a bone age delayed 2 years and **normal labs**, diagnosed as constitutional delay of growth and puberty and managed with “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.”\n\n**The one safeguard against calling it benign too soon.** A delayed bone age is shared with endocrine disease: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ **Endocrine**.” So screening comes first — Step One systemic bloods, Step Two TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, bone age, and **karyotyping in all girls**, because mosaic Turner syndrome may show “No, or minimal manifestations”, as the lecture’s Case 3 demonstrates.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Constitutional delay is a variant, commoner in boys, presenting in adolescence, with a delayed bone age, delayed puberty, and a normal adult height — but the label is earned by excluding disease, not assumed.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay is a benign variant, commoner in boys, defined by delayed puberty, and it does not reduce final adult height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.181'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-76', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following statements regarding measuring and plotting children’s growth is true?',
  options:['Correction for prematurity should be made up to the age of 6 months','Final height usually approximates the father’s height','Once a child is 5 years old, height does not usually deviate from the centile line','It is normal for an infant’s weight to cross centiles in the first months of life'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Infants often show catch-up or catch-down growth in the first months, crossing centiles before stabilizing. Final height relates to mid-parental height, not just the father’s, and deviations after age 5 usually suggest pathology.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-11`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section with the same four options and the same key. This printing carries the fuller box — it explains three of the four options rather than one — and is the version to keep. Held as one question in the app.\n\n**Expansion — catch-up and catch-down growth, the phenomenon the key describes.** Birth size is set largely by the **intrauterine environment**; the centile a child will ultimately track is set by **genetics**. In the first months the infant therefore migrates from the first to the second:\n\n- **Catch-up growth** — a baby constrained in utero (a small mother, a first pregnancy, mild placental insufficiency) climbs centiles once feeding freely.\n- **Catch-down growth** — a baby who was generously supplied in utero (a large mother, maternal diabetes) drifts down toward the family’s genetic centile.\n\nBoth are normal, and both are complete by around 12–18 months, after which a child should track *(the timing is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. This is precisely why `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` sets the threshold for concern not at *any* crossing but at a weight falling “across **two** centile spaces”. A single crossing in a thriving, feeding infant is expected.\n\n**Why each of the other three is false.**\n\n- **“Correction for prematurity... up to the age of 6 months”** — too short. `13) Short stature.txt`: correction is “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age.” `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` gives the developmental counterpart: “Correction is not required after age of 2 years”, with the worked example of a 9-month-old born at 28 weeks whose skills resemble a 6-month-old’s.\n- **“Final height usually approximates the father’s height”** — final height follows the **mid-parental** height, which uses both parents. `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Mid-parental height calculation” in the history and defines short stature partly as “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”. Using the father alone would systematically mis-predict daughters and any child of parents with dissimilar heights.\n- **“Once a child is 5 years old, height does not usually deviate from the centile line”** — the most seductive distractor, because a school-age child *should* track. But height certainly can deviate after 5, and when it does it is **pathological**, not impossible: `13) Short stature.txt` includes “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” among the definitions of short stature, and much of this chapter consists of vignettes in which exactly that happens.\n\n**The rule that unites all four statements.** “A single measurement is less important than growth trend” (`13) Short stature.txt`). Every option is really a statement about how to read a trend line: which chart (WHO 0–24 months, CDC 2–19 years, disease-specific where available), at which age (chronological or corrected), against which target (mid-parental height), and with what threshold for concern (two centile spaces in infancy, any sustained deviation later).\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Crossing centiles in the first months: watch and re-plot. Crossing centiles after the toddler years: investigate.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Catch-up and catch-down centile crossing is normal in early infancy; after the toddler years deviation is pathological, and final height follows mid-parental height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.183'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-77', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following is a character of familial short stature?',
  options:['Height falls within 2 centile spaces of mid-parental height','More common in girls','Delayed onset of puberty','Presentation is in adolescence'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Familial short stature is characterized by normal growth velocity and bone age, with height consistent with genetic potential. Unlike constitutional delay, puberty occurs at a normal age.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-12`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section with the same four options and the same key. This printing carries the fuller box, adding the normal growth velocity, the normal bone age and the explicit contrast with constitutional delay; it is the version to keep. Held as one question in the app.\n\n**Expansion — “within 2 centile spaces of mid-parental height” is the operational definition of *concordance*.** Familial short stature means the child is short **and short as expected for the family**. `13) Short stature.txt` uses the same instrument from the other direction, defining short stature partly as “Height >2 SD below **mid-parental height**” — that is, **dis**cordance. The keyed option is the mirror image: height *within* the expected band around the mid-parental target.\n\n**Three normal findings define the condition, and the box names all three.**\n\n1. **Normal growth velocity** — the line runs parallel to a centile rather than crossing it. `13) Short stature.txt` makes velocity a criterion in its own right (“Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”) and a GH-investigation trigger (“Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)”).\n2. **Normal bone age** — “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. **B. Normal → Familial.**” One radiograph of the left wrist and hand, read against Greulich and Pyle.\n3. **Normal timing of puberty** — the feature that separates it from constitutional delay.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong — note that all three belong to the *other* variant.**\n\n| Wrong option | Whose feature it actually is |\n|---|---|\n| “More common in girls” | Neither, strictly — but **CDGP is commoner in boys**, and this option is a doubly inverted version of that |\n| “Delayed onset of puberty” | **Constitutional delay** — its defining feature |\n| “Presentation is in adolescence” | **Constitutional delay** — it declares itself when peers begin their pubertal spurt |\n\nA short child with short parents is usually noticed **early**, often at school entry when height is compared with a whole class, and enters puberty on time. A constitutionally delayed child looks unremarkable until adolescence and then falls behind.\n\n**The clinical consequence of getting them the right way round.** Familial short stature ends in a **short adult** — as predicted by the mid-parental height, and nothing has been lost. Constitutional delay ends in a **normal-height adult**. Telling a family which of the two applies is the entire purpose of the consultation, and it rests on one X-ray and two parental heights.\n\n**The lecture’s own worked example.** Case 1: an 11-year-old boy, height 3rd centile, normal growth velocity, short parents, MPH 165 cm, normal proportions, normal puberty stage, “Bone age = chronological age” → **familial short stature**, no investigation required, because “Most short children are normal variants”.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Familial short stature is height concordant with mid-parental height, with a normal growth velocity, a normal bone age and normally timed puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.185'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-78', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 13-year-old girl is brought to the clinic for concerns about delayed puberty. She is in the 10th percentile for height and 25th percentile for weight. Physical examination reveals no signs of puberty, and her bone age is mildly delayed. Her mother also had a past history of delayed puberty. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Turner syndrome','Constitutional growth delay','Growth hormone deficiency','Hypothyroidism','Familial short stature'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This condition presents with delayed puberty, mildly delayed bone age, and family history of delayed puberty. The child usually catches up later.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-13`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this vignette twice in this section with the same five options and the same key. Held once in the app.\n\n**Expansion — start with the definition, because at exactly 13 it is borderline.** `14) Puberty.txt`: delayed puberty is “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by **13 yrs of age in females** or by 14 yrs of age in males.” She has just crossed the threshold, which legitimises the assessment without yet implying disease.\n\n**Three findings then converge on constitutional delay:**\n\n1. **Mildly delayed bone age** — `13) Short stature.txt`: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine.” Her skeleton is younger than her age, so growth time remains and final height will be normal.\n2. **A maternal history of delayed puberty** — CDGP is familial. The lecture’s Case 2 uses the paternal equivalent, a father who was a “late bloomer”.\n3. **Height and weight are modestly reduced and proportionate** — 10th and 25th centiles. Neither of the lecture’s pattern rules fires: not “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease”, and not “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Turner syndrome** — the diagnosis that must never be missed in a short girl with delayed puberty, and `13) Short stature.txt` demands “Karyotyping (**All girls**)” for that reason. But Turner is **hypergonadotropic**: gonadal dysgenesis means LH and FSH are **raised**. It also usually gives more severe short stature and stigmata — `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists neck webbing, widely spaced nipples, a wide carrying angle, spoon-shaped nails, neonatal lymphoedema of the hands and feet, and coarctation of the aorta. Mosaic forms can be nearly silent (“No, or minimal manifestations”), so the karyotype is still sent — but the *most likely* diagnosis here is constitutional delay.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — needs severe short stature (more than 3 SD below the mean), height affected more than weight, and a velocity under 4 cm a year. She is on the 10th centile with a preserved weight, and GH deficiency does not run in families as “delayed puberty”.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — would show height falling while weight is preserved or rises, with lethargy, constipation, cold intolerance, dry skin and declining school performance (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`). `14) Puberty.txt` lists “Acquired hypothyroidism” under hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, so TSH and fT4 are checked in Step Two — but the clinical picture is absent.\n- **Familial short stature** — requires a **normal** bone age and **normally timed** puberty. The delayed bone age alone excludes it.\n\n**Management.** `13) Short stature.txt`, Case 2: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” In a girl the analogous option is a short course of low-dose oestrogen where the psychological burden justifies it *(the female regimen is not stated in the cached decks — not taken from the course material)*. The essential intervention is an explanation, a predicted adult height, and a follow-up appointment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Delayed puberty at 13 with a mildly delayed bone age and a family history of late puberty is constitutional delay — but still karyotype the girl.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.187'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-79', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'An 8-year-old girl presents with a 6-month history of pain most days around the umbilicus which lasts for an hour and responds to paracetamol syrup. It does not occur at weekends usually. She opens her bowels daily and passes a soft stool. She is thriving with her weight and height on the 75th centile. Which of the following options is the appropriate next step?',
  options:['Abdominal radiograph','Referral to a pediatric gastroenterologist','Liver function tests','Reassurance and no further investigation','Prescription for laxatives'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The girl’s pain is functional abdominal pain since growth and examination are normal, and symptoms stop on weekends. No further tests are needed.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Filing note.** This is a **gastroenterology** question printed inside the Growth and Puberty section of the endpoint file. It is filed under `gastroenterology`; the growth data in the stem are there as evidence of normality rather than as the subject.\n\n**Expansion — `24)Abdominal pain.txt` gives the exact table this question is built on.**\n\n| Clinical feature | **Organic** | **Functional** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Site of pain** | **Localized** | **Vague midline** |\n| **Timing of pain** | May be **nocturnal** | **Never nocturnal** |\n| **Alarm signals** | **Present** | **Absent** |\n| Family history | IBD, acid peptic disease | FGIDs, anxiety, headache |\n\nEvery row favours a functional diagnosis in this girl. The pain is **periumbilical** — the definition of vague and midline; nothing suggests it wakes her; and no alarm signal is present.\n\n**The alarm signals, from the same deck, so you can confirm their absence deliberately rather than by impression.** From the history: age under 5 years; nocturnal symptoms; persistent right upper or right lower quadrant pain; referred pain; **weight loss or growth retardation**; chronic, bile- or blood-stained vomiting; dysphagia; prolonged, mucoid or bloody diarrhoea; dysuria, haematuria or flank pain; constitutional or extraintestinal symptoms; a family history of organic GI disease. On examination: an abnormal physical examination; **growth deceleration, delayed puberty**; jaundice; significant anaemia; rebound, guarding or organomegaly; perianal disease (tags, fissures, fistulas). She has **none** — and the two that are growth-related are precisely why the stem tells you she is “thriving with her weight and height on the 75th centile”.\n\n**The “not at weekends” clue.** Symptoms that remit when school does are the classic signature of a functional disorder with a psychosocial trigger. `24)Abdominal pain.txt` sets functional gastrointestinal disorders in the ROME IV framework and notes the associated pattern of “Excess healthcare visits” and “Pain behavior”, with a family history of FGIDs, anxiety and headache. The pain is entirely real; what is absent is organic disease.\n\n**The epidemiology that makes reassurance the high-probability answer.** Chronic abdominal pain is “defined as chronic or recurrent if at least one episode of pain occurs per month for two consecutive months and is severe enough to interfere with routine functioning”, and it “occurs in **10-15 %** of children”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Abdominal radiograph** — no diagnostic role here; it exposes a child to radiation and typically produces an unhelpful comment on faecal loading that starts an unnecessary treatment.\n- **Referral to a paediatric gastroenterologist** — the diagnosis is already made in the clinic. Referral without alarm features reinforces the family’s belief that something is being missed, which perpetuates the pain behaviour the deck warns about.\n- **Liver function tests** — no jaundice, no hepatomegaly, no relevant symptom. `24)Abdominal pain.txt` cautions that investigation “should be done only when indicated according to the suspected organic disease”, that “Relevant abnormalities were found in less than 1 %”, and that ultrasound in particular “will also identify **incidental findings** such as mesenteric lymphadenitis, which may not need any intervention” — the harm of testing without a question.\n- **Prescription for laxatives** — she opens her bowels daily and passes a **soft** stool. There is no constipation to treat.\n\n**What good reassurance contains.** Name the diagnosis (functional abdominal pain), affirm the pain is real, explain the gut–brain mechanism, identify the school-related trigger, agree a plan for normal school attendance, and arrange review — with a clear statement of what would change the plan (weight loss, nocturnal pain, vomiting, blood, or a change in the pattern). Note that functional pain is formally a “Diagnosis of Exclusion”, but exclusion here is achieved by a careful history and examination, not by a battery of tests.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vague periumbilical pain, never nocturnal, sparing weekends, with normal growth and no alarm signals is functional abdominal pain — reassure without investigating.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.189'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-80', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 4-month-old infant is brought in for concerns about small size. The infant was born at 32 weeks gestation and has been primarily formula-fed. Which of the following is the first most important factor to consider in managing this infant’s growth?',
  options:['Adjusting for corrected gestational age','Introducing solid foods early','Switching to a higher-calorie formula','Recommending breastfeeding'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Premature infants’ growth should be assessed using corrected age, not chronological age, to avoid mislabeling them as small.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-14`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this vignette twice in this section with the same four options and the same key. Held once in the app.\n\n**Expansion — do the arithmetic first.** Born at 32 weeks, this baby is **8 weeks preterm**. At a chronological age of 4 months (about 17 weeks) his **corrected age is roughly 2 months**. Plot a 2-month-old on a 4-month-old’s chart and you manufacture growth failure; every intervention that follows is then a response to an artefact.\n\n**The rule, and its two versions.** `13) Short stature.txt`: correction for gestational age is “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age.” `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` gives the developmental counterpart — “Correction is not required after age of 2 years” — with the worked example that “The anticipated developmental skills of a 9-month-old baby (chronological age) born 3 months early at 28 weeks’ gestation are more like those of a 6-month-old baby (corrected age).” Apply the correction to **both** growth and development, or a normal ex-preterm baby will be labelled as both small and delayed.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong — and note that they are wrong in a specific order.**\n\n- **Introducing solid foods early** — actively harmful. `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` places complementary feeding “after 6 months”, with “6m Pureed, mashed and semisolid; 8m Finger foods; 12m Family foods”, and warns to “Avoid Foods that may cause choking or allergy”. A baby at a corrected age of 2 months has neither the head control nor the swallowing maturity for solids, and early solids displace milk calories rather than adding to them.\n- **Switching to a higher-calorie formula** — a real and useful intervention in a genuinely faltering preterm infant, but it is a **treatment**, and no diagnosis has been established. Escalating nutrition before correcting the age is treating the chart rather than the child.\n- **Recommending breastfeeding** — breast milk is preferred and the syllabus devotes a whole deck to it, but at 4 months of exclusive formula feeding relactation is neither the immediate step nor a realistic single intervention, and it does not answer the question asked.\n\n**If, after correction, he is still small.** Then the standard approach begins. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines faltering growth as a weight falling “across two centile spaces”, insists on accurate serial measurement and plotting on the appropriate chart, lists the red flags — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — and manages with “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with investigations “guided by history and examination”. In an ex-preterm infant, also review the complications that would explain poor growth: chronic lung disease, feeding intolerance, iron and vitamin supplementation, and reflux (`7) Intro to neonatology.txt`).\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Correct the age, re-plot, then decide. Most “small preterms” are exactly the right size for their corrected age.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Assess a preterm infant’s growth and development at corrected age before intervening — through the first year, and to 2 years if extremely preterm.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.191'
},
{
  id:'pedep-gp-81', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the fastest period of growth in a normal human?',
  options:['Fetal','Infantile','Childhood','Prepubertal','Pubertal'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Growth is most rapid in utero, particularly during the second trimester. Postnatal growth rates are slower compared to fetal growth.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-15`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint, and the printing that settles a contradiction.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section with the same five options and the same key, **Fetal**. Elsewhere in the same section it asks “Which is the fastest period of growth throughout life?” over a four-option list that **omits Fetal**, keys **Infancy**, and prints a box claiming infancy surpasses the fetal period — a claim contradicted by both printings of *this* question and by the file’s own summary slide: “Fetal phase (30% of final height, **Fastest period of Growth**)” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7). This second printing, which adds the second-trimester detail, is what resolves it. **Answer *fetal* whenever fetal is offered.** Held as one question in the app.\n\n**Expansion — the velocity curve and its four phases.**\n\n| Phase | Velocity | Share of final height | Driver |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fetal** | **Highest of life**, peaking in the **second trimester** | **30%** | Placenta, maternal nutrition, uterine environment |\n| Infantile (0–2 y) | Highest **postnatal**, decelerating steeply | **15%** | **Nutrition** |\n| Childhood (2 y – puberty) | Slowest and steadiest | ~40% (longest phase) | **GH / IGF-1** |\n| Pubertal | A **second peak**, smaller | **15%** | **Sex steroids** |\n\n**Why the second trimester specifically.** Linear growth — length — peaks in mid-gestation, while **weight** accrues fastest in the third trimester, when fat and glycogen are laid down. This split explains a great deal of neonatal medicine: a baby delivered before the third trimester is not just small but has almost no fat or glycogen reserve, which is why `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists “Hypoglycaemia (decrease in glycogen stores)” and “Hypothermia (decrease s.c. fat & brown fat)” among the metabolic problems of prematurity *(the trimester split between length and weight accretion is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why fetal growth is so fast, and why it is fragile.** It is driven by placental substrate supply, insulin and IGF-2 rather than by growth hormone — which is why a GH-deficient baby is born of normal length, and why the infant of a diabetic mother is macrosomic (`7) Intro to neonatology.txt`: “Maternal Hyperglycemia, Fetal Hyperglycemia & Hyperinsulinemia”). Because supply is external, anything that impairs the placenta reduces growth immediately, giving the IUGR patterns the same deck sets out: **asymmetrical** (“Head spare”, onset after the 18th week, chronic hypertension, severe malnutrition, genetic mutation) and **symmetrical** (“Proportionate to the rest of the body”, TORCH, chromosomal anomalies, anaemia, fetal alcohol syndrome).\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Infantile* is the fastest **postnatal** phase — correct only when fetal is not offered. *Childhood* is the slowest. *Prepubertal* is the tail of childhood, slower still, immediately before the spurt. *Pubertal* is the second peak: `14) Puberty.txt` gives it as “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, adding “15% to final adult height”, and terminated by those same steroids fusing the epiphyses — real, but well below the fetal rate.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Fetal growth is the fastest of life, peaking in the second trimester, and supplies 30% of final height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.193'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-82', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the main driver for growth in the childhood phase?',
  options:['Nutrition','Growth hormone','Testosterone','Thyroid hormones','Testosterone and estrogen'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> During childhood (after infancy, before puberty), growth is largely regulated by growth hormone rather than nutrition or sex hormones.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-10`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — the third printing.** The endpoint file prints this question **three times** in this section, always with the same five options and the same key, the stem alternating between “drive” and “driver”. Held once in the app.\n\n**Expansion — this box defines the phase by its boundaries, which is the part worth fixing in memory.** The childhood phase runs **after infancy and before puberty** — roughly 2 years to the onset of the pubertal spurt. That is eight to ten years, the longest of the four phases, and although its velocity is the lowest it therefore contributes the largest single share of final height.\n\n| Phase | Boundaries | Driver | Share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fetal | conception → birth | Placenta, maternal nutrition | **30%** |\n| Infantile | birth → ~2 y | **Nutrition** | **15%** |\n| **Childhood** | **~2 y → puberty** | **GH / IGF-1** (thyroid permissive) | **~40%** |\n| Pubertal | puberty → epiphyseal fusion | **Sex steroids** | **15%** |\n\n**Why the length of the phase matters clinically.** A small, sustained deficit over eight years costs more height than a dramatic one over eight months. That is why `13) Short stature.txt` makes growth **velocity** a criterion in its own right — short stature is defined partly as “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”, and GH investigation is triggered by a “Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)” — and why it insists that “A single measurement is less important than growth trend” and that “Serial measurements show pattern of growth”.\n\n**Why growth hormone and not the other four.**\n\n- **Nutrition** — the **infantile** driver. By school age a well child in a food-secure home is no longer nutrition-limited, which is exactly why an isolated fall in height with a preserved weight in this age group points to hormones: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; **Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine**.”\n- **Testosterone** and **testosterone and estrogen** — the **pubertal** drivers. `14) Puberty.txt` states that the spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, oestrogen contributing “more than androgens”, and that these same steroids then close the epiphyses. Offering them for the childhood phase is a phase error.\n- **Thyroid hormones** — the strongest distractor. Thyroid hormone is genuinely required: hypothyroidism is a classic cause of childhood growth failure with a delayed bone age, and `52) hypothyrodism.txt` opens by stressing “linear growth and brain”. But it is **permissive** — needed for GH to act — rather than the pacemaker.\n\n**When the axis fails.** `13) Short stature.txt` requires severe short stature (more than 3 SD below the mean), height affected more than weight, height more than 1.5 SD below the mid-parental height, or a velocity under 4 cm a year before GH deficiency is even investigated; then “Low levels of IGF-1 and IGFBP-3” with a “Delayed bone age”, confirmed by two provocation tests because “No use of basal GH level ! GH secreted in pulses”. And across the whole slide: “**Do not jump to GH tests early !!**”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'From about 2 years to puberty, growth is driven by growth hormone — the longest phase, contributing the largest share of final height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.195'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-83', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What would be the shape of the head if craniosynostosis affects the lambdoid suture?',
  options:['Wide and broad','Long and narrow','Plagiocephalic','Prominent forehead','Flattening of the skull'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The key as printed is E, flattening of the skull — and it is correct, but ⚠️ SO IS OPTION C, because the two are the same answer under two names.**\n\n**⚠️ DEFECT — two correct options, on the bank’s own evidence.** The endpoint file prints this same question twice more in this section over a **four**-option list that does not contain “Plagiocephalic”, and its printed box there reads: “Lambdoid suture fusion -> **Posterior flattening of the skull (posterior plagiocephaly)**.” The bank therefore states in its own explanation that *flattening of the skull* and *plagiocephaly* are the same finding for this suture — yet here it offers both as separate options and keys only one. The key is left exactly as printed, per the rule that the bank’s printed key stands; a learner who chose C has not made an error of knowledge. (Strictly, *plagiocephalic* means “oblique head” and denotes asymmetry, which also arises from **unilateral coronal** fusion — anterior plagiocephaly — so the term is broader than the lambdoid answer. That is the only defence of the key, and the bank never states it.)\n\n**The whole suture map, from one principle.** A fused suture blocks growth **across** it, so the skull compensates by growing **along** it:\n\n| Suture fused | Growth blocked | Resulting shape | Name |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Sagittal** (midline, front-to-back) | Side-to-side widening | Long and narrow | **Scaphocephaly** (dolichocephaly) |\n| **Coronal**, unilateral | Forward growth on that side | Flattened forehead, facial asymmetry | **Anterior plagiocephaly** |\n| **Coronal**, bilateral | Forward growth altogether | Short, wide, tall skull; prominent forehead | **Brachycephaly** |\n| **Lambdoid** (posterior) | Backward growth | **Flat occiput** | **Posterior plagiocephaly** |\n| **Metopic** | Forehead widening | Triangular, keeled forehead | **Trigonocephaly** |\n\nThe sagittal, coronal and lambdoid rows are the endpoint file’s own (its box on the four-option printing of this question); *the metopic and bilateral-coronal rows are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material.* `1) Normal development.txt` illustrates “TOTAL CRANIOSTENOSIS” among the conditions recognisable from the face in a child with intellectual disability, but prints no suture-by-suture table.\n\n**Why the other three options are wrong.** *Wide and broad* — bilateral coronal fusion (brachycephaly), an anterior problem. *Long and narrow* — sagittal fusion (scaphocephaly), the commonest single-suture synostosis and therefore the most attractive wrong answer. *Prominent forehead* — brachycephaly or metopic fusion; again frontal, and the lambdoid suture is posterior.\n\n**The clinical discrimination that matters more than the eponyms.** True lambdoid synostosis is **rare**. Almost every flat occiput in infancy is **positional (deformational) plagiocephaly** from supine sleeping — benign, treated by repositioning and supervised tummy time, never by surgery. Look down on the head from above *(this comparison is not taken from the course material)*:\n\n| | **Positional plagiocephaly** | **Lambdoid synostosis** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Head shape from above | **Parallelogram** | **Trapezoid** |\n| Ipsilateral ear | Pushed **forward** | Pulled **backward and down** |\n| Ipsilateral forehead | **Bossed forward** | Flat or normal |\n| Suture on palpation | Normal | **Palpable ridge** |\n| Management | Repositioning; resolves | Surgical referral |\n\n**Why craniosynostosis matters at all.** A single fused suture is largely a problem of shape; **multi-suture** fusion restricts the growing brain and raises intracranial pressure, producing headache, papilloedema, and developmental consequences — the reason total craniostenosis appears in the syllabus under causes of intellectual disability.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Plot the head circumference at every visit, look at the head from above, palpate the sutures for a ridge, and check the fontanelle. Shape **plus** a ridge **plus** a crossing OFC centile is a surgical referral; shape alone in a supine-sleeping baby is not.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Lambdoid synostosis flattens the occiput — posterior plagiocephaly; but most flat occiputs are benign positional deformation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.197'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-84', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 3-year-old boy’s height has fallen from the 50th percentile to below the 3rd percentile over the past year. What is the most likely cause?',
  options:['Familial short stature','Constitutional growth delay','Growth hormone deficiency','Turner syndrome'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A child with progressive drop in growth centiles suggests pathological short stature such as growth hormone deficiency, not familial or constitutional.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-16`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section with the same four options and the same key. Held once in the app.\n\n**Expansion — the single most important sentence in this chapter is that *crossing* is not the same as *being short*.** Two of the four options describe children who are short but have **always** been short and are tracking their own line. Only pathology makes a child **become** short. `13) Short stature.txt` puts centile crossing among the definitions of short stature — “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” — alongside “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”, both of which describe the **slope** of the line, not its position.\n\nFalling from the 50th centile to below the 3rd in a single year is a catastrophic loss of velocity and cannot be a normal variant.\n\n**Why growth hormone deficiency.** The lecture’s own indications for suspecting it, and this boy meets three of the four: “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); **Height affected > Weight**; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; **Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)**.” The stem specifies **height** falling, not weight — the endocrine pattern of “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”.\n\n**How it would be worked up, in order.** Step One systemic bloods (CBC, ESR, renal functions, electrolytes, LFT); Step Two TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, **IGF-1 and IGFBP-3**, **bone age**, and karyotype in any girl; Step Three GH provocation, because “No use of basal GH level ! GH secreted in pulses” and “2 GH provocation tests (sequential or on separate days) are required” using glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine. In deficiency: “Low levels of IGF-1 and IGFBP-3” with a “Delayed bone age”. Then look for a cause — “MRI brain (pituitary view), Fundus: papilledema, Visual field assessment: Visual field loss – bitemporal hemianopia (optic chiasma)”, hunting a craniopharyngioma (4%). The lecture also gives the clues that a deficiency is congenital: “Neonate: Hypoglycemia, Prolonged jaundice, microphallus; Craniofacial abnormalities; Cranial irradiation, head trauma, CNS infection; Consanguinity or affected family member.”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Familial short stature** — a child who has always tracked a low centile in line with short parents, with a **normal** bone age. It cannot produce a fall from the 50th centile.\n- **Constitutional growth delay** — declares itself in **adolescence**, and its hallmarks are delayed puberty and a delayed bone age; the growth line runs low but roughly **parallel** rather than plunging. At 3 years old there is no puberty to be delayed.\n- **Turner syndrome** — a genuine cause of falling height and the option to take seriously in a **girl**; `13) Short stature.txt` demands “Karyotyping (All girls)” for exactly that reason. This patient is a boy, so it is excluded on sex alone.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A young child crossing centiles downward is pathological until proved otherwise, and the first question is always **which line is falling** — weight leading points to systemic disease and the gut; height leading with weight preserved points to the endocrine system.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A height centile falling while weight is preserved is the endocrine pattern — in a young child, work up growth hormone deficiency.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.199'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-85', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following is the last sign of puberty in girls?',
  options:['Breast development','Pubic hair growth','Menarche','Axillary hair growth'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Menarche is typically the last major event of puberty after breast and pubic hair development.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the same sequence as the “first sign” question, read from the other end.** The endpoint file asks both in this section, so learn the order once and answer either:\n\n| Order | Event | Name | Driver |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1** | Breast bud | **Thelarche** | Ovarian **oestrogen** |\n| 2 | Pubic hair | Pubarche | **Adrenal androgens** |\n| 3 | Axillary hair | — | Adrenal androgens |\n| 4 | Peak height velocity — the growth spurt | — | Gonadal steroids, “Estrogen more than androgens” |\n| **5** | **First menstrual period** | **Menarche** | Established oestrogen cycling |\n\n**Why menarche comes last, mechanistically.** Menstruation requires a uterus that has been oestrogenised for long enough to build an endometrium, and an axis mature enough to cycle. `14) Puberty.txt` describes the ultrasound correlate — the uterus moves from “Infantile: tubular” to “Pubertal: pear-shaped”, with “Endometrial lining identification” — a structural change that takes a sustained period of oestrogen exposure. Breast tissue, by contrast, responds to much smaller and briefer amounts, which is why premature thelarche exists as a benign variant while premature menarche does not have a comparably common benign counterpart.\n\n**The single most useful clinical corollary.** **Menarche marks the end of most of a girl’s growing.** The growth spurt precedes it, and by the time periods begin the epiphyses are already closing under the influence of oestrogen — `14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth.” Typically only a few more centimetres remain *(the residual growth figure is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. So:\n\n- A short girl who has **not** yet menstruated still has growth to work with, and intervention may help.\n- A short girl who is already **two years post-menarche** has little height left to influence, which changes both the urgency and the realistic goals of treatment.\n- **Precocious puberty** brings menarche forward and therefore closes the plates early — which is why an untreated child is tall for age and short as an adult, and why GnRH agonist therapy is aimed at preserving final height.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Breast development* is the **first** sign. *Pubic hair growth* is second and comes from a different axis (adrenal androgen rather than ovarian oestrogen), which is why premature adrenarche and premature thelarche are separate benign variants in `14) Puberty.txt`. *Axillary hair growth* follows pubic hair but still precedes menarche.\n\n**The thresholds to carry with the sequence.** Any pubertal sign before **8 years** in a girl is precocious and needs assessment; no pubertal feature by **13 years** is delayed puberty (`14) Puberty.txt`: “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by 13 yrs of age in females or by 14 yrs of age in males”).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Menarche is the last major pubertal event in girls and follows the growth spurt — so little growth remains after periods begin.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.201'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-86', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following statements is FALSE as regards growth in males?',
  options:['Adult males are taller than adult females','Males have a longer childhood growth phase than females','The peak height at puberty is higher in males than in females','Growth stops earlier in males'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Growth actually stops later in males because puberty begins later and lasts longer.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-17`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in this section with the same four options and the same key. Held once in the app.\n\n**Expansion — the entire male height advantage comes from two facts, and this question tests all four consequences of them.**\n\n**Fact 1: boys start puberty about two years later.** `14) Puberty.txt` states it directly: “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys.” Because the childhood phase ends when the pubertal spurt begins, a later start means a **longer childhood phase** — option B, true — and those extra pre-pubertal years are spent growing steadily under GH.\n\n**Fact 2: the male spurt is larger.** The spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)” and adds about 15% to final adult height; peak height velocity is higher in boys — option C, true.\n\nCombine them and adult males are taller — option A, true. And because puberty begins later and takes longer to complete, **epiphyseal fusion occurs later in boys**, so growth stops **later**, not earlier — option D is the false statement and therefore the answer.\n\n| | **Girls** | **Boys** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Onset of puberty | Earlier | **~2 years later** |\n| Length of childhood phase | Shorter | **Longer** |\n| Peak height velocity | Lower | **Higher** |\n| Position of the spurt in the sequence | Early — **before** menarche | Later in the sequence |\n| Epiphyseal fusion | Earlier | **Later** |\n| Final adult height | Shorter | **Taller** |\n\n**The mechanism of stopping is the counter-intuitive part.** The hormones that drive the spurt are the hormones that end growth: `14) Puberty.txt`, “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth.” Two clinical facts fall straight out of that single line:\n\n- **Precocious puberty** produces a child who is **tall for age but short as an adult**, because the plates close early — which is exactly the loss GnRH agonist therapy exists to prevent.\n- **Constitutional delay** produces a child who is **short for age but normal as an adult**, because the plates stay open longer.\n\n**Answering a negatively worded stem.** Three of the four options here are true statements. The commonest way to lose this item is to select the first option that reads correctly. Mark each option T or F explicitly before choosing, and note that the false one is usually an **inversion** of a true fact — here, “earlier” substituted for “later”.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In boys: later start, longer childhood, bigger spurt, later fusion, taller adult. Anything that brings puberty forward steals adult height by closing the plates prematurely.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Boys start puberty later, grow through a longer childhood phase and fuse their epiphyses later — so male growth stops later, not earlier.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.203'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-87', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following conditions may cause macrocephaly?',
  options:['Chronic subdural hematoma','Cerebral palsy','Congenital infection','Hypoglycemia','Perinatal asphyxia'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The key is A, chronic subdural haematoma, and the endpoint file grounds it on its own summary slide:** “Macrocephaly ... Causes: ... **Raised ICP (hydrocephalus, subdural hematoma, brain tumor)**” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.20).\n\n**The discrimination being tested is macrocephaly against microcephaly, and four of the five options belong to the other column.**\n\n| | **Macrocephaly** | **Microcephaly** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanism | Something inside the skull has **increased** — brain, CSF, or blood | The brain has **failed to grow** or has been **injured** |\n| Causes | **Hydrocephalus**, **chronic subdural haematoma**, **brain tumour** (all raising ICP); benign familial macrocephaly; some metabolic and storage disorders | **Congenital infection (TORCH)**, **perinatal asphyxia / HIE**, severe prenatal insults, genetic and metabolic syndromes, and any severe early brain injury — the same injuries that produce **cerebral palsy** |\n\nThe macrocephaly column is from the endpoint file’s summary slide; the microcephaly column is grounded in the syllabus by `1) Normal development.txt`, which lists **MICROCEPHALY** and **SEVERE HYDROCEPHALUS** among the conditions recognisable from the face in a child with intellectual disability, and in `12) HIE.txt` and `58) cerebral palsy.txt`, which cover perinatal asphyxia and its consequences.\n\n**Why a chronic subdural haematoma enlarges the head.** In infancy the sutures are open and the fontanelles unfused, so the skull is a **compliant** container. A slowly accumulating extra-axial collection is accommodated by the head expanding rather than by immediate herniation — which is why chronic subdurals in infants present as **a head circumference crossing centiles upward**, with a full fontanelle, prominent scalp veins, splayed sutures, vomiting, irritability, developmental regression, and in advanced cases the sun-setting sign *(the sign list is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the endpoint file names the cause, not the presentation)*.\n\n**And the safeguarding point that must always accompany it.** A chronic subdural haematoma in an infant with no adequate explanation is a **classic presentation of abusive head trauma**, and the finding mandates a full evaluation — skeletal survey, retinal examination for haemorrhages, and a child-protection assessment. This is the single most consequential fact attached to this question *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Cerebral palsy** — a disorder of movement and posture from a **non-progressive** insult to the developing brain. Where the insult was destructive, head growth is **impaired**: acquired microcephaly is common in severe spastic quadriplegic CP. It does not enlarge the head.\n- **Congenital infection** — the TORCH group. Cytomegalovirus and toxoplasmosis characteristically cause **microcephaly** with intracranial calcification. (The exception proves the rule: congenital toxoplasmosis can cause obstructive **hydrocephalus** and therefore a large head — but the option as written, “congenital infection” in the abstract, is a microcephaly answer, and the bank’s own list keys the subdural.)\n- **Hypoglycemia** — a metabolic emergency, and severe or recurrent neonatal hypoglycaemia injures the brain (`10) new Seizures . Birth injuries , Hpoglycemia 2.txt`; `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists it among the metabolic problems of prematurity, from decreased glycogen stores). Injury reduces subsequent brain growth; it does not increase head size.\n- **Perinatal asphyxia** — the cause of hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy (`12) HIE.txt`) and a leading cause of cerebral palsy. Its late consequence is **microcephaly**, not macrocephaly. Note that cerebral **oedema** in the acute phase can transiently increase intracranial volume, but that is measured in days, not as a growing head circumference.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Plot the head circumference at every infant visit alongside weight and length. It reflects brain growth (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.8: “Head circumference (OFC) ... Reflects brain growth”), and a measurement **crossing centiles in either direction** is a neurological finding requiring explanation — upward, think raised intracranial pressure and image the head; downward, think brain injury or failure of brain growth.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Macrocephaly means increased intracranial contents — hydrocephalus, subdural haematoma or tumour; asphyxia, congenital infection and cerebral palsy cause microcephaly.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.205'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-88', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 5-year-old girl is brought to the clinic because her parents are concerned about her short stature. Her height is below the 3rd percentile, and her weight is in the 25th percentile. Her growth velocity is normal. A bone age assessment reveals that her bone age is consistent with her chronological age. What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  options:['Initiate growth hormone therapy','Order a karyotype analysis','Monitor growth every 6 months','Refer to a pediatric endocrinologist','Test for celiac disease'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The child has proportionate short stature with normal bone age and velocity, suggesting a benign variant. Close monitoring is appropriate.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Note a tension with the lecture, which is worth knowing before an examination.** `13) Short stature.txt` lists **“Karyotyping (All girls)”** in its investigation ladder for short stature — an unqualified instruction, and Turner syndrome is the reason: `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` describes it as “a chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism”, and the lecture’s own Case 3 is a 7-year-old girl with “No major complaints” who proved to have **mosaic Turner syndrome**, in which there may be “No, or minimal manifestations” because “Only a small percentage of cells have a chromosomal abnormality 45,XO”. By that instruction, option B would be defensible in this girl. The bank keys monitoring because **both** her growth velocity **and** her bone age are normal, which is the pattern of a benign variant. The key is left as printed; the discrepancy is recorded here so that a learner who answered “karyotype” knows the lecture supports them, and so that in clinical practice the karyotype is not forgotten in a short girl.\n\n**Why monitoring is nonetheless a sound answer on the stem as written.**\n\n1. **Normal growth velocity.** She is short but not *becoming* shorter. `13) Short stature.txt` defines pathological short stature partly by the opposite — “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” and “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”.\n2. **Bone age equal to chronological age.** The lecture’s rule: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. **B. Normal → Familial.**” A normal bone age argues against both constitutional delay and endocrine disease, including hypothyroidism and GH deficiency, both of which delay skeletal maturation.\n3. **Weight above height (25th vs <3rd), but growth is not decelerating.** Height affected more than weight is nominally the endocrine pattern — “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine” — which is precisely why this stem is not trivially benign, and why the normal velocity and normal bone age have to be doing the work.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Initiate growth hormone therapy** — treating without a diagnosis. `13) Short stature.txt` sets the bar for even suspecting GH deficiency (“Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)”), requires two provocation tests to confirm it, and lists real harms of treatment: pseudotumor cerebri, slipped capital femoral epiphysis, worsening of scoliosis, insulin resistance, pancreatitis.\n- **Order a karyotype analysis** — see the note above. It is the option with the strongest claim to be also correct, and in practice it should be sent in a short girl.\n- **Refer to a pediatric endocrinologist** — premature. The screening that would justify referral has not shown anything abnormal, and the lecture warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n- **Test for celiac disease** — the coeliac screen sits in Step Two and is the right test when the **weight** line falls faster than the height line, or with gut symptoms. Her weight is comfortably above her height centile, which is the opposite pattern.\n\n**What monitoring must actually mean.** Not “do nothing”. Re-measure height accurately at defined intervals, plot on the correct chart, calculate the growth **velocity** between visits, calculate and record the **mid-parental height** so that her centile can be judged against her family, and re-examine for pubertal timing. `13) Short stature.txt`: “A single measurement is less important than growth trend.” If the velocity falls, or she does not enter puberty on time, the work-up restarts.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Short stature with a normal growth velocity and a bone age equal to chronological age is a benign variant to monitor — though the lecture still asks for a karyotype in every short girl.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.207'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-89', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Which of the following should be avoided in an infant’s diet during the first year of life?',
  options:['Pureed vegetables','Honey','Mashed fruits','Iron-fortified cereals'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Honey can cause infant botulism and must be avoided before 12 months.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Filing note.** This is a **nutrition** question, printed as the last item of the Growth and Puberty section. It is filed under `nutrition`.\n\n**Expansion — infant botulism, since the box states the conclusion without the mechanism.** Honey can contain **spores** of *Clostridium botulinum*. In an older child or adult the spores pass harmlessly through a mature gut whose established flora and acidity prevent them germinating. In an infant under 12 months the colonic flora is not yet established, so the spores **germinate in the gut, colonise it, and produce botulinum toxin in situ** — this is a *toxico-infection*, not food poisoning by pre-formed toxin. The toxin blocks acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, giving a **descending, symmetrical flaccid paralysis**: constipation is characteristically the first sign, then poor feeding, a weak cry, a lost head control, ptosis, sluggish pupils, and hypotonia — the classic “floppy baby” — with respiratory failure in severe cases. Treatment is supportive care with ventilation as needed plus human-derived botulism immune globulin; antibiotics are avoided because bacterial lysis releases more toxin. Prevention is simply the rule in the key: **no honey before 12 months**, in any form, including on a dummy or in cooking, since spores survive normal cooking temperatures. *(This entire paragraph is filled from general medical knowledge — none of the cached Pediatrics lecture files covers infant botulism; `56) Floppy infant .txt` addresses the floppy infant without naming it.)*\n\n**Why each of the other three options is not merely harmless but actively recommended.** `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` sets out complementary feeding from **6 months**, with texture graded by developmental stage:\n\n| Age | Texture (from the lecture) |\n|---|---|\n| **6 months** | “Pureed, mashed and semisolid” |\n| **8 months** | “Finger foods” |\n| **12 months** | “Family foods” |\n\n- **Pureed vegetables** — exactly the 6-month texture. The same deck adds a practical warning: “Do not use mixer/ blender in preparation of vegetable foods, it transforms it into a liquid form unsuitable for training the baby to swallow semisolid foods. Better use strainer.”\n- **Mashed fruits** — likewise the 6-month texture.\n- **Iron-fortified cereals** — appropriate and important. The lecture stresses that “Nutritional gaps starts to appear from 6 months”, which is why complementary feeding begins then; iron is the classic gap, because fetal iron stores are exhausted around that age and breast milk is low in iron *(the iron-store rationale is not taken from the course material)*. The deck also requires **variety** — “Eating a wide selection of foods within and among the major food groups” — and that foods “must be prepared, served, and stored safely”, with the instruction to “**Avoid Foods that may cause choking or allergy**”.\n\n**Two other first-year rules worth carrying alongside honey** *(not taken from the course material)*: **whole cow’s milk should not be the main drink before 12 months**, because of its low iron content and the risk of occult gastrointestinal blood loss; and **choking hazards** — whole nuts, whole grapes, popcorn, hard raw vegetable pieces — should be avoided or modified in shape, which is the specific content of the deck’s own instruction to avoid foods that may cause choking.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** From 6 months, offer a wide variety of appropriately textured foods including iron-rich ones, and give **no honey until the first birthday**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Honey must be avoided in the first year because C. botulinum spores germinate in the immature gut and cause infant botulism.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.209'
}
